\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 What Is  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2 ? It Isn't as Clear as You Think.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kate Zernike \par \uc2 1,735 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 October 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 In medical terms, the definition is clear. But when disputes arise, opponents argue that not every termination is an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Even after five decades of argument about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the United States, the most contentious question newly at the forefront is a very basic one: What is \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 Major medical societies, and medical billing codes, define \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as any procedure that terminates a pregnancy -- whether that pregnancy is wanted or unwanted, whether a woman is seeking the procedure to clean out her uterus after a miscarriage, or because of a dire fetal diagnosis, or to terminate a pregnancy that she had not expected.\par \par \uc2 ''An \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' said Dr. Louise King, an obstetrician-gynecologist and bioethicist at \uc2 Harvard Medical School\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  lawmakers and groups disagree, arguing that it's an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  only if the woman or her medical provider elects to end the pregnancy. This generally means that terminating a pregnancy in a dire medical situation is acceptable, while terminating an unwanted pregnancy is not.\par \par \uc2 During the five decades that Roe v. Wade established a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , this was mostly a semantic dispute. But in the aftermath of the Supreme Court's decision to overturn Roe, simply defining the word \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has taken on new political, legal and medical consequences.\par \par \uc2 States are struggling to define what they will and will not allow. Doctors, too, are grappling: those in states that now ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  say they have stopped providing the procedures because violations of the law can result in lengthy prison terms, large fines and the loss of a medical license.\par \par \uc2 Most laws allow for exceptions to save the life of the pregnant woman. But uncertainty about what qualifies as life-threatening has resulted in what the president of the \uc2 American Medical Association\uc2  called ''chaos,'' as medical professionals try to decide what conditions fall under those exceptions. Women are being denied abortions for miscarriages and to end pregnancies that have little or no chance of survival, or left to become sicker before they can have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  deemed to be lifesaving.\par \par \uc2 That has put politicians who helped enact those laws, largely Republicans, on the defensive in midterm election campaigns. Under fire, they have tried to carve out new definitions of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and blame doctors for misunderstanding.\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  politicians in Louisiana faced outrage in August when a hospital denied an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a woman carrying a fetus that doctors said would be born without a skull. One of the state's three bans allowed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to end ''medically futile'' pregnancies. But the fetus's condition, acrania, was not specifically included on a list of exceptions allowed under the law.\par \par \uc2 Jeff Landry, the state's attorney general, blamed doctors, saying, ''it is the hospital that has created ambiguity where there is none.''\par \par \uc2 ''Assuming such a diagnosis was properly certified, the removal of the unborn child is 'not an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,''' he wrote in a letter to the hospital's general counsel.\par \par \uc2 Katrina Jackson, a Democrat who sponsored one of the state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, told a local television station that the procedure the woman had been seeking was not an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 : ''This woman is seeking a medical procedure for a pregnancy that is not viable outside of the womb.''\par \par \uc2 The woman ended up traveling roughly 1,400 miles to a \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  clinic in New York, where she terminated her pregnancy with an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Medical societies and doctors who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights say defining \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  by intent is a distinction without a difference, because any termination proceeds with the intent to end that pregnancy. And the procedures are the same regardless of whether a woman has had a miscarriage or seeks to end an unwanted pregnancy: Surgically, an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  involves dilation and curettage, or dilation and evacuation; a medical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is done with pills.\par \par \uc2 They accuse anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists of trying to add a value judgment, one intended to suggest that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is something only promiscuous women get.\par \par \uc2 ''When something sad or devastating happens, you're always going to hear the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement saying, 'That is not \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,' because they can't come to terms with the fact that that is also an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' said Jenny Ma, who has litigated against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans as senior staff attorney for the \uc2 Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''You can add all sorts of different words, you can say induced delivery, you can say quote-unquote elective, but it's the same,'' she said. ''It's all part of how \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been stigmatized.''\par \par \uc2 Soon after Roe was overturned, Ohio doctors denied an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a 10-year-old girl who had become pregnant by rape, because the state's ban on abortions after detection of fetal cardiac activity -- generally around six weeks of pregnancy -- does not include any exception for rape victims.\par \par \uc2 However, Catherine Glenn Foster, the president of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group \uc2 Americans United for Life\uc2 , testified in Congress that ''it would probably impact her life and so therefore it would fall under any exception and would not be an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups argue that states have carefully crafted bans to make sure that anyone who needs an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for medical reasons receives one.\par \par \uc2 ''That's not the same as elective \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that is done for the primary purpose of producing a dead baby,'' said Dr. Donna Harrison, the chief executive of the American Association of Pro-Life Obstetricians and Gynecologists.\par \par \uc2 Still, only some state bans -- Texas, for example -- specify that the procedure is ''not \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 '' if it is done to treat a miscarriage; others, including Arizona and Wisconsin, make no exception for that. (The terms for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and miscarriage are so intertwined that in those laws, written in the 19th century, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is defined as ''procuring a miscarriage.'')\par \par \uc2 The language around pregnancy has long been subject to fierce debate. In the early 2000s, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups successfully pushed for a federal ban on an extremely rare termination procedure that is typically done in the second trimester -- known medically as intact dilation and evacuation -- by rebranding it ''partial birth \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 But in the new debate over defining \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights groups say they themselves may have unintentionally created confusion.\par \par \uc2 Even when Roe was the law of the land, hospitals often set up committees to decide whether \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was ethical, or justified as ''therapeutic.'' So the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights groups set up free-standing clinics to try to expand access. Dr. Jamila Perritt, an obstetrician-gynecologist and the president of Physicians for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Health\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said that encouraged the belief that abortions done at clinics were not the same as those done in doctors' offices or hospitals where a woman was ending a pregnancy because of cancer treatment, or because of a fetal abnormality.\par \par \uc2 ''I had patients tell me, 'I'm not like the rest of them, they were careless,''' Dr. Perritt said.\par \par \uc2 Doctors often soften language when speaking to patients, especially women who are devastated by a miscarriage or a dire fetal diagnosis, both of which carry their own stigmas. Rather than saying ''termination,'' they may talk about miscarriage and evacuation, or helping along a natural process, even as they note it as ''\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 '' in the medical chart.\par \par \uc2 Dr. King, at Harvard, compared it to discussions of other sensitive topics: talking with patients about body mass index rather than morbid obesity, or a ''mass'' instead of a tumor for cancer diagnoses.\par \par \uc2 ''But then weaponizing and politicizing language is highly inappropriate, and unethical,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 ''We don't want patients to have to be mired in politics when they're going through an incredibly difficult time in their lives,'' she added, ''but when you write a law, the law has to use the accepted terminology, and when I make sure I'm adhering to that law, I can only use that accepted terminology.''\par \par \uc2 Even women who have had abortions may draw a distinction between a procedure after a miscarriage and an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Some are now reconsidering their own medical histories.\par \par \uc2 Chrissy Teigen, the model and television personality, announced at a conference and on social media in September that discussions after the Supreme Court decision made her realize that a miscarriage she publicized in 2020 had in fact been an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Her fetus, in the second trimester, ''had absolutely no chance'' and her own life was at risk. Her announcement -- The Hollywood Reporter said she ''confessed'' it -- inspired the actress Jenny Mollen to announce that she, too, had abortions to treat two miscarriages.\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups and lawmakers argued that Ms. Teigen was wrong to call the procedure an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and accused her of changing her words for political gain.\par \par \uc2 LifeNews.com wrote that doctors did not ''purposefully kill her unborn son,'' and Ms. Teigen and her family ''did not want him to die and mourned his death even though he never lived outside the womb.''\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups don't always agree on the definition of acceptable \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Dr. Harrison, for example, said she does not believe that the procedure is the right response to rape, or for a fetus diagnosed with a deadly anomaly. While the pregnancy of the 10-year-old Ohio girl was ''a horrible social disaster,'' Dr. Harrison said, ''that pregnancy was not a threat to her life.'' Similarly, she said, fetuses with what she termed a ''life-limiting diagnosis'' can be carried to term in perinatal hospice care.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights supporters say the debate should not be about what exceptions to allow in state laws, because those carve-outs can't account for every possibility; they may even aggravate the stigma around \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and pregnancy loss.\par \par \uc2 ''We're creating this narrative that some people deserve care and some people don't,'' said Dr. Kristyn Brandi, the Darney-Landy fellow at the \uc2 American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists\uc2 . ''In medicine, we don't have that divide based on reason or the divide based on situation. It's more, the pregnancy needs to end for whatever reason, and we will provide that care because that's what the person in front of us needs.''\par \par \uc2 the word ''\uc2 '' has taken on new political, legal and medical consequences since Roe v. Wade was overturned in June. (PHOTOGRAPH BY JAMIE KELTER DAVIS FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A14) This article appeared in print on page A1, A14. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020221019eiaj00037\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
