\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Ryan and Vance Spar In Ohio Senate Debate As Race Stays Tight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Katie Glueck \par \uc2 1,274 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 October 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 13\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The debate between Representative \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  of Ohio, a Democrat, and J.D. Vance, a Republican, was strikingly contentious and sometimes personal.\par \par \uc2 Representative \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  of Ohio, a Democrat, and J.D. Vance, a Republican, clashed bitterly over issues of ideology and fitness for office in their final debate in the state's marquee Senate race, as Mr. Ryan painted his opponent as extreme, and Mr. Vance lashed Mr. Ryan as ineffective and more liberal than he lets on.\par \par \uc2 The contest in a state that former President Donald J. Trump comfortably won twice has remained more competitive than national observers initially expected with Mr. Ryan, a strong fund-raiser, casting himself as an independent voice. Still, he has an uphill climb, and he has not had much assistance so far from national Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Here are five takeaways from their heated hourlong debate in Youngstown, Ohio:\par \par \uc2 A strikingly contentious and sometimes personal hour.\par \par \uc2 The debate crackled with condescension as the two men sparred, reflecting the close nature of the race. At times they referred to each other as ''pal,'' ''our guy'' or ''buddy.'' Mr. Ryan even accused his opponent, in two languages, of being a fraud.\par \par \uc2 ''J.D. Vance, all due respect, is a fraud,'' Mr. Ryan said as they discussed immigration. Then he invoked a saying from his ''little Italian grandmother'' that he said translated into, '''You have two faces.' One for the camera, and one for your business dealings.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Vance, for his part, argued that Mr. Ryan was only masquerading as a moderate, and that, for all of his years in government, he had not delivered.\par \par \uc2 Each man tried to saddle the other with the problems of their national parties.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Vance sought to paint Mr. Ryan as a generic Democrat closely tied to President Biden as well as House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  -- whom, Mr. Ryan noted, he once challenged for a leadership role.\par \par \uc2 ''You can't run from the policies that she supported, that she has shoved down the throat of the people in Ohio,'' Mr. Vance said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Ryan declared, ''If you want to run against \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , move back to San Francisco and run against \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 ,'' alluding to Mr. Vance's time there as a venture capitalist.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Ryan, for his part, sought to tie Mr. Vance to the far-right congresswoman \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 , who has campaigned with Mr. Vance. And he looked for ways to highlight his independence from his party, saying, for instance, that ''I disagree with President Biden when he's talking about relaxing some of the regulations down on the border, completely disagree with that.''\par \par \uc2 He also sharply criticized Mr. Vance's evolution in supporting Mr. Trump. ''You were calling Trump America's Hitler,'' he said, adding later that ''then he endorsed you and you said he's the greatest president of all time.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Vance disputed the characterization. When he was pressed to name an area of disagreement with Mr. Trump, he expressed concerns with some of the more hawkish members of the administration.\par \par \uc2 ''The thing that was wrong about the Trump administration is they put a lot of people in the administration, a lot of bad personnel folks, who actually advocated limitless nonstop wars,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 A fight over what it means to be extreme on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Ohio became the center of a debate over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights earlier this year when a 10-year-old girl who had been raped traveled across state lines to receive an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  because of Ohio's ban on the procedure in many instances. The issue was a flash point in the debate.\par \par \uc2 ''J.D. and his extreme crew, they want to have a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban,'' Mr. Ryan said as he pushed for codifying Roe v. Wade, which the Supreme Court overturned in June. ''They're not happy with people having to go to Illinois. They want people to get a passport and have to go to Canada. Largest governmental overreach in the history of our lifetime.''\par \par \uc2 It was language reminiscent of the privacy-focused message used in Kansas by defenders of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights ahead of a ballot measure on the issue this summer.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Vance has said the 10-year-old should have been able to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and tried to cast Mr. Ryan as being extreme in his support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. He also tried to change the subject by noting that the man who was arrested and charged in the Ohio case was an undocumented immigrant. A spokesman for U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement previously confirmed his undocumented status.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Vance, in an extraordinary leap, tried to pin the blame for the attack on Mr. Ryan: ''She would never have been raped in the first place if \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  had done his job on border security.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Vance spoke supportively of Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 's proposed 15-week national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, but like many Republicans, was reluctant to discuss specifics on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  exceptions he supported, saying that, for example, while many support exceptions in instances of incest, ''an incest exception looks different at three weeks of pregnancy versus 39 weeks of pregnancy.''\par \par \uc2 A question about ''great replacement theory'' led to the most raw exchange of the night.\par \par \uc2 The candidates were asked about a racist conspiracy theory that concerns the notion of white people being replaced by nonwhite people and has helped inspire mass shootings across the country.\par \par \uc2 Replacement theory has crept into right-wing media, most notably \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2 's show on Fox News, where Mr. Vance has been a favorite guest. Mr. Ryan lashed Mr. Vance for spending time with lawmakers who ''stoke this racial violence'' as he described the role the theory played in the Buffalo mass shooting.\par \par \uc2 ''You are so desperate for political power that you'll accuse me, the father of three beautiful biracial babies, of engaging in racism,'' shot back Mr. Vance, who added that he is ''married to the daughter of South Asian immigrants.''\par \par \uc2 He has warned of an ''invasion'' from immigrants, and in a campaign ad, suggested loose border policies ensured ''more Democrat voters pouring into this country.'' But he said on Monday that the issue was not ''about whites or nonwhites,'' but that he was concerned about illegal immigration. ''You can believe in a border without being a racist,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 ''I would never talk about your family, J.D.,'' Mr. Ryan said. ''You don't want to talk about the fact that you're with the extremists in that belief.''\par \par \uc2 ''That's disgusting and I've never endorsed it,'' Mr. Vance insisted.\par \par \uc2 Senator Rob Portman was the most popular person onstage.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Vance and Mr. Ryan are vying to replace Mr. Portman, the center-right Ohio Republican who is retiring -- and while he has endorsed Mr. Vance, both invoked him repeatedly.\par \par \uc2 For Mr. Ryan, doing so signals his interest in bipartisanship. For Mr. Vance, who won the primary with strength from the right, it is a nod to the mainstream.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Vance repeatedly invoked the endorsement. Mr. Ryan noted times when they have collaborated.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm not quite sure why Rob Portman endorsed you,'' Mr. Ryan said at one point. ''You don't agree with any of the compromises that he's been able to make in the last year.''\par \par \uc2 Trip Gabriel contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Trip Gabriel contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Supporters for the candidates outside the auditorium in Youngstown, Ohio, before the one-hour debate began on Monday. (PHOTOGRAPH BY GAELEN MORSE FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A13. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020221018eiai0004h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
