\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Is Motivating Voters, but Republicans Are Trying to Change the Subject\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lisa Lerer and Katie Glueck \par \uc2 1,983 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 October 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 G.O.P. candidates have been changing their positions, erasing sections about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from their websites and trying to focus on topics like inflation, crime and immigration.\par \par \uc2 In Wisconsin, \uc2 Tim Michels\uc2 , a Republican running for governor, promised activists that he would never ''flip-flop'' on his support for an 1849 law that bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  except when a woman's life is threatened. Less than three weeks later, he changed his stance.\par \par \uc2 In the Phoenix suburbs, staffers whisked away Juan Ciscomani, a Republican House candidate, citing an urgent text, after he was asked by a voter whether he supported \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans.\par \par \uc2 And in New Hampshire, Don Bolduc, the Republican running for Senate, described \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a distraction from the ''really important issues.''\par \par \uc2 In races across the nation, Republican candidates are waffling on their \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions, denying past behavior or simply trying to avoid a topic that has long been a bedrock principle of American conservatism. Less than a month before the midterm elections on Nov. 8, the party lacks a unified policy on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , unable to broadly adopt a consistent response in the three and a half months since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Republican positioning on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drew renewed attention last week, when \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , the party's Senate nominee in Georgia, was accused by an ex-girlfriend of paying for one \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and unsuccessfully urging her to get a second one. Mr. Walker takes a hard-line stance against the procedure, supporting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans with no exceptions for rape, incest or to save the life of the mother.\par \par \uc2 For decades, Republicans pushed to overturn federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, viewing the issue as an easy rallying cry to identify with a culturally conservative base. Focusing on the country's highest court allowed them to largely avoid difficult, life-or-death issues -- life-threatening pregnancy complications, exceptions for child rape, diagnoses of rare and fatal conditions in fetuses. And given that few voters fully believed Roe would be overturned, they were rarely pressed on the specifics of their views.\par \par \uc2 The court ruled in June that each state can formulate its own \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, exactly what small-government conservatives had long wanted. But it had another consequence, plunging the party into months of politically toxic debates.\par \par \uc2 ''You hear some of these Republican state legislators, and it's like, for the first time they are thinking about this and realize that this is a complicated issue with lots and lots of circumstances that are not black and white,'' said Christine Matthews, a pollster who has worked for Republicans. ''A lot of these male legislators are realizing, 'Oh, this is really hard to legislate.'''\par \par \uc2 To escape some of those difficult questions, many Republican candidates have been trying to avoid the debate altogether. For weeks, some Republicans have been erasing sections about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from their websites, changing their positions on state bans and trying to refocus the national conversation on inflation, crime and the country's southern border.\par \par \uc2 ''I do believe it's caught them slightly off guard with just how bad an issue this is for them,'' said Sarah Longwell, an anti-Trump Republican strategist who leads focus groups. ''The party has opted for changing the conversation entirely because \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is just bad terrain for them.''\par \par \uc2 Some party leaders and strategists have urged candidates to adopt poll-tested positions popular with large swaths of independent voters: No restrictions on contraception, no bans before about 15 weeks and including exceptions for rape, incest and life of the mother. But those policies are a compromise position, falling short of the long-held goal of the party's socially conservative wing to end all abortions. They also clash with some of the past language and positions of Republican candidates.\par \par \uc2 That has left candidates, particularly those in purple states, caught between the more moderate views of independent voters and a conservative base that views the court's ruling as the beginning of restrictions, not the end. Now, many of the party's candidates in the most competitive contests are racing to recast their positions.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm winning because people see a strong leader, a man of conviction, a man who doesn't waffle, a man who doesn't flip-flop,'' Mr. Michels, the Republican nominee for governor in Wisconsin, told Republican activists and officials on Sept. 6 about the state ban. ''I'm going to stick with what I know is right.''\par \par \uc2 But within weeks, he reversed his stance on opposing expanding exceptions to a Wisconsin \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, saying that, if elected, he would sign legislation to include rape and incest.\par \par \uc2 Many of the pivots have been even less artful. In Maine, a former governor, Paul LePage, is running to lead the state again and repeatedly stumbled over a question about whether he would sign more restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws if elected. ''I don't know what you mean by 15 weeks, 28 weeks. Because I don't know,'' Mr. LePage said after a protracted exchange on a debate stage last week.\par \par \uc2 And in Arizona, a spokesman for Kari Lake, the Republican nominee for governor, had to clarify last week that Ms. Lake was not advocating changes to the state's near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban after she told a Phoenix talk-radio host that the procedure should be ''rare and legal.''\par \par \uc2 In an interview with CBS News on Sunday, Ms. Lake said she was trying to articulate how far the Democratic Party had moved from its Clinton-era talking points of ''safe, legal and rare,'' asserting that the procedure has become ''anything but rare.'' But she refused to say whether she would pursue restrictions on abortions sooner than 15 weeks into pregnancy, diverting the conversation to adoption and falsely casting her Democratic opponent as supportive of ''\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  right up until birth.''\par \par \uc2 Her remarks follow guidance circulated by some party strategists who are urging their candidates to flip the script, labeling Democrats as the ''extremists'' on the issue. That message has been adopted by a number of high-profile Republican Senate candidates in recent debates, including \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2  in North Carolina, Blake Masters in Arizona and J.D. Vance in Ohio.\par \par \uc2 ''We've been encouraged to see pro-life Republicans in high-profile races increasingly use this strategy to show contrast to their advantage,'' said Mallory Carroll, a spokeswoman for Susan B. Anthony Pro-Life America, an advocacy group that opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 But other Republican strategists and party officials argue that the potency of the issue is fading as economic concerns grow more intense, and they are urging candidates to focus instead on rising prices and violent crime.\par \par \uc2 ''To sustain that level of interest and enthusiasm in the current political climate for five months is very difficult, especially with more pressing personal pocketbook issues hurting voters,'' said \uc2 Robert Blizzard\uc2 , a Republican pollster engaged in a number of midterm races.\par \par \uc2 Mark Graul, a longtime Republican strategist based in Wisconsin, said that right after the Supreme Court decision, the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue was ''very much front and center.''\par \par \uc2 But in the final weeks of the race, Mr. Graul said, voters are saying, '''I care about that, but I care about how much it costs to fill up my car and buy groceries. And is my family going to be safe?''' He added: ''I think they're starting to care about that more.''\par \par \uc2 While polls show that the majority of voters support a federal right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Democrats are not favored to maintain control of Congress, given still-high inflation, concerns about crime and President Biden's low approval ratings.\par \par \uc2 Still, Democrats are trying to ensure that Republicans cannot escape so easily. After decades of treating the issue as a second-tier priority, the Democratic Party has made \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights a centerpiece of its fall campaign, spending nearly $213 million to blanket the airwaves with ads about it, according to AdImpact, an advertising-tracking firm.\par \par \uc2 Celinda Lake, a veteran Democratic pollster and strategist, called the political debate over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights ''the best thing going for the Democrats.''\par \par \uc2 ''It can't be the only thing going for the Democrats,'' she added. But many Republicans, she said, are ''having a lot of difficulty'' discussing the issue.\par \par \uc2 The need to square decades of opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights with the new political environment has led to some complicated contortions for Republicans, some of whom have tried to cast themselves less as drivers of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans and more as bystanders.\par \par \uc2 Representative \uc2 Don Bacon\uc2  of Nebraska, a Republican who faces a tough race for re-election, said he supported not only the 15-week federal ban but prohibiting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  starting at conception. But Mr. Bacon also argues that such a policy would never pass the Senate because it would be unable to garner the 60 votes necessary to overcome a filibuster -- essentially telling voters not to worry about his positions because they will be blocked by Democrats.\par \par \uc2 ''Whether we have a pro-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  majority in the House and Senate, or a pro-life majority in the Senate or House, you're not going to get past a 60-vote threshold in the Senate,'' he said, in an interview on NBC's ''Meet The Press.'' ''So the reality is, most of this is going to be done at the state level.''\par \par \uc2 In his primary race, \uc2 Joe Lombardo\uc2 , the sheriff of the Las Vegas area who is running for Nevada governor, summarized his position on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  with three words: ''Joe is pro-life.''\par \par \uc2 But a 747-word note published on his campaign website late last month reversed his stance on an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rule in Nevada. He said he would not repeal an executive order protecting women from being prosecuted for seeking an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state, which has emerged as a safe haven for the procedure as neighboring Utah, Arizona and Idaho have restricted access.\par \par \uc2 An ad by a conservative group in Nevada echoes that argument, accusing Democrats of ''scaring'' voters about the state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws and saying politicians cannot change the rules allowing the procedure until 24 weeks.\par \par \uc2 The claims by Mr. Lombardo and the group ignore the power of executive orders to add new restrictions and the possibility that Congress could pass a national ban, superseding state law with a stricter federal standard.\par \par \uc2 Not all Republicans have been so quick to finesse their stances.\par \par \uc2 A campaign ad released last week by Jeff Crossman, the Democratic candidate for Ohio attorney general, takes aim at the Republican incumbent and his public questioning of the existence of a 10-year-old rape victim who left the state for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The child was blocked from obtaining an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Ohio because she was three days past a six-week limit on abortions. The attorney general, \uc2 Dave Yost\uc2 , initially said the report was likely to be a ''fabrication.''\par \par \uc2 ''\uc2 Dave Yost\uc2 , you disgust me,'' a woman identified only as Geri of Northeast Ohio says to the camera in the ad. ''When a 10-year-old was raped and impregnated, Yost went on national TV and called it a hoax? I am a grandmother, and I have a 10-year-old granddaughter.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Yost has resisted calls to apologize for doubting the victim. ''I don't understand what you think I need to apologize for,'' he said in an interview with a local television program. ''We didn't even know the identity, and still don't, of that poor victim.''\par \par \uc2 Tim Michels, above, a Republican running for governor, reversed his stance on exceptions to a Wisconsin \uc2  ban. Kari Lake, below, the Republican nominee for governor in Arizona, on Sunday refused to say where she stood on certain restrictions. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY JAMIE KELTER DAVIS FOR THE NEW YORK TIME; ADRIANA 