\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 When Debates For Key Races Will Take Place\b0\par\par\uc2 By Alyce McFadden \par \uc2 1,085 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 October 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 18\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 With less than a month until Election Day, candidates are meeting for debates. Here's what has taken place so far and how to watch the debates to come.\par \par \uc2 With less than a month to go until Election Day, candidates in the most competitive races of this midterm season are hashing out their differences in debates. Read about those that have taken place so far, and see where and when to watch the events to come.\par \par \uc2 Monday, Oct. 10: Ohio\par \par \uc2 The candidates for Senate in Ohio, Representative \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 , a Democrat, and J.D. Vance, a Republican, will debate at 7 p.m. Eastern time. A livestream of the debate will be available online.\par \par \uc2 Wednesday, Oct. 12: Maryland, Massachusetts, New Mexico\par \par \uc2 Maryland's candidates for governor, Dan Cox, a Republican, and Wes Moore, a Democrat, will debate at 7 p.m. Eastern time. A livestream will be available to Maryland residents online.\par \par \uc2 In Massachusetts, the candidates for governor, \uc2 Geoff Diehl\uc2 , a Republican, and Maura Healey, a Democrat, will debate at 7 p.m. Eastern time. The event will be streamed live online.\par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 Michelle Lujan Grisham\uc2  of New Mexico, a Democrat, will debate her Republican challenger, Mark Ronchetti, at 8:30 p.m. Eastern time.\par \par \uc2 Thursday, Oct. 13: Colorado, Michigan, Wisconsin\par \par \uc2 Candidates for a newly created House seat in Colorado will debate at 8:30 p.m. Eastern time. A livestream of the matchup, between Yadira Caraveo, a Democrat, and Barbara Kirkmeyer, a Republican, will be available online.\par \par \uc2 In Michigan, Gov. \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 , a Democrat, will debate Tudor Dixon, a Republican, at 7 p.m. Eastern time. It will be streamed live.\par \par \uc2 Senator Ron Johnson of Wisconsin, a Republican, and his Democratic challenger, Lt. Gov. Mandela Barnes, will debate for a second time at 7:30 p.m. Eastern time. A livestream will be available online.\par \par \uc2 Friday, Oct. 14: Georgia, Wisconsin\par \par \uc2 Senator Raphael Warnock of Georgia and his Republican challenger, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , will debate at 7 p.m. Eastern time. The debate will be hosted by WSAV in Savannah and aired on other TV stations in the state.\par \par \uc2 In Wisconsin, Gov. \uc2 Tony Evers\uc2 , a Democrat, and \uc2 Tim Michels\uc2 , a Republican, will debate at 8 p.m. Eastern time. A livestream will be available online.\par \par \uc2 Monday, Oct. 17: Georgia, Iowa, Utah\par \par \uc2 In Georgia, Gov. \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  and his Democratic challenger, \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , will debate at 7 p.m. Eastern time. A livestream will be available on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and online.\par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 Kim Reynolds\uc2  of Iowa, a Republican, will debate Deirdre DeJear, a Democrat, at 8 p.m. Eastern time. It will be streamed live.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Mike Lee\uc2  of Utah, a Republican, will debate an independent challenger, Evan McMullin, at 8 p.m. Eastern time. A livestream will be available online.\par \par \uc2 Tuesday, Oct. 18: Illinois, Minnesota\par \par \uc2 In Illinois, Gov. \uc2 J.B. Pritzker\uc2 , a Democrat, will debate a Republican challenger, \uc2 Darren Bailey\uc2 , at 8 p.m. Eastern time. The debate will be aired on WGN-TV in Chicago and other stations across the state.\par \par \uc2 Minnesota's governor, \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , will debate Scott Jensen, a Republican, at 8 p.m. Eastern time. A livestream will be available online.\par \par \uc2 Monday, Oct. 24: Florida\par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  of Florida, a Republican, will debate his Democratic challenger, Representative \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2 , at 7 p.m. Eastern time. The debate was originally scheduled for Oct. 12 but was postponed because of Hurricane Ian.\par \par \uc2 Tuesday, Oct. 25: Pennsylvania\par \par \uc2 Lt. Gov. \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , a Democrat, and Dr. Mehmet Oz, a Republican, are running for Senate in Pennsylvania and will debate at 8 p.m. Eastern time. A livestream will be available online.\par \par \uc2 Thursday, Oct. 27: Maine\par \par \uc2 In Maine, Gov. Janet T. Mills, a Democrat, will debate former Gov. Paul LePage, a Republican. The debate will begin at 7 p.m. Eastern time.\par \par \uc2 Friday, Oct. 28: Colorado, Minnesota\par \par \uc2 Senator Michael Bennet of Colorado, a Democrat, will debate Joe O'Dea, a Republican, at 9 p.m. Eastern time. The event will be streamed live.\par \par \uc2 The candidates for governor of Minnesota, Mr. Walz and Mr. Jensen, will debate again at 8 p.m. Eastern time. A livestream will be available online.\par \par \uc2 Past debates\par \par \uc2 Friday, Oct. 7: North Carolina, Wisconsin\par \par \uc2 Cheri Beasley, a Democratic former chief justice of the State Supreme Court, and Representative \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2 , who are competing for a Senate seat in North Carolina, met for a debate in Raleigh. Mr. Budd, a Republican, tried to paint the race as a referendum on President Biden, while Ms. Beasley sought to tie her opponent to election denialism and former President Donald J. Trump.\par \par \uc2 Read: 'The Key Issues That Defined North Carolina's Senate Debate'\par \par \uc2 Mr. Johnson and Mr. Barnes previously met for a debate in Madison that put their ideological differences on full display: Mr. Barnes embraced progressive ideas like marijuana legalization and the defense of \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2  protesters, while Mr. Johnson derided efforts to curb climate change.\par \par \uc2 Read: 'Five Takeaways From the Wisconsin Senate Debate'\par \par \uc2 Thursday, Oct. 6: Arizona, Illinois\par \par \uc2 Senator Mark Kelly of Arizona, a Democrat, and \uc2 Blake Masters\uc2 , his Republican challenger, met for a debate in Phoenix, where the topics included \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , immigration and California's water use.\par \par \uc2 Read: 'Five Takeaways From the Arizona Senate Debate'\par \par \uc2 Mr. Pritzker and Mr. Bailey debated in Normal, Ill., as part of their contest for governor. Mr. Bailey pressed Mr. Pritzker, whose presidential ambitions are no secret, to pledge to serve out all four years of his term if re-elected. Moderators asked Mr. Bailey to explain comments that compared \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the Holocaust.\par \par \uc2 Read: 'In Illinois Governor's Debate, Bailey Tries to Put Pritzker on Defensive'\par \par \uc2 Wednesday, Oct. 5: Kansas\par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 Laura Kelly\uc2  of Kansas, a Democrat, and her Republican opponent, Derek Schmidt, the state attorney general, met for a debate in Kansas City. Mr. Schmidt danced around the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , saying that while he preferred ''a Kansas that has fewer abortions, not more,'' he would respect the outcome of an August referendum that preserved \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Read: 'G.O.P. Governor Candidate in Kansas Walks \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Tightrope in a Debate'\par \par \uc2 Tuesday, Oct. 4: Maine\par \par \uc2 Ms. Mills and Mr. LePage met before in a debate in Lewiston. Mr. LePage struggled to answer a question from a moderator about whether he would veto additional restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if a Republican legislature were to pass them.\par \par \uc2 Read: 'LePage Stumbles on \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Questioning in Maine Governor's Debate'\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A18. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020221011eiab00040\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict