\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Candid Talk By First Lady On  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  A Friend Had\b0\par\par\uc2 By Katie Rogers \par \uc2 912 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 October 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 15\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The first lady said she had once helped a friend recover from an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before there was a constitutional right to the procedure. ''Women will not let this country go backwards,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Jill Biden, the first lady, said on Friday that she had once helped a friend recover from an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before there was a constitutional right to the procedure, evoking the issue in deeply personal terms at a political fund-raiser as she warned of further restrictions from ''extremist Republicans.''\par \par \uc2 Dr. Biden, who was introduced by Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  before speaking to a group of donors in San Francisco, said that in the late 1960s -- years before the Supreme Court's decision in Roe v. Wade established a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- a friend got pregnant. At that time, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was outlawed in Pennsylvania, where Dr. Biden grew up.\par \par \uc2 Her friend, whom she did not name, told her that she had undergone a psychological evaluation to be declared mentally unfit before a doctor agreed to administer one.\par \par \uc2 ''I went to see her in the hospital and then cried the whole drive home,'' said Dr. Biden, who said she was 17 at the time. ''When she was discharged from the hospital, she couldn't go back to her house, so I gathered my courage and asked my mom, 'Can she come stay with us?'''\par \par \uc2 Dr. Biden, now 71, said that her mother, Bonny Jean Jacobs, allowed her friend to visit and that the two kept it a secret. Mrs. Jacobs died in 2008.\par \par \uc2 ''Secrecy. Shame. Silence. Danger. Even death,'' Dr. Biden said. ''That's what defined that time for so many women.''\par \par \uc2 President Biden, a Roman Catholic who has struggled with his views over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, often connects his argument to the broader right for Americans to make private medical decisions. In speeches and public statements, he uses the word ''\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 '' sparingly, focusing instead on broader phrases, like ''\b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2 '' and ''the right to choose,'' that might resonate more widely with the public.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Biden has also been judicious with her use of the word. But her story, shared publicly for the first time, cast the issue in a personal light as Democrats seek to capitalize on voter anger over the Supreme Court's overturning of Roe v. Wade this summer to hold onto Congress in the November midterm elections. As \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans have taken effect in more than a dozen states, there are already signs that the issue has helped buoy the party against rampant inflation and Mr. Biden's poor approval ratings.\par \par \uc2 ''I was shocked when the Dobbs decision came out,'' Dr. Biden said, referring to the case that overturned Roe. ''It was devastating -- how could we go back to that time?\par \par \uc2 ''I thought of all the girls and women, like my friend, whose education, careers and future depended on the ability to choose when they have children,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 After decades of marriage to Mr. Biden, the first lady, who teaches full-time at a community college in Virginia, has evolved into an avid campaigner whose remarks often carry a personal touch.\par \par \uc2 Like her husband, she has often avoided confrontational language when talking about the Republican Party in public. (During Mr. Biden's presidential campaign, Dr. Biden and her aides had decided that they could draw a contrast between her husband and former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  just by describing her husband, rather than attacking Mr. Trump directly.)\par \par \uc2 Still, both Bidens have started to take a more aggressive stance toward Republicans, who have broadly backed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions, even as they have struggled to unite around the idea of a national ban. In her remarks, Dr. Biden repeatedly called their agenda ''extremist.''\par \par \uc2 ''But here's the thing that those extremists don't understand about women,'' she said. ''This isn't the first time that we've been underestimated. It's not the first time that someone has tried to tell us what we can and can't do.''\par \par \uc2 As the midterms grow closer, Dr. Biden is expected to ramp up her traveling and deliver speeches related to her own portfolio of issues, including cancer research, education and support for the military. But she will also emphasize fund-raising and supporting Democrats in tight races, according to a person familiar with her plans.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, the fund-raiser, which raised money for congressional Democrats, starting at $500 a plate, was tucked between a visit to a cancer research center and a Saturday event focused on military families in Seattle, where she plans to appear with Senator \uc2 Patty Murray\uc2  of Washington.\par \par \uc2 During the event, Dr. Biden urged supporters to ''defend congressional seats held by women like Teresa and Mary'' -- referring to Representative \uc2 Teresa Leger Fernandez\uc2  of New Mexico, a swing-district Democrat, and Representative Mary Peltola of Alaska, a Democrat who won an August special election to replace Don Young, a Republican who died in March after serving there for 49 years.\par \par \uc2 ''Women will not let this country go backwards,'' Dr. Biden said. ''We've fought too hard for too long. And we know that there is just too much on the line.''\par \par \uc2 Jill Biden, the first lady, speaking during a visit to a cancer center in San Francisco. During the trip, she also went to a fund-raiser where she discussed \uc2  rights. (PHOTOGRAPH BY Pool photo by Jeff Chiu FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020221008eia80003p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
