\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Judges in Ohio and Arizona Block States'  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Bans\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ava Sasani \par \uc2 920 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 October 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The decisions offered a window into which legal arguments might be working in the broader strategy to re-establish \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights through state courts.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters won two temporary victories on Friday when judges in Ohio and Arizona suspended state laws banning the procedures.\par \par \uc2 In Ohio, a county judge indefinitely suspended a state law prohibiting most abortions after six weeks of pregnancy. A few hours later, an appeals court in Arizona temporarily blocked its pre-statehood law banning the procedure.\par \par \uc2 The decisions marked progress for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates who have been fighting to restore access to the procedure in states that ban it.\par \par \uc2 The Ohio decision extends an earlier, temporary suspension of the law that was set to expire next week. The ruling means that the state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban is suspended while the court case proceeds, providing a bit more certainty for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers and women.\par \par \uc2 Without the ban in effect, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Ohio is legal up to 22 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Since the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  overturned the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  this summer, states have been free to regulate the procedure. More than a dozen, including Ohio, have passed laws banning most abortions. Some include narrow exceptions for rape, incest or if a pregnant woman's life is in danger.\par \par \uc2 Those in favor of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights have worked to overturn bans and restrictions by suing in state courts.\par \par \uc2 Attorney General David Yost of Ohio, who supports the six-week ban, could still appeal the case to a higher court.\par \par \uc2 ''We will wait and review the judge's actual written order and consult with the governor's administration,'' on next steps, said a spokesman for Mr. Yost.\par \par \uc2 The ruling in Arizona blocks a near-total ban dating back to 1864. The strict law was reinstated last month by a lower court in Arizona. Though Friday's ruling temporarily restored the injunction on the 158-year-old ban, a law passed this year restricting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 15 weeks of pregnancy is in effect.\par \par \uc2 In the court's brief, Presiding Judge Peter J. Eckerstrom wrote that the lower court erred by failing to consider Arizona's more recent \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws, which attorneys for Planned Parenthood say conflict with the pre-statehood ban.\par \par \uc2 ''Arizona courts have a responsibility to attempt to harmonize all of this state's relevant statutes,'' the judge wrote.\par \par \uc2 Friday's decisions, though not the final word on the cases, offered a window into which legal arguments might be working in the broader strategy to re-establish \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights through state courts.\par \par \uc2 The plaintiffs in the Ohio case, a collective of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers including Planned Parenthood of Greater Ohio, argued that Ohio's state Constitution offered ''broad protections for individual liberties,'' which includes the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Ohio law, passed in 2019, went into effect this year after the Supreme Court decision and remained in effect for over two months.\par \par \uc2 In his opinion, Judge Christian Jenkins, of the Hamilton County Court of Common Pleas, argued that the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was found in the Ohio Constitution, saying, ''\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is health care.''\par \par \uc2 ''Ohio's Constitution specifically and unambiguously recognized as fundamental the right to liberty,'' Judge Jenkins said, adding that the state Constitution guaranteed the right to ''seek and obtain safety.''\par \par \uc2 State officials had argued that the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not explicitly found in Ohio's Constitution.\par \par \uc2 Judge Jenkins, a Democrat, said that the state was ''simply wrong'' to argue ''that a right does not exist because it is not specifically listed in the Constitution.''\par \par \uc2 ''By the state's reasoning, the Obergefell decision recognizing the right to gay marriage is wrong,'' the judge said, referring to the 2015 Supreme Court decision that made same-sex marriage a nationwide right.\par \par \uc2 The Ohio law restricts \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after fetal cardiac activity can be detected, which is generally six weeks after the start of a pregnant woman's last period, and before many women realize they are pregnant.\par \par \uc2 The law does not include exceptions for rape and incest, a feature that drew national attention this summer when a 10-year-old Ohio girl who had become pregnant through rape was denied an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in her home state.\par \par \uc2 The plaintiffs argued that the Ohio law deprived women of liberty and due process rights, citing several examples of unnamed women. They included a cancer patient who had to travel out of state for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to affidavits submitted by a Cleveland \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic. The patient, who had stage III melanoma, ''broke down and cried'' after learning she would have to travel outside of the state for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  so she could begin cancer treatment. Cancer treatments can be harmful to the fetus, so patients are often given the option of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before beginning treatment.\par \par \uc2 ''Does a law that prevents a cancer patient from getting lifesaving treatment infringe on those rights? The answer is obviously it does,'' Judge Jenkins said. The plaintiffs said they were ''relieved that patients in Ohio can continue to access \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as we work to fight this unjust and dangerous ban in court.''\par \par \uc2 ''The preliminary injunction will be in place for the duration of our case, which means abortions will be legal in Ohio for a period much, much longer'' than the initial suspension, they added.\par \par \uc2 A June protest in support of \uc2  rights in Columbus, Ohio. The state's ban on abor- tion was suspended on Friday. (PHOTOGRAPH BY MEGAN JELINGER/REUTERS) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020221008eia80003b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
