\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Are Democrats Gaining Steam? A Tightening New Jersey Race Offers Hints.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Tracey Tully \par \uc2 1,575 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 October 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 11\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 If Democrats have a chance of holding onto the House, their path runs through districts like \uc2 Tom Malinowski\uc2 's.\par \par \uc2 SCOTCH PLAINS, N.J. -- When New Jersey's congressional map was redrawn last year, Representative \uc2 Tom Malinowski\uc2 , a second-term Democrat, was widely considered a political goner.\par \par \uc2 President Biden's popularity had plummeted, gas prices were soaring and Mr. Malinowski's Seventh Congressional District -- in which he barely eked out a re-election victory in 2020 -- had been redrawn to include nearly 27,000 more registered Republicans. When Mr. Malinowski announced he would run for a third term, he did so in a terse statement, quoting an ominous Shakespearean battle cry: ''Once more unto the breach, dear friends, once more.''\par \par \uc2 But 10 months later, as voters have absorbed the impact of the Supreme Court decision overturning the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , there are signs that Democrats believe the national political momentum has shifted to a degree that even this race, written off by some as a strategic sacrifice, is narrowing.\par \par \uc2 Any path by which Democrats are able to stave off a midterm rout or retain a slim House majority cuts straight through districts like Mr. Malinowski's, where moderate, well-educated voters helped Democrats win control of the House in 2018 and are seen as crucial to holding it.\par \par \uc2 ''I do see it as a bit of a bellwether -- an indicator of how things are going to go nationally,'' said Mike DuHaime, a Republican strategist who was a key architect of former Gov. \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 's victories in 2009 and 2013.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Malinowski is running for a second time against \uc2 Tom Kean Jr\uc2 ., the namesake of a beloved former New Jersey governor making his fourth run for Congress. Mr. Kean came within about 5,000 votes of winning in 2020 and remains a formidable opponent this year.\par \par \uc2 Still, a national political action committee dedicated to preserving the Democratic majority in the House has suddenly begun buying up its first television time for Malinowski ads. And Democratic loyalists who have been knocking on doors for Mr. Malinowski say concern over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights has grown palpable within the suburban swing district, which stretches from one side of northern New Jersey to the other.\par \par \uc2 ''I don't know a woman who isn't really angry and really scared,'' Jennifer Robinson of Tewksbury, N.J., who supports Mr. Malinowski, said on Sunday night after a forum with both candidates sponsored by the Jewish Federation of Greater MetroWest New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 ''Republicans targeted this race thinking \uc2 Tom Kean Jr\uc2 . was going to ride a red wave,'' said James Singer, a spokesman for the Democratic Congressional Campaign Committee. ''Instead, with five weeks to go, this race remains neck and neck.''\par \par \uc2 None of the major independent polling operations in New Jersey have released surveys about the race. A poll conducted in late July, paid for by a group that supports term limits, showed Mr. Kean leading by eight percentage points; 11 percent of the 400 people surveyed said they were undecided.\par \par \uc2 On Sunday, an internal poll memo released by Mr. Malinowski's campaign suggested that the race had narrowed, and that he and Mr. Kean were statistically tied, 48 percent to 48 percent.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kean's campaign spokesman dismissed the poll and called its release a ''desperate cry for help.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Malinowski and three other New Jersey Democrats rode a wave of anti-Trump fervor to Congress during the 2018 midterm cycle, temporarily leaving the state with just one Republican in its 12-person congressional delegation. But many of these newly blue swing districts remained highly competitive.\par \par \uc2 Last year, the new congressional map, redrawn to reflect the 2020 census, eased some of the pressure on Democrats. As it added Republican-leaning towns to Mr. Malinowski's district, it shored up the districts of several other vulnerable incumbents at a time when Democrats were bracing for a midterm shellacking.\par \par \uc2 The districts of Democratic Representatives \uc2 Josh Gottheimer\uc2 , \uc2 Andy Kim\uc2  and \uc2 Mikie Sherrill\uc2  all shed Republican-leaning towns -- territory that in southern and central New Jersey the state's two Republican congressmen, Christopher Smith and \uc2 Jeff Van Drew\uc2 , mainly absorbed, making their seats safer, too. Only Mr. Malinowski's race, on paper, got harder.\par \par \uc2 Yet until last month, the Democrats' House Majority PAC had not made ad buys for Mr. Malinowski's race, even as Republican special interest groups prepared to pump millions of dollars into Mr. Kean's.\par \par \uc2 But in late September the political action committee began booking television airtime, and it has now reserved between $100,000 and $185,000 in ads each week until Election Day, according to data maintained by Ad Impact, a company that tracks political advertising.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Malinowski's district includes affluent commuter towns close to New York City, communities filled with horse-country estates (and a former president's golf course) and rural, Republican bastions. Voters in the district backed Mr. Biden by less than four percentage points, even though he beat former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  by nearly 16 percentage points in New Jersey, where Democrats outnumber Republicans by just over one million voters.\par \par \uc2 Even if it has narrowed, the race remains a decidedly uphill battle for Mr. Malinowski.\par \par \uc2 Inflation has been stubborn, and consumers are still feeling an economic pinch -- an issue that a \uc2 Monmouth University\uc2  poll released on Monday found is likely to overshadow \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access as a motivator heading into the midterms. Only 42 percent of voters across the country support Mr. Biden, according to last month's New York Times/Siena College poll, a threshold that is just as bad or worse than any president whose party went on to lose control of Congress in midterm elections, going back to 1978.\par \par \uc2 And Mr. Malinowski remains under investigation by the House Ethics Committee over allegations he failed to properly disclose hundreds of thousands of dollars in stock trades, an error he has taken responsibility for and said resulted from carelessness.\par \par \uc2 ''It's better for Democrats than six months ago,'' Mr. DuHaime said. ''But it's still a better political environment for Republicans than it was two years ago -- and certainly four years ago.''\par \par \uc2 At the forum on Sunday, questions from an audience filled almost entirely with Malinowski supporters centered largely on Mr. Kean's position on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mr. Malinowski supports access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at any point in a pregnancy, and he said on Sunday that he would vote to enshrine a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  into federal law.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kean, a former state senator and assemblyman, has said he supported a ''woman's right to choose.'' But he opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 20 weeks of pregnancy absent extenuating circumstances, according to his campaign.\par \par \uc2 ''I think there are meaningful exceptions that should be rape, incest, life and the health of the mother,'' he said Sunday. ''Those are exceptions for a reasonable amount of time.''\par \par \uc2 In the Senate, he voted against a bill affirming \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a right in New Jersey. He said he opposed the legislation, which was later signed into law, because it permitted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at any point in a pregnancy, including what he called late-term \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Abortions after 21 weeks of pregnancy are rare, accounting for less than 1 percent of all abortions performed in the United States in 2019, according to the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A Kean campaign website is less nuanced.\par \par \uc2 ''Tom is a fierce defender of the sanctity of life, fighting every step of the way to protect the unborn from egregious \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws proposed in New Jersey, and will continue to do so in Congress,'' it reads.\par \par \uc2 ''When I'm talking about the egregious piece of legislation, the ability to choose to terminate, for not valid reasons, when a baby can stay alive, be alive, outside of the womb, is wrong,'' he said at the forum.\par \par \uc2 Of the 616,000 registered voters in the district, about a third are not enrolled in either major party. It is these moderate voters who tend to sway elections in New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 Motivating supporters to turn in mail ballots or to show up at polling places during an election year with no statewide races is crucial for any candidate, said \uc2 Debbie Walsh\uc2 , director of the Center for American Women and Politics at Rutgers.\par \par \uc2 ''Elections are about turnout,'' Ms. Walsh said. ''The people who turn out are the people who feel they have the most at stake.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Walsh, whose organization studies voting trends among women, said she believed the Supreme Court decision that overturned Roe v. Wade and ended nearly 50 years of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the United States would be an ''energizer.''\par \par \uc2 ''I think it all feels very real to people,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Tracy Keegan, a founder of Summit Marches On, a left-leaning group in Mr. Malinowski's district that formed after the 2017 Women's March and includes mainly women with children, said she believed the growing energy among voters extended beyond concern over \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''It's not just about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' she said. ''It's about a government's willingness to remove freedoms.''\par \par \uc2 A gun control rally in Summit, N.J., after the mass school shooting in Uvalde, Texas, drew hundreds of people, said Ms. Keegan. a 51-year-old mother of three.\par \par \uc2 ''It wasn't just Democrats,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Representative Tom Malinowski, left, and Tom Kean Jr. are neck-and-neck. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020221008eia80002w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
