\par\par \uc2 Movies, Performing Arts/Weekend Desk; SECTC\par \b \uc2 Battleground\b0\par\par\uc2 By Beatrice Loayza \par \uc2 444 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 October 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 8\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Months after Roe v. Wade was overturned, this documentary provides, if nothing else, a glimpse into the minds of the mobilized minority leading the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  charge.\par \par \uc2 Cynthia Lowen's straightforward documentary ''Battleground'' follows three prominent anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaigners in the few years leading up to 2022, when the Supreme Court ruled to overturn Roe v. Wade, the 1973 decision protecting a woman's right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The documentary is a cookie-cutter presentation intent on showing viewers how leaders of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement have managed to advance their goals and consolidate power by mobilizing an evangelical minority.\par \par \uc2 Appearing as talking heads, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights voices -- namely current and former representatives of Planned Parenthood -- are featured as well, but those women primarily retread familiar talking points: banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  threatens a woman's bodily autonomy, her health and her economic future. These segments do, however, provide some minimal context that cuts through the rhetoric of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists who are at the center of the film.\par \par \uc2 [Video: Watch on \uc2 YouTube\uc2 .]\par \par \uc2 Lowen spotlights the strategies employed by those anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups, who are composed not just of evangelical Christians, but also young people and people who profess to be neither religious nor conservative. The teenagers' cheery dedication comes off as cultlike, with Lowen doing her best to keep the camera's gaze strictly observant.\par \par \uc2 Of the three anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  leaders Lowen trails, Terrisa Bukovinac, the founder of Pro-Life San Francisco, is perhaps the most baffling. A self-proclaimed feminist and atheist who claims that women who support anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legislation can be promiscuous, too, she is the face of a modern anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign that understands that optics are everything. For her, the movement would benefit from distancing itself from its moralistic image, appearing instead more like an approachable lifestyle brand.\par \par \uc2 A recorded conversation between evangelical leaders and President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is played in bits and pieces throughout the film. It suggests a kind of devil's pact between those religious honchos and the former president's administration, one depicted as more concerned with amassing the support of single-issue voters and willing to assume their ideological position as a result. It's a haunting reality, and ''Battleground'' provides a glimpse into the minds of a minority leading the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  charge -- if nothing else.\par \par \uc2 Battleground\par \par \uc2 Not rated. Running time: 1 hour 45 minutes. In theaters.\par \par \uc2 Not rated. Running time: 1 hour 45 minutes. In theaters.\par \par \uc2 Activists on both sides of the \uc2  debate at the Supreme Court in the documentary ''Battleground.'' (PHOTOGRAPH BY Gabriella Garcia Pardo/Abramorama FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020221007eia70003b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
