\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 For G.O.P., 'Uphill' Race in Michigan Tests Wisdom of Embracing Trump\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jazmine Ulloa \par \uc2 1,924 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 October 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 22\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Tudor Dixon, the party's nominee for governor, has ground to make up in her race against Gov. \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 . She is hoping the former president can rally their party's base.\par \par \uc2 CLARKSTON, Mich. -- As she runs to lead a narrowly divided swing state, Tudor Dixon is pursuing a hazardous strategy in the Michigan governor's race: embracing Donald J. Trump, and at times emulating his no-holds-barred political style.\par \par \uc2 She hit the campaign trail recently with the former president's son \uc2 Donald Trump Jr\uc2 . and Kellyanne Conway, the onetime Trump White House adviser -- and, in Trumpian fashion, made headlines for mocking her Democratic opponent, Gov. \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 , over a 2020 kidnapping plot hatched against her by right-wing militia members.\par \par \uc2 In other appearances, Ms. Dixon called for a ban on transgender girls playing in girl's and women's sports. And on a recent afternoon at an athletic club in an affluent suburb northwest of Detroit, where a life-size cutout of Mr. Trump stood by the doors, she promoted his so-called America First business policies.\par \par \uc2 '''America First' -- Michigan First -- will bring Michigan back together,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 The governor's race between Ms. Dixon and Ms. Whitmer carries high stakes for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, schools and the future of elections. It is historic -- the first time two women have ever gone head-to-head for the position in the state.\par \par \uc2 The contest also serves as a test of whether Ms. Dixon and other Republican candidates can win their general elections by harnessing the grass-roots energy of Trump supporters that propelled them to the top of crowded and chaotic primaries. That approach -- which entails a close association with Mr. Trump's election denialism and other political baggage -- worries some Michigan Republicans who believe Ms. Dixon is failing to win over the kinds of suburban and independent voters who are crucial in tight races.\par \par \uc2 But it might be the only option she has. Early voting began on Thursday, and with time running out, Ms. Dixon is short on cash, well behind in polls, still working to shore up support among her Republican base and being pummeled by Democrats on the television airwaves.\par \par \uc2 ''Uphill, on icy roads,'' said Dennis Darnoi, a longtime Republican strategist in Michigan, describing her path to victory. ''It is a challenge, with a month left, for her to make up the kind of ground that she is going to need.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Dixon, who joined Mr. Trump at a rally on Saturday in Macomb County, has appeared unfazed, arguing that her recent fund-raising numbers have been high and that her message will ultimately resonate with voters more than Ms. Whitmer's.\par \par \uc2 Onstage on Saturday afternoon, Ms. Dixon pledged to protect women's sports and attacked Ms. Whitmer's pandemic and economic policies, suggesting the governor was hiding from voters. Her remarks at times elicited thunderous chants of ''Lock her up.'' Asked about the challenges ahead for her campaign after she spoke, her team pointed to a new poll from a Republican-aligned firm that put her within six points of Ms. Whitmer.\par \par \uc2 ''We feel great about it -- it means that their message is not resonating,'' Ms. Dixon said. ''She spent millions of dollars to try to take us out and still people are going to vote red in November.''\par \par \uc2 Not all Republicans who closely aligned themselves with Mr. Trump have struggled to pivot from the primary election to the general. In Arizona, the Republican nominee for governor, Kari Lake, has taken a similar approach, and has narrowed her race to a dead heat -- but unlike Ms. Dixon, she is not facing an incumbent governor like Ms. Whitmer.\par \par \uc2 Other candidates backed by Mr. Trump, like \uc2 Blake Masters\uc2  in Arizona's Senate race and \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2  in Pennsylvania's contest for governor, have fallen behind their Democratic opponents as they have struggled to raise money. Another Republican Senate hopeful, J.D. Vance, is facing a closer-than-expected race in Ohio.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump has maintained a keen interest in Michigan. He eked out a victory in the state in 2016 by fewer than 11,000 votes before losing to \uc2 Joseph R. Biden Jr\uc2 . in 2020 by more than 154,000 votes.\par \par \uc2 ''Six weeks from now the people of Michigan are going to vote to fire your radical left Democrat Governor \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 , and you are going to send a very good person, a very, very good woman, Tudor Dixon, to the governor's mansion,'' he said on Saturday in Warren, Mich., to cheers from the audience.\par \par \uc2 In a statement released after Ms. Dixon's remarks, Lavora Barnes, the chair of the Michigan Democratic Party, called her appearance ''divisive and desperate.''\par \par \uc2 ''Tonight, Michiganders saw a schoolyard bully on stage -- not a leader,'' Ms. Barnes said. ''Tudor Dixon hurled insults and rattled off a litany of grievances because she knows that her dangerous agenda to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and throw nurses in jail, dismantle public education and slash funding for law enforcement is out-of-step.''\par \par \uc2 Days before the Republican primary in early August, Mr. Trump endorsed Ms. Dixon, a conservative media personality backed by Michigan's powerful DeVos family.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Dixon, 45, a breast cancer survivor, worked as a steel industry executive until 2017, when she helped create Lumen Student News, a company that produces conservative TV news and history lessons for middle and high school students.\par \par \uc2 In a December 2021 radio interview, she said she aimed to restore students' faith in the country and combat what she described as ''indoctrination'' in schools. After helping found Lumen, Ms. Dixon went on to host a news show, ''America's Voice Live,'' on weekday afternoons.\par \par \uc2 On the stump, Ms. Dixon says she became a vocal critic of Ms. Whitmer's coronavirus restrictions as she witnessed their negative impact on Michigan's economy. The safety measures ''took a deeply personal turn,'' Ms. Dixon's website states, after her grandmother died in a Norton Shores nursing home that prohibited visits during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Dixon, who has the delivery of someone comfortable in front of an audience, has generated criticism for spreading unfounded claims about voter fraud in the 2020 election and for some of her stances on L.G.B.T.Q. issues, including calling for ''severe criminal penalties for adults who involve children in drag shows.''\par \par \uc2 On her website, she calls for a ban to prevent school employees from talking to children in kindergarten through third grade ''about sex and gender theory secretly behind their parents' backs.'' And she has said that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be allowed only if it is necessary to save the life of a mother, not in cases of rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Dixon's stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in particular -- in a state where voters tend to favor \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and that in November will weigh a ballot measure to enshrine the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state Constitution -- is a big reason that some Republicans are worried about her chances. They also fear that underperformance at the top of the ballot could cause the G.O.P. to lose control of the State Legislature.\par \par \uc2 Michigan's Republican Party has been in a state of turmoil for months.\par \par \uc2 The party's primary was defined by fierce infighting between its establishment and Trump factions. Its two front-runners for governor were disqualified for turning in petitions with thousands of forged signatures. Another candidate was charged with four misdemeanors related to the Capitol riot.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Dixon managed to rally her fractious party behind her in the race's final weeks. But even after winning the primary, she remained a relatively little-known political outsider. It did not help that at the G.O.P. state convention later in August, Republicans officially endorsed two preachers of 2020 election falsehoods for top state offices: Matthew DePerno for attorney general and Kristina Karamo for secretary of state.\par \par \uc2 The bruising battles, as well as the lack of financial networks and campaign experience among leading Republican candidates, have made for what Richard Czuba, an independent pollster in Lansing, Mich., called ''the worst ticket I have seen from any party in the last 40 years.''\par \par \uc2 ''It is great to run as an outsider, especially when you run against an incumbent,'' Mr. Czuba said. ''But there are two sides of that outsider coin. On the one hand, you can run as the outsider against the establishment. On the flip side, you don't know how to do this -- and that is what is showing.''\par \par \uc2 As the general election began, Democrats rushed to define Ms. Dixon before she had a chance to define herself. Ms. Whitmer had kept $14 million in her war chest by late August, after accounting for debts and expenditures. Ms. Dixon's end balance was $523,000, according to the state's latest campaign finance reports. Democratic groups have poured more than $41 million into television ads since the August primary, according to the firm AdImpact, which analyzes campaign ad spending. Republican groups, by contrast, have invested about $5.5 million.\par \par \uc2 State party leaders and national Republicans this week pushed back against any notion that the race was out of reach and that Ms. Dixon had been left to fend for herself. This past week, the Michigan Republican Party announced its largest ad push against Ms. Whitmer, seeking to paint her as ''soft on crime.'' Chris Gustafson, a spokesman for the \uc2 Republican Governors Association\uc2 , said it might to jump in with more ads soon.\par \par \uc2 For now, she continues to stump with far-right figures. At the Trump rally Saturday in Warren, Representative \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  of Georgia said Ms. Dixon had Mr. Trump's support and that she would be happy to raise funds for Ms. Dixon.\par \par \uc2 ''She is not by herself,'' Ms. Greene said. ''We are bringing in enforcements, and you can see us here today.''\par \par \uc2 At Ms. Dixon's event at the athletic club in Oakland County, a panel including former Trump administration officials sat against the tall glass walls of a serene, sunlit indoor pool, as they blasted Mr. Biden's economic policies and painted a harrowing picture of crime-filled American cities and unchecked immigration at the southwestern border.\par \par \uc2 In a short speech, Ms. Dixon slammed what she characterized as a ''radical sex and gender theory'' permeating schools and denounced Ms. Whitmer for providing tax incentives to bring a Chinese company to Michigan, rather than an American one.\par \par \uc2 But mostly, she displayed a rare dose of moderation, critiquing Mr. Whitmer's pandemic restrictions and economic policies, rising crime in the state's cities, and schools that Ms. Dixon argued had failed to adequately teach students to read and write. They were the kinds of remarks that some establishment and moderate Republicans might be hoping for -- and they also seemed to appease the people in the room.\par \par \uc2 Susan Savich, 64, and her 24-year-old son, Jonathan, asked to take photos with Ms. Dixon on her way out. They were opposed to schools teaching children anything but basic skills and traditional beliefs, they said, and Mr. Savich liked that Ms. Dixon was ''education first.''\par \par \uc2 They were also relieved to hear that Mr. Trump was coming to the state. ''Ms. Dixon is going up against a lot,'' Mr. Savich said.\par \par \uc2 Tudor Dixon, a Republican running a no-holds-barred race for governor in Michigan, is well behind in polls. (PHOTOGRAPH BY EMILY ELCONIN FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020221002eia20003g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
