\par\par \uc2 Foreign Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 She Could Soon Lead Italy, but Not All Women Are Happy About That\b0\par\par\uc2 By Elisabetta Povoledo and Gaia Pianigiani \par \uc2 1,697 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 September 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 11\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Some fear that the hard-right politician, whose party is expected to be the big winner in the election on Sunday, will continue policies that have kept women back.\par \par \uc2 ROME -- Being a woman, and mother, has been central to the political pitch of \uc2 Giorgia Meloni\uc2 , the hard-right politician who is likely to become Italy's prime minister after elections on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 She once ran for mayor seven months pregnant because she said powerful men had told her she couldn't. Her most famous speech includes the refrain ''I am a woman. I am a mother.'' She often talks with pride about how she started a party, Brothers of Italy, and rose to the top of national politics without any special treatment.\par \par \uc2 But as happy as women's rights activists are about that fact that a woman could finally run Italy, many wish it was essentially any other woman in Italy. They fear that Ms. Meloni's hard-right agenda, her talk about preventing abortions, opposing quotas and other measures will set back the cause of women.\par \par \uc2 ''It's not a gain at all and, indeed, a possible setback from the point of view of women's rights,'' said Giorgia Serughetti, who writes about women's issues and teaches political philosophy at Bicocca University in Milan.\par \par \uc2 More than in neighboring \uc2 European Union\uc2  countries, women in Italy have struggled to emerge in the country's traditionally patriarchal society. Four out of 10 Italian women don't work. Unemployment rates are even higher for young women starting careers. Female chief executive officers lead only a tiny percentage of companies listed on Milan's stock exchange, and there are fewer than 10 female rectors at Italy's more than 80 universities.\par \par \uc2 And for many Italian women, finding a suitable work-life balance becomes nearly impossible once children enter the equation. Affordable, all-day, public child care is nonexistent in many areas, and women paid the highest price during the pandemic, staying home even after periods of lockdown when schools were shut.\par \par \uc2 All national and international indicators suggest that if women in Italy worked more, gross domestic product would largely benefit and increase.\par \par \uc2 ''Half of Italian women do not have economic independence,'' said Linda Laura Sabbadini, a statistician and director of new technologies at Italy's National Institute of Statistics. ''That can't just be cultural; politics clearly hasn't done enough for women so far.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Meloni has presented herself as someone who will help, but on key issues to women, the coalition has been vague and short on details. And a coalition partner, \uc2 Matteo Salvini\uc2  of the anti-immigrant League party, has admired \uc2 Victor Orban\uc2 , the conservative prime minister of Hungary, and his family policies. The League's leader recently said that Mr. Orban had drafted the ''most advanced family policy'' giving ''the best results at the European level.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Orban has encouraged Hungarian mothers to procreate prolifically to counter the dropping birthrate. This month, the Hungarian government passed a decree that would require women seeking an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to observe fetal vital signs before moving forward with the procedure.\par \par \uc2 Concerns have emerged in Italy that Ms. Meloni's center-right coalition could make it harder for women to have abortions in a country where the procedure has been legal since 1978 but is still very difficult to obtain.\par \par \uc2 Asked about the law, Ms. Meloni, who has said her mother nearly aborted her, vowed in an interview that she ''wouldn't change it'' as prime minister, and that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would remain ''accessible and safe and legal.'' But she added that she wanted to more fully apply a part of the law ''about prevention,'' which, she said, had been effectively ignored until now.\par \par \uc2 Critics fear that approach would allow anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  organizations to play a more prominent role in family-planning clinics and encourage even more doctors to avoid the procedure. Only about 33 percent of doctors perform legal abortions in Italy, and even less, 10 percent, in some regions.\par \par \uc2 Laura Lattuada, an actress in Rome, said she was concerned that the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law could be chipped away with Ms. Meloni in power.\par \par \uc2 ''She's constantly saying she wants to improve it, but I am not sure that her conception of protecting women and the family corresponds to the improvement of women's rights,'' she said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is hardly the only issue that has given activists pause. Italy introduced and has progressively extended the so-called pink quotas, a mandated percentage of female representation in politics and boardrooms. Many women say quotas in politics better reflect the population, while quotas in companies help overcome ''old boys'' networks, giving women equal access to higher paying jobs. They also give women greater visibility, they said.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Meloni is against the quotas. She argues that as a woman, she climbed the political ladder on her own and is now poised to run the country. She says that she is proof that women don't need government interference to enforce diversity.\par \par \uc2 Her supporters agreed.\par \par \uc2 ''They never gave her anything, she took it. She won on her own,'' said Lucia Loddo, 54, who was waving a banner supporting Ms. Meloni at a rally in Cagliari. She said that for women, Ms. Meloni's ascent ''is the most beautiful thing. All of the men have been disasters. She is prepared.''\par \par \uc2 About 25 percent of Italian woman voting on Sunday are expected to cast their ballots for Ms. Meloni, though pollsters failed to ask women whether her gender was a factor in their vote, which is itself telling of the attention given to women voters here. Ms. Meloni is polling at least 25 percent nationally, the highest of any candidate.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Meloni has won voters over with her down-to-earth and straight-talking manner (she often speaks in Roman dialect). But the secret to her popularity has less to do with her personality or policy proposals than that she was essentially the lone leader of a major party to stay in the opposition during the national unity government of \uc2 Mario Draghi\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 That allowed her to campaign in a country that is perennially looking for someone new as a fresh face, even though she has been in Parliament for nearly two decades and was a minister in a past government.\par \par \uc2 In that time, Italy has had a lackluster track record in empowering women in the work force, and experts say something else needs to be done.\par \par \uc2 ''We have to create the conditions for employment because we are at the bottom of the list in Europe,'' said Ida Maggi of Stati Generali delle Donne, an association working to get women's issues on the electoral agenda. It makes Italy ''look bad,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 One area where Ms. Meloni and even her most committed critics agree is the need for more nursery schools. The government of Mr. Draghi last year allocated billions of euros to build nurseries and extend child care services. But the problem is by no means solved.\par \par \uc2 In many Italian regions, a shortage of free nursery schools, along with short school days and three-month vacations, makes it difficult for working mothers to juggle their schedules. Even though many women are staying at home, the country has one of the lowest birthrates in Europe, something Ms. Meloni's center-right coalition has pledged to redress.\par \par \uc2 Speaking to supporters in Milan this month, Ms. Meloni said that she and her allies would work toward getting free child-care services, part of ''a huge plan to boost the birthrate, to support motherhood.'' With only 400,000 births last year, Italy was going through more than a demographic winter, she said: ''It's an ice age.''\par \par \uc2 ''I don't want this nation to disappear,'' she said, adding that the problem should not be solved through immigration. ''I want our families to have children,'' she added to a roar of applause.\par \par \uc2 But critics are not convinced her party, or likely coalition, is entirely committed to the cause of women.\par \par \uc2 Polls carried out last year show that while the majority of Italians said more should be done to reach gender equality, those numbers were considerably lower among supporters of Brothers of Italy and the League.\par \par \uc2 One campaign video for a candidate from the Forza Italia party, another coalition ally, was roundly mocked for promising a salary to women who don't work outside the home. The party is led by \uc2 Silvio Berlusconi\uc2 , who, Ms. Meloni said in the interview, put her ''in difficulty as a woman'' with his sex scandals when she was a young minister in his government.\par \par \uc2 After decades of unfulfilled campaign promises, there is skepticism writ large that any of the parties will really champion women's causes.\par \par \uc2 Promises about ''the needs and priorities of women'' -- including free day care and subsidies for families -- tend to vanish once it's time to actually put measures in place, said Laura Moschini, whose organization, the Gender Interuniversity Observatory, has drafted a ''handbook for good government'' highlighting women's concerns.\par \par \uc2 Those issues have discouraged women from voting, and the possibility of electing Ms. Meloni as the first female prime minister is not motivating women. Heading into the election on Sunday, polls suggest that more than a third of Italian women probably won't vote.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm disgusted by the entire political system,'' said Laura Porrega, who described herself as a ''desperate housewife'' because she wasn't able to find a job. ''When they want your taxes, they remember your name, but I've gotten nothing from the country at all.'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Serughetti, the Bicocca professor, said that women ''don't see their interests being represented,'' so they'd rather abstain.\par \par \uc2 ''The decision of women not to vote is a sort of protest to this order of things,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Jason Horowitz contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Jason Horowitz contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Giorgia Meloni campaigning in Naples. The founder of the hard-right Brothers of Italy party is leading in the race for prime minister. (PHOTOGRAPH BY ALESSANDRO GAROFALO/LAPRESSE, VIA ASSOCIATED PRESS) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220924ei9o0003u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
