\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Judge Stays Indiana's Ban on Most Abortions\b0\par\par\uc2 By Eliza Fawcett \par \uc2 931 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 September 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 20\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The state prohibition had been in effect for only a week, and now abortions are legal again while a court fight goes forward.\par \par \uc2 An Indiana judge temporarily halted the state's ban on most abortions on Thursday, a week after the law took effect.\par \par \uc2 The decision came as part of a lawsuit brought by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers challenging the state ban, which prohibits most abortions from conception. Indiana was the first state to pass new, sweeping restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  overturned the constitutional right to the procedure in June.\par \par \uc2 The judge's ruling for now restores wider legal rights to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state, which has played a prominent role in the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate, while the court case proceeds.\par \par \uc2 It also adds to the shifting and often chaotic patchwork of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulations across the country as conservative legislatures push for tighter restrictions and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups challenge them in court. The legal challenges are seeking to establish a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in individual state constitutions. Such cases, though in preliminary stages, are being watched closely to see whether that strategy works.\par \par \uc2 The Indiana law, signed by Gov. \uc2 Eric Holcomb\uc2 , a Republican, in early August, bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from conception except in some cases of rape, incest, fatal fetal abnormality or when the pregnant woman faces risk of death or certain severe health risks.\par \par \uc2 The decision of Special Judge Kelsey Hanlon, a Republican, of the Owen County Circuit Court, means that abortions up to 20 weeks after fertilization, or 22 weeks after a woman's last menstrual cycle, can resume in the state. The ruling specifically prohibits prosecutors from charging providers in the seven counties where abortions are performed. It also prevents the Medical Licensing Board of Indiana from revoking the licenses of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers.\par \par \uc2 In her ruling on Thursday, Judge Hanlon wrote that the Indiana Constitution includes a right to liberty and bodily autonomy, which reasonably includes decisions about family planning and ''whether to carry a pregnancy to term.''\par \par \uc2 The narrow exceptions included in the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban mean that the state allows a woman to have the procedure only after ''first experiencing extreme sexual violence or significant loss of physical health or death,'' Judge Hanlon wrote. And the ban -- which strips \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics of their licenses -- ''materially burdens the bodily autonomy of Indiana's women and girls by significantly and arbitrarily limiting their access to care.''\par \par \uc2 Leaders from a coalition of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers who brought the case said on Thursday that they ''are grateful that the court granted much-needed relief for patients, clients and providers, but this fight is far from over.''\par \par \uc2 Indiana's attorney general, \uc2 Todd Rokita\uc2 , a Republican, said his office planned to appeal the ruling and remained ''determined to fight for the lives of the unborn.''\par \par \uc2 The state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban requires that the procedure be performed only in hospitals or ambulatory outpatient surgical centers. A doctor who performs an illegal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Indiana could be charged with a felony and face one to six years in prison and a fine of up to $10,000.\par \par \uc2 Challenging the ban in late August in the Monroe County Circuit Court, a coalition represented by the American Civil Liberties Union of Indiana filed the lawsuit against members of the Medical Licensing Board of Indiana and other county officials. The coalition, which includes Planned Parenthood, argues that the state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban infringes on residents' right to privacy and violates the state's guarantee of equal privileges and immunities.\par \par \uc2 In a hearing Monday before Judge Hanlon, Kenneth J. Falk, the legal director of the A.C.L.U. of Indiana, argued that the state legislature had overstepped its authority in enacting the ban.\par \par \uc2 ''We are not asking the court to rewrite the legislation,'' Mr. Falk said. ''We're asking the court to recognize that the legislature has gone too far.''\par \par \uc2 Tom Fisher, the state's solicitor general, said during the hearing that the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Indiana was ''historically unsupported'' in the state, noting that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was prohibited when the present-day Indiana Constitution was written in 1851.\par \par \uc2 ''That history forecloses any legitimate claim to a right to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  under the Indiana Constitution,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Fisher said the argument that the State Constitution's right to liberty leads to a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  involved ''too many leaps.''\par \par \uc2 Judge Hanlon wrote that the State Constitution had a history of providing greater protection to individual citizens than the United States Constitution, and could still be interpreted ''as protecting bodily autonomy, including a qualified right by women not to carry a pregnancy to term.''\par \par \uc2 Indiana assumed an outsize role in the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate this summer when a 10-year-old rape victim from Ohio was forced to travel to Indiana to seek an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  because of her state's ban.\par \par \uc2 The Planned Parenthood lawsuit is one of two that have been brought recently against the Indiana ban. The other, a class-action lawsuit filed this month by the A.C.L.U. of Indiana on behalf of Hoosier Jews for Choice and five individual plaintiffs, argues that the ban violates Indiana's Religious Freedom Restoration Act, which prohibits government action that substantially burdens a person's exercise of religion.\par \par \uc2 Patricia Mazzei contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Patricia Mazzei contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 An \uc2  rights rally in July at the Indiana Statehouse. The state has played a prominent role in the nation's \uc2  debate. (PHOTOGRAPH BY KAITI SULLIVAN FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220923ei9n00043\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
