\par\par \uc2 POLITICAL MEMO\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 As Inflation Unnerves Voters, G.O.P. Holds Tight to Message\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jonathan Weisman \par \uc2 1,847 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 September 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Democrats and Republicans are running parallel campaigns, with one party emphasizing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and democracy, the other inflation and the economy -- and both talking past each other.\par \par \uc2 Zach Nunn, an Iowa Republican challenging one of the House's most vulnerable Democrats, had been talking for months about rising prices when a Texas congressman two weeks ago invited him to visit the Mexican border -- to see the fentanyl confiscated, hear tales of dying migrants and witness overwhelmed border agents.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Nunn took it all in, he said. Then, he went back to a district that stretches from Des Moines to the Missouri line to talk about inflation some more.\par \par \uc2 ''You know, from knocking on 10,000 doors, what people are interested in,'' Mr. Nunn said. It would not matter, he said, if he were speaking in Clarinda, Iowa -- a city of 5,300 -- or West Des Moines, a city of 70,000. ''People are all talking about what is going on with the economy,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 In the six-month primary season that came to a close on Tuesday, issues like \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , crime, immigration, the Jan. 6 attack on the Capitol, and \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  have risen and fallen, but nothing has dislodged inflation and the economy from the top of voters' minds. On Wednesday, polls out of Wisconsin and Georgia again found inflation to be the issue of greatest concern.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 New York Times\uc2 /Siena poll released on Friday had bright spots for Democrats, but 49 percent of respondents said that ''economic issues such as jobs, taxes or the cost of living'' were likely to determine their votes in November, compared with 31 percent who saw ''societal issues such as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , guns or democracy'' as decisive. And 52 percent of registered voters said they agreed with Republicans on the economy, versus 38 percent who said they agreed with Democrats.\par \par \uc2 And Republican candidates aren't letting go.\par \par \uc2 ''Inflation is now high enough to rob every working American of a month's pay over the course of a year,'' said Tom Barrett, a Michigan state senator challenging Representative \uc2 Elissa Slotkin\uc2 , a Democrat, in a Republican-leaning district around Lansing.\par \par \uc2 Representative Nancy Mace, Republican of South Carolina, released her first advertisement of the general election on Thursday -- and it focused solely on inflation.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm Nancy Mace, and I have had it with crazy inflation,'' she says to the camera as she counts up the cost of cooking an eggs-and-bacon breakfast. (Milk, $4 a gallon, a dozen eggs, nearly $4, and bacon, $8 a pack.)\par \par \uc2 For all the losers in an inflationary economy, there are also winners: people with large mortgages or student loan burdens that shrink away in real terms as incomes rise with inflation but debts do not; workers whose wages suddenly rise, sometimes enough to keep pace with prices; frugal seniors who enjoy Social Security cost-of-living increases tied to the inflation rate and higher interest rates on their savings accounts.\par \par \uc2 Even so, inflation has had outsize potency as a political issue for at least a century -- and since hyperinflation after World War I helped usher in authoritarianism across Europe, few issues have been quite so politically destabilizing.\par \par \uc2 In the mid-1990s, Robert J. Shiller, a Nobel Prize-winning economist at Yale who was puzzled by the power of inflation as a disruptive force, surveyed people in the United States, Germany and Brazil to determine why inflation had always produced so much anger, wounded national pride and a feeling that an unwritten social contract between citizens and their government had been broken.\par \par \uc2 Facing deep feelings of insecurity, anxiety and unfairness, ''not a single respondent volunteered anywhere on the questionnaire that he or she benefited from inflation,'' he marveled.\par \par \uc2 For Republicans seeking control of Congress, that history still could prove determinative, even as Democrats try to center their campaigns on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and democratic pluralism and Republican strategists test other themes, like crime, the border and Democratic ''radicalism.''\par \par \uc2 Representative \uc2 Kim Schrier\uc2 , a Democrat in the suburbs of Seattle who is locked in a tossup contest for re-election, has gone after her Republican opponent, Matt Larkin, on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , using her background as a physician to press a persona of earnest trustworthiness. Democratic campaign officials in Washington, D.C., have accused Mr. Larkin of questioning the election results of 2020 and refusing to acknowledge President Biden as legitimately elected.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Larkin's response? The price of eggs, ''up 52 percent in Washington State,'' he said Thursday, and milk, ''way, way up in the Eighth District.''\par \par \uc2 Voters, he said in an interview, ''are very, very concerned.''\par \par \uc2 And, in a country where one party controls the House, the Senate and the White House -- and in a state where Democrats control pretty much everything -- ''there's also a sense that the Democrats in general are doing this,'' Mr. Larkin added.\par \par \uc2 That, too, is consistent with economic history: Citizens of countries suffering from inflation have routinely sought to assign blame -- to the government, to greedy companies or to politicians. Inflationary periods often yield labor strife, as workers and unions press for wage increases to keep up with rising prices, point fingers at ''price-gouging'' companies and, more than anything, rage at those in power.\par \par \uc2 Richard M. Nixon\uc2 's 1968 victory over Hubert Humphrey is popularly attributed to the Vietnam War and domestic unrest, but inflation was a ''top three'' issue, even though price increases were a relatively mild 4.27 percent, said \uc2 Jason Furman\uc2 , a Harvard economist with deep connections to the national Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 Gerald R. Ford's defeat in 1976 is often ascribed to the hangover from Watergate, but his WIN (Whip Inflation Now) buttons became an object of ridicule in a year in which inflation was still pushing 6 percent. Four years later, \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2 's dreams of a second term were vaporized by 13.5 percent inflation.\par \par \uc2 And in 1982, as the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  was engineering a recession to finally get control of price gains and \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  was absorbing the blame, Democrats beat Republicans by nearly 12 percentage points in the midterm elections -- and padded their House majority by 27 seats.\par \par \uc2 ''From bitter historical experience, we know how quickly inflation destroys confidence in the reliability of political institutions and ends up endangering democracy,'' \uc2 Helmut Kohl\uc2 , the chancellor of Germany, said in 1995, harking back to the hyperinflation of the Weimar Republic.\par \par \uc2 In 2022, Republicans like State Senator Jen Kiggans, who is challenging Representative \uc2 Elaine Luria\uc2  in southeast Virginia, are using inflation not only to go after the party in power, but also to deflect Democratic charges of ''radicalism'' by portraying themselves as ordinary family folks, in touch with consumer reality.\par \par \uc2 For voters, however, the signals from the actual economy are mixed. The official inflation report last Tuesday showed that prices in August rose 8.3 percent from a year earlier, only slightly better than July's 8.5 percent. But because of rapidly falling gasoline prices, overall consumer costs from the month before rose a barely noticeable 0.1 percent. Prices at the pump, the most visible inflationary signal to consumers, are expected to continue their fall in the weeks leading up to the election. That could offer at least psychological relief to consumers -- and Democrats -- as other cost-of-living indicators like food and rent send stock traders and the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  running for shelter.\par \par \uc2 ''If you're going to have 8 percent inflation over the year before up to the election, you'd like to have the last three months at zero, so the sequencing is about as good as it could be'' for Democrats, Mr. Furman said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Barrett conceded that these fresh signals could blunt the political impact. ''To some degree, they boiled the frog, then turned the temperature down a notch on the stove,'' he said, ''but it's still raging hot.''\par \par \uc2 And his Democratic opponent, Ms, Slotkin, has taken pains to address the issue, too, ticking off legislation and administrative actions that she said she supported to address inflation. They include suspending the federal gas tax, releasing oil from the strategic petroleum reserve, pressing companies on ''price gouging'' and granting Medicare the authority to negotiate drug prices.\par \par \uc2 ''Certainly if there was a silver bullet to fix it, it would have been fired,'' she said, adding, ''leaders need to do all they can -- not just use it as a political issue.''\par \par \uc2 Republicans say that, as children go back to school, higher prices on clothes, food and school supplies will come more into focus, and cold weather will bring the sticker shock of soaring heating bills. The disorienting power of price increases is all the more potent, experts say, because Americans have not weathered them in four decades.\par \par \uc2 Democrats hope to turn voters' minds elsewhere. On the lengthy ''issues'' web page of Mr. Nunn's opponent, Representative \uc2 Cindy Axne\uc2 , the word inflation does not appear, though she does mention inflation in one campaign ad as being among a litany of travails hitting Iowans recently. In another ad, Ms. Axne acknowledges that ''rising costs are hurting Iowa families everywhere.''\par \par \uc2 On Friday, Emilia Sykes, the Democratic candidate for an open House seat in northeast Ohio, released a new ad saying she has ''a plan to lower costs,'' though she avoided the word inflation.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, Democrats are focusing almost exclusively on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , democracy and the overall theme that Republicans who have undermined the integrity of elections and democratic institutions cannot be trusted with power.\par \par \uc2 In that sense, the parties are entering the final sprint to Nov. 8 largely talking past each other.\par \par \uc2 But the unique ability of inflation to anger voters and undermine authorities in power should not be underestimated, economists say.\par \par \uc2 Wage increases, though stronger than they have been in years, have not kept pace with inflation this year, but in 2021, when voters' anger showed up most clearly in polling, average family incomes ''far exceeded'' price gains, thanks in large part to temporary tax cuts and income supplements approved in successive pandemic-relief measures, said Austan Goolsbee, a \uc2 University of Chicago\uc2  economist.\par \par \uc2 ''Average actual real incomes went up, not down,'' he said, ''so it's still a puzzle.''\par \par \uc2 For Republicans, there is no mystery, only the challenge of staying on the issue as Democrats try to direct voters' attention anywhere else.\par \par \uc2 ''This stuff is real,'' Mr. Barrett said on Thursday. ''The Democrats are whistling past the graveyard.''\par \par \uc2 ''People are all talking about what is going on with the economy,'' said Zach Nunn, a Republican running for a House seat in Iowa. (PHOTOGRAPH BY BRYON HOULGRAVE/THE DES MOINES REGISTER, VIA ASSOCIATED PRESS); Representative Nancy Mace released her first election ad last week. ''I have had it with crazy inflation,'' she says in the ad. (PHOTOGRAPH BY LOGAN R. CYRUS FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); Jen Kiggans, challenging Elaine Luria for a House seat in Virginia, is using inflation to argue that Democrats seem out of touch. (PHOTOGRAPH BY CARLOS BERNATE FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A20) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220920ei9k0004a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
