\par\par \uc2 ON WASHINGTON\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 This Time, the G.O.P. Is on the Defensive On Social Issues\b0\par\par\uc2 By Carl Hulse \par \uc2 1,445 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 September 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 27\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Republican missteps have helped to spotlight the party's divisions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and same-sex marriage, two issues on which their base is out of step with the general public.\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- Republicans have perfected the art of keeping the heat on Democrats on the searing social issues of the day, but this election year, it seems to be Republicans who are getting scorched.\par \par \uc2 During a midterm cycle that seemed tailor-made for significant Republican gains in the House and Senate, Democrats have managed to grab the advantage on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and same-sex marriage, steering the conversation away from topics that are thornier for them, such as inflation and crime.\par \par \uc2 They have had substantial help from Republican miscues, confounding Democrats who typically expect more craftiness from across the aisle.\par \par \uc2 ''They can't seem to get out of their own way,'' said Senator Richard Blumenthal of Connecticut, one of the Democratic incumbents on the ballot in November.\par \par \uc2 One reason for their struggles is that a large swath of the Republican base has fallen out of step with broader public opinion on these issues. Most Americans favor same-sex marriage rights and at least some \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, but many Republican voters continue to oppose same-sex marriage and want strict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  limits if not an outright ban. The disconnect makes navigating those topics treacherous for Republicans, who are faced with the choice of turning off their core supporters or alienating the independents whose support they need to prevail in November.\par \par \uc2 The trouble shows.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, Democrats announced they would postpone until after the election a vote to protect same-sex marriages because its backers had failed to secure enough Republican support to overcome a G.O.P. filibuster.\par \par \uc2 It was an intriguing decision by Senator \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , the New York Democrat and majority leader, who is not usually inclined to pass up an opportunity to inflict political pain on the opposition. But he acquiesced to a request from bipartisan backers of the legislation for more time -- and a less charged environment.\par \par \uc2 While it spared Republicans what was looking like a difficult moment, damage had already been done.\par \par \uc2 The threatened filibuster made it clear that some Republicans weren't comfortable voting in favor of same-sex marriage before the midterm election, and others didn't want to go on record against it at an inopportune time. Either way, Republicans looked shaky on an issue that most Americans consider to be long resolved.\par \par \uc2 The Republican posture in the Senate was sufficient to prompt hundreds of prominent Republicans, including Senate candidates in Pennsylvania and Colorado, to sign a letter calling for passage of the same-sex marriage legislation to ''reaffirm that marriage for gay and lesbian couples is settled law.''\par \par \uc2 On \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Republicans knew that the Supreme Court ruling striking down Roe v. Wade would complicate their push to reclaim Congress, and they sought to quickly rid themselves of the problem. Senator \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  of Kentucky, the minority leader, said a G.O.P.-controlled Congress could pursue a nationwide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, but Republicans soft-pedaled that idea and instead chose to emphasize that the ruling returned the question of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights to each state, where they said it belonged. Case closed.\par \par \uc2 Enter Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , Republican of South Carolina, who surprised his colleagues on Tuesday by rolling out his plan, backed by anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups, to enact a nationwide ban on abortions after 15 weeks, which would impose federal restrictions on blue and purple states that have not joined the post-Roe race to enact strict new limits on the procedure.\par \par \uc2 To cringes from many of his Republican colleagues, Mr. Graham declared that the coming election was essentially a referendum on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- and that if his party won control of Congress, it would, in fact, consider a ban.\par \par \uc2 Despite their determination to shift the issue away from the Capitol, Senate Republicans -- and their midterm candidates -- suddenly found themselves forced to answer whether they backed such a prohibition, potentially driving off suburban women who will be crucial to the election outcome. Again, some Republican lawmakers and candidates sought to distance themselves from the proposal.\par \par \uc2 Privately, many of Mr. Graham's colleagues wanted to throttle him. Others were more diplomatic.\par \par \uc2 ''I didn't know anything about it,'' said Senator \uc2 John Cornyn\uc2 , Republican of Texas. ''I don't know what his motivation was.''\par \par \uc2 Democrats could not believe their good fortune. On the day new inflation numbers were driving down the stock market, Mr. Graham had turned the conversation back to a topic that has so far proved advantageous for Democrats in the aftermath of the court ruling, which Senator Chris Coons, Democrat of Delaware, said had already shocked much of the nation.\par \par \uc2 ''Republicans, having succeeded in putting in a conservative majority to overturn Roe v. Wade, are proposing to go even farther,'' Mr. Coons said of the Graham legislation.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Graham insists he will be proved right in the end.\par \par \uc2 ''I think that my position is reasonable and logical and over time, I feel good about it winning the day,'' he said. ''When the dust settles, this will all make sense.''\par \par \uc2 Until then, Democrats are gleefully running ads portraying Republicans as reactionaries.\par \par \uc2 Republicans were also in danger of running afoul of public opinion on another volatile social issue -- immigration -- after Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  of Florida sent air charters of migrants to Martha's Vineyard, the island retreat in the blue state of Massachusetts. The stunt was aimed at highlighting the uneven impact of federal border policies, and had many Republicans celebrating having steered the campaign conversation to the dysfunctional immigration system.\par \par \uc2 But it also risked spurring a backlash. While polls show that most Republicans draw a hard line on immigration, they also find that the majority of Americans regard immigration as a positive and are particularly sympathetic to refugees, suggesting that the G.O.P. stunt -- which stranded vulnerable people in a place unprepared for their arrival -- could also prompt outrage among voters who regard it as cruel.\par \par \uc2 Republicans concede they could do without the turmoil surrounding the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and same-sex marriage battles, but contend that the focus on those issues is mainly a Washington preoccupation.\par \par \uc2 ''Could we do without the distractions?'' asked Senator \uc2 Kevin Cramer\uc2 , Republican of North Dakota. ''Perhaps. But I think the voters are still focused on the main things.''\par \par \uc2 ''At the end of the day, I think it is still the economy, stupid,'' he added, quoting the famous line from \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 's successful 1992 presidential campaign. ''Everybody is still paying too much for groceries and other things, and that's what the election is going to be about.''\par \par \uc2 Republicans are also trying to regain the upper hand on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , portraying Democrats as extremists who don't support any restrictions at all, a position that is also at odds with those of many Americans.\par \par \uc2 ''The Democrat position used to be Roe v. Wade,'' said Mr. Cornyn. ''Now it is \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  without limitations up to the time of delivery. It is just shocking to me. Most people's views on this are more nuanced. They may be pro-choice but would say there is a limit.''\par \par \uc2 Democrats have not explicitly proposed such a sweeping policy, but they have put forward legislation that would protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access nationwide by prohibiting a long list of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions, including some enacted after Roe was decided in 1973. It failed in May when Senate Republicans, joined by one Democrat, blocked it.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Cornyn joined the Republican chorus in saying that the economy, border security and rising crime would remain the decisive topics in the election, even as he conceded that Democrats had been successful in stoking voter enthusiasm on the social issues.\par \par \uc2 ''Inflation is not going away, the Fed is going to raise interest rates more,'' Mr. Cornyn said. ''People are still going to be grumpy.''\par \par \uc2 Democrats, more accustomed to being on the losing end of the culture clash, say Republicans are misreading where the public stands on such issues and will pay a price for it.\par \par \uc2 ''Republicans are just way out of the American mainstream,'' said Mr. Blumenthal. ''On a woman's right to make a personal decision, individual women may make very different decisions. But the vast majority think they ought to be trusted to make those decisions -- not some government official.''\par \par \uc2 Above, Senator Lindsey Graham presented plans for a national \uc2  ban. Senator Chuck Schumer, left, put off a vote on same-sex marriage. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY HAIYUN JIANG/THE NEW YORK TIMES; AL DRAGO FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220918ei9i00054\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
