\par\par \uc2 MICHELLE GOLDBERG\par \uc2 Sunday Review Desk; SECTSR\par \b \uc2 Graham's Cruel  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Ban\b0\par\par\uc2 By Michelle Goldberg \par \uc2 1,091 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 September 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 At the end of Senator Lindsey Graham's news conference on Tuesday proposing a national ban on abortions after 15 weeks of pregnancy, a woman named Ashbey Beasley stood up and asked him a question inspired by her own excruciating loss.\par \par \uc2 ''What would you say to somebody like me who found out that their son had an anomaly that was incompatible with life at 16 weeks?'' she began. Beasley chose not to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , delivering her son at 28 weeks. ''When he was born, he lived for eight days,'' she said. ''He bled from every orifice of his body, but we were allowed to make that choice for him. You would be robbing that choice from those women. What would you say to someone like me?''\par \par \uc2 Graham had no real answer. His bill contains narrow exceptions for rape, incest and life-threatening pregnancies, but not for severe fetal anomalies or pregnancies that are otherwise nonviable. So, faced with someone insisting that he consider the consequences of his proposal, he defaulted to a duplicitous anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  talking point about global \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws.\par \par \uc2 ''The world pretty much has spoken on this issue,'' said Graham. ''The developed world has said at this stage into the pregnancy the child feels pain, and we're saying we're going to join the rest of the world and not be like Iran.''\par \par \uc2 Graham was making an argument, common in anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  circles, that American \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws are unusually permissive and that banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 12 or 15 weeks would bring us in line with Europe. France and Spain, for example, both permit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for any reason through 14 weeks, and Germany through 12 weeks post-conception. ''If we adopted my bill, our bill, we would be in the mainstream of most everybody else in the world,'' said Graham. ''I think there are 47 of the 50 European countries have a ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from 12 to 15 weeks.''\par \par \uc2 This is, at best, a highly selective reading of European \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws. It ignores the fact that on most of the continent, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is state-subsidized and easily accessible early in pregnancy, so women aren't pushed into later terminations as they struggle to raise money. More significantly, the restrictions on later abortions have broad exceptions.\par \par \uc2 Take German \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws, which are, for Europe, quite stringent. Until this summer, a Nazi-era ban on advertising \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was still in effect, and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still technically illegal, though it's been decriminalized during the first trimester. After that, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is allowed to protect a woman's physical or mental health, taking into account her ''present and future circumstances.'' For low-income women, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is publicly funded.\par \par \uc2 A woman in Beasley's devastating situation would be able to end her pregnancy almost anywhere in Europe. Indeed, what Graham is proposing has little analogue in the developed world. Even Iran -- where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is, despite Graham's nonsensical reference to the country, mostly illegal -- allows women to petition a panel to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in cases of severe fetal disability.\par \par \uc2 Why did Graham leave such an exception, which the vast majority of Americans would almost certainly support, out of his proposed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban? There are two possibilities, which are not mutually exclusive. Either he was pandering to the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists who on Monday sent a letter to Congress demanding federal action against states with liberal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws, or he simply hasn't thought very much about what pregnancy entails.\par \par \uc2 Most people who have gone through a pregnancy, or watched someone close to them go through one, know that there are certain white-knuckle benchmarks. At 10 weeks, you can get a blood test that checks for some prenatal genetic disorders, but it can tell you only your risk level. ''Most of the time we make diagnoses around things like fetal abnormalities, genetic abnormalities, at around 15 to 20 weeks, when we can do an amniocentesis,'' said Dr. Kristyn Brandi, an OB-GYN and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider in New Jersey and the board chair of Physicians for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Health\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Then, at 20 weeks, pregnant patients are typically offered an anatomy scan, which checks, among other things, for problems like anencephaly, in which a fetus's brain and skull fail to develop.\par \par \uc2 Graham would condemn every single woman who gets disastrous news from her amnio or her anatomy scan to carry a doomed pregnancy to term, unless she could prove that it was going to kill her. Whether thoughtless or deliberate, the cruelty of this is almost unfathomable.\par \par \uc2 Politically, Graham's bill is a boon to Democrats. He seems to have been trying to shift the focus of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate to later abortions, where Republicans think they can paint their opponents as extremists. Instead, he has underlined Republican callousness toward the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients likely to elicit the most public sympathy.\par \par \uc2 But Democrats shouldn't be gleeful. Republicans have shown themselves willing to impose such draconian prohibitions in places where they have complete power. Recently, Kailee Lingo DeSpain, who said that in the past she was ''your quintessential pro-life Texan,'' told CNN about having to leave the state for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after finding out that her fetus had heart, lung, brain, kidney and genetic defects and ''would either be stillborn or die within minutes of birth.'' How, asked DeSpain, ''could you be so cruel as to pass a law that you know will hurt women and that you know will cause babies to be born in pain?''\par \par \uc2 At the moment, Republicans don't have the ability to impose such a regime on the entire country. Nor are many of them interested in talking about national bans; some Republicans were furious at Graham for thrusting the issue into the spotlight. But Graham was probably right when he said, ''If we take back the House and the Senate, I can assure you we'll have a vote on our bill.'' So far, Republicans have tried their best to give the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement what it wants. When Graham tells us what they intend to do to us, we should listen.\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Twitter\uc2  (@NYTopinion) and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY DAMON 