\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Roe's Reversal Changes Ways Doctors Work\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kate Zernike \par \uc2 2,090 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 September 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 State \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans carry narrow but sometimes vague exceptions, and years of prison time. That's forcing doctors to think like lawyers, and hospitals to create new protocols.\par \par \uc2 In Wisconsin, a group of doctors and lawyers is trying to come up with guidelines on how to comply with a newly revived 173-year-old law that prohibits \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  except to save the life of a pregnant woman. They face the daunting task of defining all the emergencies and conditions that might result in a pregnant woman's death, and the fact that doctors could be punished with six years in prison if a prosecutor disagrees that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was necessary.\par \par \uc2 A similar task force at an Arizona hospital recommends having a lawyer on call to help doctors determine whether a woman's condition threatens her life enough to justify an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Already, the hospital has added questions to its electronic medical forms so they can be used to argue that patients who had abortions would have died without them.\par \par \uc2 And in Texas, oncologists say they now wait for pregnant women with cancer to get sicker before they treat them, because the standard of care would be to abort the fetus rather than allow treatments that damage it, but a state law allows \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  only ''at risk of death.'' Some hospitals have established committees to evaluate whether a pregnancy complication is severe enough to justify an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Two months after the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade and the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the medical consequences extend far beyond \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics and women seeking to end unwanted pregnancies. Doctors who never thought of themselves as ''abortionists,'' to use the language of the court's decision, say the criminalization of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is changing how they treat women who arrive in emergency rooms and on labor and delivery floors with wanted but complicated pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 During the 50 years of Roe, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  became the standard of care in many medical situations. Now, laws ban it or make it unavailable in about half the states, usually with exceptions only for rape and incest or to save the life of the pregnant woman. While a few states have attempted to specify conditions that qualify, the laws are generally vague and have failed to account for every possibility. With lawmakers attempting to regulate medical procedures, medical providers say they have to think like lawyers.\par \par \uc2 ''A lot of us go into emergency medicine because of the imperative to take care of every patient -- the person without housing and a C.E.O. -- and we're really proud of that ethical obligation to say, 'Here's the patient in front of me and I'm going to do everything I can for them,''' said Dr. Alison Haddock, an emergency physician in Houston and chair of the board of the \uc2 American College of Emergency Physicians\uc2 . Now, she said, ''We're no longer basing our judgment on the clinical needs of the woman, we're basing it on what we understand the legal situation to be.''\par \par \uc2 Physicians would more typically talk to hospital lawyers about guardianship when caring for elderly or psychiatric patients, Dr. Haddock said. Now, when patients arrive with ectopic pregnancies, miscarriages or hemorrhaging -- all situations where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been established as standard care -- the questions for the lawyers are more pressing: ''Do we wait until the fetus is definitely dead, or is mostly dead good enough?'' she asked. ''If they're telling us to wait for the condition to be fully emergent, how much bleeding is too much?''\par \par \uc2 ''Having to consult a lawyer in an emergent situation is a whole new ballgame,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Doctors in Texas began dealing with the questions even before the Supreme Court overturned Roe with its decision in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization. A law that took effect a year ago effectively banned most abortions after six weeks.\par \par \uc2 Some hospitals have instituted policies requiring one or two additional physicians to review the decision before an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  can proceed. In states including Indiana and Louisiana, the law requires two doctors to certify that a woman faces life-threatening risk before she can get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Dr. Julie Kwatra, an obstetrician in Scottsdale, Ariz., faced so many questions from doctors and nurses the weekend of the Dobbs decision in June that she and others at her hospital formed a committee to come up with guidelines to protect patients' health and doctors from liability. A court has blocked one state law banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and the governor said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was still legal. But the state's attorney general said he intended to enforce a ban that was written before Arizona became a state, so providers have almost entirely stopped.\par \par \uc2 The confusion, Dr. Kwatra said, was ''eminently predictable,'' given the number of situations where physicians have to terminate pregnancies to protect the health or life of the pregnant woman. But even she was surprised at the number and range of hospital employees who have emerged with concerns.\par \par \uc2 Forensic nurses who care for sexual assault victims in the emergency room said they would no longer provide morning-after contraception for fear it would be considered an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drug. Because the old law punishes those who ''aid and abet'' an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , an anesthesiologist worried that he might be prosecuted for putting a patient to sleep for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . A neonatologist worried about liability for declining to resuscitate a fetus judged no longer viable.\par \par \uc2 ''We already work under a cloud of getting sued. That's what we signed up for,'' Dr. Kwatra said. ''This is different. This is criminal liability, not civil liability. This is jail time.''\par \par \uc2 Some anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctors argue that the concerns about not being able to provide lifesaving \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care are overblown -- ''blatantly absurd,'' as Dr. Christina Francis, the chair of the American Association of Pro-life Obstetricians and Gynecologists, said at a congressional hearing in July.\par \par \uc2 ''Not a single state law restricting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  prevents treating these conditions,'' Dr. Francis argued, because they make exceptions for any life-threatening emergency.\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups contend that life-threatening conditions are rare in pregnancy and can be treated by inducing labor or performing a C-section rather than an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ''Even if the baby does not survive,'' wrote Dr. Ingrid Skop, an obstetrician and the director of medical affairs at the Charlotte Lozier Institute, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group, ''these humane procedures allow a grieving family to show love and say good-bye.''\par \par \uc2 Several high-profile cases of women denied care have captured headlines and set doctors on edge. But doctors say these extreme cases are not isolated; hospitals are routinely refusing or delaying care. One study of two Dallas hospitals in the nine months after the Texas ban took effect found that women had to wait an average of nine days for their conditions to be considered life threatening enough to justify \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Many suffered serious health consequences while they waited, including hemorrhaging and sepsis, and one woman had to have a hysterectomy as a result.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration wrote medical providers in July, reminding them that they had to comply with a federal law known as the Emergency Medical Treatment and Labor Act. The law requires emergency rooms to provide stabilizing treatment to any patient who arrives with an emergency condition or in labor, or transfer them to a hospital that can provide it. That, the letter said, meant they ''must provide'' an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even in states that ban it, if it is required to stabilize a woman's health.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department also sued Idaho, saying its new ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  made it impossible for providers to comply with the federal law. A brief filed by a coalition of states in support of the lawsuit enumerated cases across the country where emergency physicians have had to perform abortions to save women's lives. Well beyond common complications like miscarriage or a separated placenta, they included heart conditions, kidney disorders, sickle cell anemia, acute leukemia and at least one case of pre-eclampsia so severe that the woman's liver began to fail.\par \par \uc2 But Texas' attorney general, \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2 , sued the administration for its guidance on the federal law, accusing it of an end-run to ''turn hospitals and emergency rooms into walk-in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics.'' Courts in the two states reached different conclusions: A federal judge in Texas agreed with the state and temporarily blocked implementation of the federal guidance on emergency treatment; one in Idaho agreed with the Biden administration and blocked the state law.\par \par \uc2 ''There's such confusion,'' said Dr. Allison Linton, an obstetrician in Milwaukee, ''and when doctors are hearing this risk of a felony charge, they're erring on the side of fear.''\par \par \uc2 In Wisconsin, an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban on the books since 1849 was blocked while Roe was in effect. Now, the governor and attorney general, who do not support the law, have asked a court to determine whether it can be enforced. In the meantime, prosecutors say they intend to enforce it, so providers have stopped abortions.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Linton recalled a woman who recently arrived at a nearby hospital with a stillborn fetus. The required procedure was an induced delivery, not \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but still, doctors declined to do it, so she and others received an alert to help find a physician who was willing. ''Patients with conditions that don't even fall under the ban are being denied care,'' Dr. Linton said.\par \par \uc2 Even before the Dobbs decision, she said, a committee of lawyers and doctors across the state began working to try to come up with a list of what qualified as exceptions under the law, and indications to help other doctors determine when a woman's life can be said to be at risk. The \uc2 American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists\uc2  has recommended that hospitals set up such task forces. But it also warned that it is ''impossible'' and ''dangerous'' to attempt to create a finite list of conditions to guide doctors. Medicine is too complex, no patient's symptoms or conditions are the same, and they can deteriorate rapidly.\par \par \uc2 Roe, which prohibited states from banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before viability, allowed doctors to offer patients options of how they wanted to be treated. ''Now that patient autonomy has gone away,'' said Dr. Abigail Cutler, an obstetrician-gynecologist at the \uc2 University of Wisconsin-Madison\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''I'm compelled by my conscience to provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, and I have the training and the skills to do so compassionately and well,'' she said. ''And so to have my hands tied and not be able to help a person in front of me is devastating.''\par \par \uc2 Feeling squeezed between the law and their duty to care for patients, doctors are becoming more outspoken in their opposition. In The New England Journal of Medicine, two breast cancer doctors in Colorado, Dr. Nicole Christian and Dr. Virginia Borges, argued that because so many therapies can result in fetal anomalies or stillbirth, breast cancer patients had to be able to choose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . And given their broad impact on medicine, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions ''should be of concern to any physician who has a patient who could be, could become, or is pregnant.''\par \par \uc2 More than a dozen medical and public health associations, including the \uc2 American Hospital Association\uc2 , the \uc2 American College of Emergency Physicians\uc2 , and the \uc2 American Academy of Pediatrics\uc2 , joined Democracy Forward, a group aligned with Democratic causes, to file briefs in the Texas and Idaho lawsuits regarding emergency \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care.\par \par \uc2 The Idaho ban, one of the briefs argues, ''willfully disregards what it means to pregnant patients -- and their doctors -- to be told that, alone among all patients seeking emergency care and contrary to medical guidelines and ethics, they must wait until their life is in jeopardy to receive treatment.''\par \par \uc2 ''Some others have said that these are incredibly rare situations,'' Dr. Jack Resneck Jr., the president of the \uc2 American Medical Association\uc2 , said in an interview. ''To the contrary, this is happening every day, all the time in these states.''\par \par \uc2 J. David Goodman contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 J. David Goodman contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 DR. JULIE KWATRA, an obstetrician in Scottsdale, Ariz., said that doctors know the risk of being sued. She and others at her hospital formed a committee to come up with guidelines. (PHOTOGRAPH BY REBECCA NOBLE FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); DR. ALISON HADDOCK, an emergency physician in Houston and chair of the board of the American College of Emergency Physicians. (PHOTOGRAPH BY CALLAGHAN O'HARE FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A19) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220911ei9b0004k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
