\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Total Ban on  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Falters in South Carolina Senate\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ava Sasani \par \uc2 1,081 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 September 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 19\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Legislators failed to advance a total ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , instead adding more restrictions to an existing law. The G.O.P. split over exceptions for rape and incest.\par \par \uc2 The South Carolina Senate voted Thursday night to tighten \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions but failed to pass a total ban after a heated debate revealed the ongoing struggle among Republicans to define a cohesive post-Roe strategy.\par \par \uc2 The chamber voted to gut a bill that would have prohibited \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  without exception for rape or incest, instead choosing to add more limits to the state's existing law that bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after six weeks. That law is temporarily blocked by the state Supreme Court because of ongoing litigation.\par \par \uc2 The vote came after two days of debate in the chamber, during which Republicans haggled over whether there ought to be exceptions for victims of rape and incest.\par \par \uc2 Under the state's current six-week ban, rape and incest victims can receive an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before 20 weeks. The State Senate proposal reduces that time to 12 weeks.\par \par \uc2 The proposal also requires doctors who perform abortions under the rape and incest exception to report the crime to law enforcement and take a DNA sample of the fetal tissue. A local sheriff's office must collect the sample and store it -- but the bill does not provide funding to help law enforcement with that process, leading some in the G.O.P. to call the bill an ''unfunded mandate.''\par \par \uc2 Senate Majority Leader Shane Massey, who backed a more complete ban without exceptions for rape or incest, could not overcome resistance from within his own party.\par \par \uc2 ''We don't have the votes, and I hate that I have to admit that,'' said Mr. Massey, who instead shifted to a more modest goal of further restrictions.\par \par \uc2 Three Republican women spoke against the total ban and in favor of exceptions for rape and incest, arguing that it was wrong to compel young victims of sexual violence to give birth.\par \par \uc2 ''I care about the children who are forced into adulthood'' because of laws ''made up by a legislature full of men so they can take a victory lap and feel good about it,'' Republican Senator Katrina Shealy said.\par \par \uc2 Those in favor of a complete ban argued that a fetus conceived through sexual violence was innocent and deserved protection. ''Does an unborn human being have the most fundamental civil right of all, which is the right to life?'' Republican Senator Richard Cash asked.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Massey and his allies forfeited the original goal of a complete ban after a fellow Republican, Senator Tom Davis, launched a filibuster supported by Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Davis ended up voting in favor of the modified bill, as did 26 other Republicans. Democrats voted against the bill.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Davis had urged his colleagues to consider the ''competing rights'' of the woman and the fetus, both of whom -- in his view -- deserved rights and protections that ought to be balanced against each other.\par \par \uc2 ''This is euthanasia in or outside the womb,'' Sen. Billy Garrett said, in response to Mr. Davis's amendment to allow \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the case of a fatal fetal anomaly.\par \par \uc2 The split in South Carolina illustrates a broader challenge for the Republican Party on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as the midterms draw closer. The party is caught between two competing goals: win political victory by appeasing moderates who feel uneasy about sweeping \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, or push for the more extreme position that a fetus deserves the same legal protections as any person.\par \par \uc2 The state announced plans for a special session just days after the Supreme Court struck down Roe v. Wade, ending the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and sending the issue back to the states to decide.\par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 Henry McMaster\uc2 , a Republican who is facing re-election this November, has not commented on the bill but has broadly supported \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions.\par \par \uc2 The State Senate vote comes a week after the South Carolina House passed its own version of the bill after days of similar debate among its Republican members. The Senate version of the bill will now go back to the House.\par \par \uc2 An initial House version of the bill, which did not include exceptions for rape and incest, failed by eight votes last week. House Republicans quickly amended the bill through a last-minute maneuver to include exceptions for rape and incest before 12 weeks of pregnancy. Representative John McCravy, a Republican, urged his colleagues to vote for the amended bill so that they would not ''throw the baby out with the bath water.''\par \par \uc2 Earlier, Mr. McCravy -- one of the lead sponsors of the House bill -- said that abortions should not be permitted even in cases of rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 ''As much as a tragedy as this is, we must also consider the life of the innocent preborn child as precious as the life of any child,'' Mr. McCravy said. ''To kill an innocent child will never erase the criminal act.''\par \par \uc2 South Carolina Democrats are betting that the Republican Party will face political backlash in November for defending legislation that would force women to carry a pregnancy conceived through sexual violence.\par \par \uc2 ''The fact is this, Republicans are going to have to answer one simple question: Do they believe the rights of rapists and pedophiles are more important than the rights of 11- or 12-year-old victims?'' Trav Robertson, the chair of the South Carolina Democratic Party, said.\par \par \uc2 Before Tuesday's session, Representative Neil Collins, a Republican, captured widespread attention online in a video in which he told colleagues that he ''lost sleep'' over his vote for South Carolina's six-week ban, which the legislature passed in February 2021.\par \par \uc2 In the clip, Mr. Collins explained that a doctor told him that a 19-year-old girl's life was threatened after she had a miscarriage at 15 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 ''The doctor told me at that point, well first, there's a greater than 50 percent chance that she's going to lose her uterus,'' Mr. Collins said in the clip. ''There's a 10 percent chance that she will develop sepsis and herself die. That weighs on me. I voted for that bill. These are affecting people.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Collins voted in favor of the final House bill.\par \par \uc2 Protesters gathered inside the South Carolina House on Tuesday, as members debated a new near-total ban on \uc2 . (PHOTOGRAPH BY Sam Wolfe/Reuters FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220910ei9a0004b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
