\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Pressing Limits to Ease Delivery of  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Pill\b0\par\par\uc2 By Pam Belluck \par \uc2 2,109 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 September 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 As bans and restrictions proliferate across the country, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill providers are pushing the envelope of regulations and laws to meet the surging demand for medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in post-Roe America.\par \par \uc2 Some are using physician discretion to prescribe pills to patients further along in pregnancy than the 10-week limit set by the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2 . Some are making pills available to women who are not pregnant but feel they could need them someday. Some are employing a don't-ask-don't-tell approach, providing telemedicine consultations and prescriptions without verifying that patients are in states that permit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 These changes are easing access to the pills for patients in states that have curtailed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and also in states where it remains legal, but where clinics have longer wait times as patients flood in from restrictive states.\par \par \uc2 Some of the practices, like not confirming that telemedicine patients are located in states that allow \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , may run afoul of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  state laws or fall into uncharted legal territory, but they may also be challenging to police, \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  experts said.\par \par \uc2 ''We're going to see these different approaches by organizations as they assess what the laws say and develop their rationale for how to provide care,'' said \uc2 Elizabeth Nash\uc2 , state policy analyst for the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , a research group supporting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. ''We just don't have a road map about how to provide medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  post-Roe, so it's all being created right now.''\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents criticized the efforts, calling them risky for women. Tessa Longbons, senior research associate at the Charlotte Lozier Institute, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  organization, said the method had greater rates of complications and failure after 10 weeks that ''could just put women at risk.'' Providing pills to women who are not pregnant, she said, creates a risk that a woman ''could take it anytime or someone that it wasn't initially prescribed to could end up taking it.''\par \par \uc2 Medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which was legalized in the United States in 2000, typically involves two drugs: mifepristone, which blocks a hormone necessary for pregnancy development, followed 24 to 48 hours later by misoprostol, which causes contractions that expel pregnancy tissue.\par \par \uc2 Patients with some medical issues, like bleeding disorders, are not prescribed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills. But for the many patients who are medically eligible, data indicates medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is safe and effective, with a small percentage of patients requiring a procedure to fully remove pregnancy tissue and an even smaller proportion experiencing serious complications.\par \par \uc2 The method is less expensive, less invasive and, especially with telemedicine, more private than surgical abortions. By 2020, it accounted for over half of U.S. abortions and has become even more sought-after since Roe was overturned.\par \par \uc2 In the fast-changing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  landscape, new online medication-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services are starting, and existing services are expanding, often adopting some of the new practices. Legally, this is a new frontier, both supporters and opponents of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights say. It's unclear, for example, whether any anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  state laws -- which typically target providers, not patients -- address providing pills to someone who is not pregnant.\par \par \uc2 ''As these new practices are developing, it is likely that they will be in a legal gray area,'' said John Seago, president of Texas Right to Life, who said current Texas laws only involve providing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to women known to be pregnant.\par \par \uc2 Other practices, like those that help patients in states with bans obtain pills, might violate those laws. But questions remain. Most providers using these practices prescribe and distribute pills only within states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal. If those legally prescribed pills are then used by residents of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  states, it's unclear if those states can prosecute out-of-state providers.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Seago, who has a doctorate in bioethics, said new legislation would probably be introduced to combat the new practices. ''Legislators are committed to figuring out how to enforce these laws,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Beyond the F.D.A. Threshold\par \par \uc2 Before Roe was overturned, few American services offered pills beyond the F.D.A.'s 10-week threshold, but many are now.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Telemedicine, which was started after a draft of the Supreme Court ruling overturning Roe leaked in May, serves patients throughout the first trimester, which is 13 weeks into pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 That company, and others serving patients at 11 or 12 weeks' gestation, can legally use medical discretion to do so because studies suggest that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills are safe and effective at that stage. The \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  supports medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  through 12 weeks' gestation.\par \par \uc2 Dr. \uc2 Daniel Grossman\uc2 , a professor of obstetrics, gynecology and reproductive sciences at the \uc2 University of California, San Francisco\uc2 , said that late in the first trimester, medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is safe and effective, but that there's ''a somewhat higher risk of some complications, including heavy bleeding,'' and an additional dose of misoprostol is often needed to fully expel the tissue.\par \par \uc2 Some services, including \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Telemedicine, automatically send a second round of the four misoprostol tablets for patients undergoing late first-trimester abortions.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  experts said patients should be advised that, while earlier in pregnancy, expelled tissue resembles a heavy period, after 10 weeks, it can appear more fetus-like. Dr. Abigail R.A. Aiken, an associate professor at the \uc2 University of Texas, Austin\uc2 , who leads a medication-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  research group, said preparing patients for what the tissue might look like could also help them guard against legal risk in states banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- for example, in a situation where a patient is surprised by what they see and ''then is disclosing that to someone who's like 'Well, I'm going to report you.'''\par \par \uc2 Joann, 23, a single mother, was already 10 weeks pregnant when she decided to abort, so she contacted \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Telemedicine. She said she initially planned to carry her pregnancy to term, but then her 3-year-old son was diagnosed with autism and her employer, the U.S. military, decided to transfer her to another state. Joann, who asked to be identified only by her first name to protect her privacy, was in Colorado at the time, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal, but her community was conservative.\par \par \uc2 The service's nurse practitioner told her that since she'd be taking the pills after 10 weeks' gestation, she should expect more pain and bleeding, and counseled that the expelled tissue might resemble a fetus ''so that I would be prepared for it,'' Joann said.\par \par \uc2 At 11 weeks and two days pregnant, she took the mifepristone, followed by the first four-tablet dose of misoprostol the next day and the second round six hours later. The cramping hurt, but ''it was bearable,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Providing Pills Before Pregnancy\par \par \uc2 Some services are offering ''advance provision'' of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to patients who aren't pregnant. Christie Pitney, a midwife who works with Aid Access, a medication-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  service based in Europe that works with U.S. providers, likened it to travelers who ''get medication for traveler's diarrhea or for altitude sickness before you actually need to use it.'' She said patients completed medical consultations before receiving prescriptions and were asked to contact the service again for an evaluation before taking the pills. Mifepristone's shelf life is three to five years and misoprostol's is 18 to 24 months, experts say.\par \par \uc2 Several \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  experts have recently endorsed advance provision, suggesting that safety risks are low if patients are medically screened before being prescribed pills and before taking them, and if they have access to medical care if needed. Still, providers acknowledge that in an environment where many states restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , some patients may not follow appropriate guidelines. An article supporting advance provision, co-written by Dr. Grossman at U.C.S.F., noted that patients could take the pills in inappropriate circumstances or give them to others, and recommended further study.\par \par \uc2 Ms. \uc2 Pitney\uc2  said Aid Access, which began offering advance provision in the United States in September 2021, was receiving about 40 requests per day earlier this year, but that the week after the Supreme Court decision, over 10,000 requests poured in.\par \par \uc2 Hana, 31, said she sought advance provision because she lives in Arizona, a conservative state where a ban or other restrictions could take effect, and which is one of 19 states prohibiting telemedicine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Hana, a claims researcher for a health insurer, who asked to be identified by only her first name to protect her privacy, said she tried to order from Aid Access the day the Supreme Court overturned Roe, but couldn't get onto the website because traffic was so heavy. Two days later, she succeeded.\par \par \uc2 After patients complete medical consultation forms, Aid Access ships from within the United States to patients in states with legal telemedicine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while patients in restrictive states, like Hana's, receive pills from a pharmacy in India. In 2019, the F.D.A. tried unsuccessfully to get Aid Access to halt overseas shipping. The organization's founder, Dr. Rebecca Gomperts, a Dutch physician, said that U.S. Customs had occasionally stopped packages, but that most arrived without incident.\par \par \uc2 ''I have it hidden in my closet,'' said Hana, who lives alone, but doesn't want guests to stumble upon the medication. ''I'm a little nervous,'' she said, but added, ''It's really nice to have it just in case.''\par \par \uc2 An Honor System for Patients' Location\par \par \uc2 Some services check IP addresses to ensure that during telemedicine-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  consultations patients are located in states where the practice is legal, but a growing number have decided not to verify location.\par \par \uc2 ''We don't have any barriers such as an ID verification or GPS validation,'' Dr. Jayaram Brindala, the founder of \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Telemedicine, said. His company arranges video consultations in 17 states where telemedicine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal and its appointment-booking form asks patients where they live, but ''it's on their honor,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Some patients indicate they reside in states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions and are traveling to states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal to receive pills at an address there, where the company can legally ship them, Dr. Brindala said.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Pitney, the midwife with Aid Access, who also runs a service based in the United States called Forward Midwifery, said a shipping address was the only geographical information those services required. Only if patients mention they are doing the medical consultation from a state that prohibits telemedicine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or shipping of pills will she say she cannot legally treat them and refer them to Plan C, an online clearinghouse for information about medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Plan C recently added information about ''virtual mailboxes'' with commercial mail-forwarding companies: addresses in states where pills can legally be shipped and forwarded to patients in restrictive states. Forwarding companies are most likely unaware of the contents of the nondescript packages.\par \par \uc2 ''You're using a legal mailing service and you're using a legal telehealth service and you're getting F.D.A.-approved products from a clinician with a prescription and the providers are fully compliant with the rules,'' said Elisa Wells, co-founder and co-director of Plan C, which tested mail-forwarding companies by shipping bottles filled with garbanzo beans to approximate the rattling of pill bottles.\par \par \uc2 Some medication-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers are trying to broaden access while following F.D.A. guidelines and verifying patients' locations.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Demand, which serves patients only up to nine weeks' gestation, checks that patients do their video consultations from one of 22 states or Washington, D.C., all locations where telemedicine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal, and it will not ship to mail-forwarding companies or P.O. Boxes.\par \par \uc2 ''We very explicitly say that we're sort of banning anything that may not just put us in legal jeopardy, but really put patients and put patients' friends who are helping them in legal jeopardy,'' Leah Coplon, director of clinical operations, said.\par \par \uc2 Still, she said, her organization recently expanded to Pennsylvania. That state allows telemedicine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but since \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Demand has no office in Pennsylvania, state law prevents it from shipping pills there. Instead, Pennsylvania patients pick up pills at \uc2 FedEx\uc2  sites in New Jersey or Maryland.\par \par \uc2 ''We've had a lot of interest,'' she said. ''If the Pennsylvania model works well, there may be the potential to expand it in other areas.''\par \par \uc2 New medication \uc2  services are springing up and existing services are expanding, some adopting new practices that increase access while pushing the envelope of regulations and laws. (PHOTOGRAPH BY Kelly Burgess for The New York Times FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220904ei9400046\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
