\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Michigan  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Rights Issue Is Off Ballot\b0\par\par\uc2 By Mitch Smith \par \uc2 548 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 September 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters gathered more than 750,000 signatures, but the petitions they circulated had formatting problems.\par \par \uc2 A state board in Michigan refused on Wednesday to place an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights referendum on the November ballot because of a dispute over word spacing on the petition, an embarrassing blow to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters who had gathered more than 750,000 signatures.\par \par \uc2 The decision, which came when the Board of State Canvassers deadlocked along party lines, could still be overturned by the courts. But it injected further uncertainty into the fate of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Michigan, a swing state where enforcement of a pre-Roe v. Wade \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban has been temporarily blocked by a judge and where many closely watched races are on this year's ballot.\par \par \uc2 Tony Daunt, the Republican chair of the canvassing board, said a lack of visible spaces between words on the petitions shown to voters represented ''an egregious error.''\par \par \uc2 Reproductive Freedom for All, a group supporting the amendment, accused the canvassers of disenfranchising voters and vowed to challenge the decision in court. The two Republicans on the board voted against placing the issue on the ballot, while the two Democratic canvassers supported presenting the question to voters.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters across the country have turned to referendums as a way to maintain or restore access to the procedure after Roe was overturned in June. Voters in California and Vermont are poised to decide whether to enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in their state constitutions, while Kentuckians will choose whether to make it clear that there is no state right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In Kansas, voters overwhelmingly decided in August to preserve a state-level right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Michigan voters seemed likely to have their say, as well, after \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters submitted hundreds of thousands more signatures than they needed to put a constitutional amendment to a statewide vote. But questions quickly emerged about the formatting on the petitions, which included no discernible spaces between some words.\par \par \uc2 Eric Doster, a lawyer for a group that challenged the petitions, told the canvassers on Wednesday that the document ''contains nonsense passages, and nonsense cannot be put into the Michigan Constitution.''\par \par \uc2 Supporters of the amendment insisted that there were indeed spaces between the words and argued that, in any case, it was not the job of the state canvassing board, an appointed group that certifies statewide elections and approves language for ballot proposals, to weigh in on the content of the petitions. ''You must ignore the challenges and their fabricated criteria,'' said Olivia Flower, a lawyer for supporters of the amendment.\par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  and Attorney General Dana Nessel, both Democrats facing re-election races, have made support of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights central to their campaigns.\par \par \uc2 But unless the Michigan Supreme Court overrules the canvassers, the future of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state is likely to be determined by a disputed law that was passed in 1931. That measure, which has been blocked by a judge, bans most abortions in Michigan and is unlikely to be repealed by the Republican-controlled Legislature.\par \par \uc2 An \uc2  rights demonstrator in Detroit after the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade in June. (PHOTOGRAPH BY Emily Elconin/Getty Images FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220901ei910004f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
