\par\par \uc2 ROUND TABLE\par \uc2 Sunday Review Desk; SECTSR\par \b \uc2 'A Stirring of Democratic Hearts': Three Writers Discuss an Altered Midterm Landscape\b0\par\par\uc2 By Frank Bruni, Molly Jong-Fast and Doug Sosnik \par \uc2 2,246 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 August 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Frank Bruni, a contributing Opinion writer, hosted an online conversation with Molly Jong-Fast, the writer of the ''Wait, What?'' newsletter for The Atlantic, and Doug Sosnik, a former senior adviser to President \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 , to discuss whether the Democrats have shifted the narrative of the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 FRANK BRUNI: Doug, Molly, an apology -- because we're doing this in cyberspace rather than a physical place, I cannot offer you any refreshments, which is a shame, because I do a killer crudit\u233\'00\'E9.\par \par \uc2 MOLLY JONG-FAST: The case of Dr. Oz is baffling. I continue to be completely in awe of how bad he is at this.\par \par \uc2 DOUG SOSNIK: He is a terrible candidate, but he is really just one of many right-wing and unqualified candidates running for the Senate and governor. \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  in Georgia and most of the Republican ticket in Arizona are probably even more unqualified.\par \par \uc2 BRUNI: Let's pivot from roughage to the rough-and-tumble of the midterms. There's a stirring of Democratic hearts, a blooming of Democratic hopes, a belief that falling gas prices, key legislative accomplishments and concern about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights equal a reprieve from the kind of midterm debacle that Democrats feared just a month or two ago.\par \par \uc2 Doug, do you now envision Democrats doing much better than we once thought possible?\par \par \uc2 SOSNIK: I do. Up until the start of the primaries and the Dobbs decision overturning Roe, this looked like a classic midterm election in which the party in power gets shellacked. It has happened in the past four midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 BRUNI: Is it possible we're reading too much into the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  factor?\par \par \uc2 JONG-FAST: No, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a much bigger deal than any of the pundit class realizes. Because \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't just about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BRUNI: Doug, do you agree?\par \par \uc2 SOSNIK: I am increasingly nervous about making predictions, but I do feel safe in saying that this issue will increase in importance as more people see the real-life implications of the Roe decision. So, yes, I agree that it will impact the midterms. But it will actually take on even more importance in 2024 and beyond.\par \par \uc2 JONG-FAST: One of the biggest things we've seen since the Dobbs decision is doctors terrified to treat women who are having gynecological complications. In 1973, one of the reasons Roe was decided so broadly was because some doctors didn't feel safe treating women. We're having a messy return to that, which is a nightmare for the right.\par \par \uc2 SOSNIK: For decades, the getting-candidates-elected wing of the Republican Party -- which means people like \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  -- has had a free ride with the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . They have been able to use it to seed their base but have not been forced to pay a political price. With the overturning of Roe, that has all changed. And polling shows that a majority of Americans don't agree with their extreme positions.\par \par \uc2 JONG-FAST: I also think a lot of suburban women are really, really mad, and people who don't care about politics at all are furious. Remember the whole news cycle devoted to the 10-year-old rape victim in Ohio having to go out of state for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Roe is seismic.\par \par \uc2 BRUNI: I noticed that in an NBC News poll released last week, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  wasn't one of the top five answers when voters were asked about the most important issue facing the country. Fascinatingly -- and to me, hearteningly -- more voters chose threats to democracy than the cost of living or jobs and the economy. Do you think that could truly be a motivating, consequential factor in the midterms? Or do you think \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will still make the bigger difference?\par \par \uc2 SOSNIK: There are two issues in midterms: turnout and persuasion. I am quite confident that the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue will motivate people to vote. The NBC poll shows that Democrats have closed the enthusiasm gap for voting to two points, which since March is a 15-point improvement. And for persuasion, those suburban women swing voters will be motivated by this issue to not only vote but to vote against the Republicans.\par \par \uc2 BRUNI: Is this election really going to be all about turnout, or will swing voters matter just as much? And which groups of Democratic voters are you most worried won't, in the end, turn out to the extent that they should?\par \par \uc2 SOSNIK: Yes, this midterm will be primarily about turnout. For Democrats, I would start by worrying about young people turning out, which was no doubt on the administration's mind when it released a plan on Wednesday to forgive student loans.\par \par \uc2 There is also a pretty sizable group of Democrats who have soured on President Biden. They are critical for the Democrats to turn out.\par \par \uc2 BRUNI: Molly, Doug just mentioned President Biden's announcement that he was forgiving some college debt for some Americans. Is that decision likely to be a net positive for the party, drawing grateful voters to the polls, or a net negative, alienating some Democrats -- and energizing many Republicans -- who think he's being fiscally profligate and playing favorites?\par \par \uc2 JONG-FAST: I grew up extremely privileged and for years grappled with the issue of fairness. In my mind, $10,000 was the floor for debt forgiveness. I am particularly pleased with the $20,000 for Pell grant recipients who qualify. I never thought America was a fair country, and it's become increasingly unfair. Biden was elected with this promise, and he's keeping it. I think that should help turn out the base.\par \par \uc2 SOSNIK: Student loan forgiveness is a Rorschach test for voters. If you believe in government and a progressive agenda, it is great news. If you think that the Democrats are a bunch of big spenders and worried about the elites -- the 38 percent of the country that gets a four-year college degree -- then it will work against them.\par \par \uc2 BRUNI: Will former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's feud with the Department of Justice and the F.B.I. after the Mar-a-Lago search boost Republican turnout and work to the party's advantage?\par \par \uc2 JONG-FAST: Trump has been fighting with parts of the government for years. I'm not sure how fresh that narrative is. The people who are Trump's people will continue to be Trump's people, but much of this persecution-complex narrative is old.\par \par \uc2 SOSNIK: The F.B.I. raid goes with several other items -- Jan. 6, Roe, the Trump-endorsed right-wing nominees -- that are driving this to be what I'd call a choice election.\par \par \uc2 There have been only two elections since World War II when the incumbent party did not lose House seats in the midterms -- 1998 and 2002 -- 2002 was an outlier, since it was really a reaction to 9/11.\par \par \uc2 Nineteen ninety-eight was a choice election: We were in the middle of impeachment when the country largely felt that the Republicans were overreaching; 2022 could be only the second choice midterm election since World War II.\par \par \uc2 BRUNI: Democratic hopes focus on keeping control of the Senate or even expanding their majority there. Is the House a lost cause?\par \par \uc2 JONG-FAST: The result of the special election in New York's 19th Congressional District on Tuesday -- widely considered a bellwether contest for control of the House in November, and in which the Democrat, Pat Ryan, beat a well-known, favored Republican, \uc2 Marc Molinaro\uc2 , by two points -- makes people think that it is possible for Democrats to keep the House.\par \par \uc2 I know that Democrats have about dozens of fewer safe seats than Republicans. And they hold a very slim majority -- Republicans need to pick up a net of five seats to regain the majority. But I still think it's possible Democrats hold the House.\par \par \uc2 SOSNIK: It will be very difficult for the Democrats to hold the House. They have one of the narrowest margins in the House since the late-19th century. Because of reapportionment and redistricting, the Republicans have a much more favorable battlefield. There are now, in the new map, 16 seats held by Democrats in districts that would have likely voted for Trump. Expecting a bad cycle, over 30 Democrats in the House announced that they would retire.\par \par \uc2 The Cook Report has the Republicans already picking up a net of seven seats, with the majority of the remaining competitive races held by Democrats.\par \par \uc2 BRUNI: I'm going to list Democratic candidates in high-profile Senate races in purple or reddish states that aren't incontrovertibly hostile terrain for the party. For each candidate, tell me if you think victory is probable, possible or improbable. Be bold.\par \par \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 SOSNIK: Probable.\par \par \uc2 JONG-FAST: Probable.\par \par \uc2 BRUNI: Raphael Warnock, Georgia.\par \par \uc2 SOSNIK: Probable.\par \par \uc2 JONG-FAST: Probable.\par \par \uc2 BRUNI: Cheri Beasley, North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 SOSNIK: Possible.\par \par \uc2 JONG-FAST: Possible.\par \par \uc2 BRUNI: Val Demings, Florida.\par \par \uc2 SOSNIK: Possible.\par \par \uc2 JONG-FAST: Ugh, Florida.\par \par \uc2 BRUNI: Mark Kelly, Arizona.\par \par \uc2 SOSNIK: Probable.\par \par \uc2 JONG-FAST: Probable.\par \par \uc2 BRUNI: Mandela Barnes, Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 SOSNIK: Possible.\par \par \uc2 JONG-FAST: Probable.\par \par \uc2 BRUNI: \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 , Ohio.\par \par \uc2 SOSNIK: Possible.\par \par \uc2 JONG-FAST: Possible.\par \par \uc2 BRUNI: \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2 , Nevada.\par \par \uc2 SOSNIK: Possible.\par \par \uc2 JONG-FAST: Probable.\par \par \uc2 BRUNI: Name a Democratic candidate this cycle -- for Senate, House or governor -- who has most positively surprised and impressed you, and tell me why.\par \par \uc2 JONG-FAST: Fetterman is really good at this, and so is his wife. Ryan has been really good. I think Mandela Barnes is really smart. I've interviewed all of those guys for my podcast and thought they were just really good at messaging in a way Democrats are historically not. Val Demings is a once-in-a-lifetime politician, but Florida is Florida.\par \par \uc2 SOSNIK: \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 . I don't know if he can win, but he has proved that a Democrat can be competitive in a state that I now consider a Republican stronghold.\par \par \uc2 BRUNI: OK, let's do a lightning round of final questions. For starters, the Biden presidency so far, rated on a scale of 1 (big disappointment) to 5 (big success), with a sentence or less justifying your rating.\par \par \uc2 JONG-FAST: Four. I wasn't a Biden person, but he's quietly gotten a lot done, more than I thought he could.\par \par \uc2 SOSNIK: Four. They have accomplished a lot under very difficult circumstances.\par \par \uc2 BRUNI: The percentage chance that Biden runs for a second term?\par \par \uc2 JONG-FAST: Fifty percent.\par \par \uc2 SOSNIK: Twenty-five percent.\par \par \uc2 BRUNI: If Biden doesn't run and there's a Democratic primary, name someone other than or in addition to \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  whom you'd like to see enter the fray, and tell me in a phrase why.\par \par \uc2 JONG-FAST: I hate this question. I want to move to a pineapple under the sea.\par \par \uc2 SOSNIK: \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2 . He is an authentic person who understands the pulse of this country.\par \par \uc2 JONG-FAST: I also like \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BRUNI: What's the one issue you think is being most shortchanged, not just in discussions about the midterms but in our political discussions generally?\par \par \uc2 JONG-FAST: The Supreme Court. If Democrats keep the House and the Senate, Biden is still going to have to deal with the wildly out-of-step courts. He will hate doing that, but he's going to have to.\par \par \uc2 SOSNIK: I agree with Molly. On a broader level, we have just completed a realignment in American politics where class, more than race, is driving our politics.\par \par \uc2 BRUNI: Last but by no means least, you must spend either an hour over crudit\u233\'00\'E9 with the noted gourmand Mehmet Oz or an hour gardening with the noted environmentalist \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . What do you choose, and briefly, why?\par \par \uc2 JONG-FAST: I'm a terrible hypochondriac, and Oz was an extremely good surgeon. I would spend an hour with him talking about all my medical anxieties. Does this mole look like anything?\par \par \uc2 SOSNIK: The fact that you are raising that question tells you how bad the candidate recruitment has been for the Republicans this cycle.\par \par \uc2 Other than carrying a football and not getting tackled, Walker has not accomplished much in his life, and his pattern of personal behavior shows him to be unfit to hold elected office.\par \par \uc2 BRUNI: Well, I once spent hours with Oz for a profile and watched him do open-heart surgery, so I'm pulling weeds with Walker, just out of curiosity. And for the fresh air.\par \par \uc2 Frank Bruni (@FrankBruni) is a professor of public policy at Duke, the author of the book ''The Beauty of Dusk'' and a contributing Opinion writer. He writes a weekly email newsletter and can be found on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , \uc2 Instagram\uc2  and \uc2 Facebook\uc2 . Molly Jong-Fast (@MollyJongFast) writes the Wait, What? newsletter for The Atlantic. Doug Sosnik was a senior adviser in President \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 's White House from 1994 to 2000 and is a counselor to the \uc2 Brunswick Group\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Twitter\uc2  (@NYTopinion) and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPHS BY KEN FLOREY SUFFRAGE COLLECTION/GADO, PARAS GRIFFIN, MICHAEL M. SANTIAGO AND SMITH COLLECTION/GADO, VIA GETTY IMAGES) (SR4-SR5) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220828ei8s00044\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
