\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Issue Plays a Part in New York Special Election for House Seat\b0\par\par\uc2 By Katie Glueck \par \uc2 1,111 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 August 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 13\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Pat Ryan, a Democrat, warns against ''pulling our punches'' on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other issues during the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 Within an hour of the Supreme Court's decision overturning Roe v. Wade in June, Pat Ryan, a combat veteran, released an ad for his congressional campaign, stressing his support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. After Kansans overwhelmingly voted to defend \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections this month, Mr. Ryan cast his upcoming race as the next major test of the issue's power.\par \par \uc2 And on Wednesday, hours after Mr. Ryan won his special election in a battleground district in New York's Hudson Valley, he said that the lessons from his contest were clear.\par \par \uc2 ''Stand up and fight. Stop pulling our punches,'' Mr. Ryan said in an interview. ''Conventional wisdom is that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  are a really emotional, very personal topic, but to me that calls even more for being clear, so people really know where your heart is,'' he added.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Ryan's victory in the 19th District of New York quickly topped the list of signs that the fall campaigns may be more competitive, in more places, than strategists in both parties had once anticipated.\par \par \uc2 The New York race also came a few weeks after the Kansas referendum, which showed that voters even in traditionally conservative states believe it is possible to go too far in restricting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights -- but that was an up-or-down vote on a single ballot question. Democrats quickly looked to Mr. Ryan's race to test whether the issue could resonate in a congressional contest, as voters weighed two personalities and a range of other considerations.\par \par \uc2 Special elections are always an imperfect gauge of the electorate. But Mr. Ryan was one of several Democrats to outperform President Biden's 2020 numbers in contests this summer. Mr. Ryan's victory is the latest evidence that once apathetic Democrats who had trailed Republicans on questions of enthusiasm are now increasingly engaged, even as the party continues to face significant political headwinds. And some Republicans acknowledge that they no longer have total ownership on enthusiasm.\par \par \uc2 ''I worry about us being complacent going into the fall, everyone thinking that these are going to be kind of massive waves, no end in sight, red, red, red, and -- doesn't appear to be that way,'' said \uc2 Robert Blizzard\uc2 , a veteran Republican pollster. ''It's as if the goal posts have been pushed back a bit on us.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Blizzard cautioned against overreading the results of special elections and stressed that Democrats continue to face staggering challenges, especially amid Mr. Biden's abysmal approval ratings.\par \par \uc2 ''Until Biden's approval rating gets out of the toilet with independent voters, it's still going to be a very good year for Republicans,'' he said. Still, he added, ''The angrier people is where the energy always is. But you can't overturn Roe v. Wade and not have some more fired-up Democrats.''\par \par \uc2 A recent NBC poll found that Democrats had closed a significant enthusiasm gap: 68 percent of Republicans expressed a high level of interest in the upcoming election, while 66 percent of Democrats said the same. In March, Republicans had a 17 percentage point advantage on that question, according to NBC.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Tom Bonier\uc2 , the chief executive of TargetSmart, a Democratic data firm, has released data showing that since the Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization decision handed control over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections back to the states, there has been a surge in voter registration among women. They are ''mostly younger, and they're overwhelmingly Democrats,'' he said in an interview, adding on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  on Wednesday, ''Women accounted for 58% of the early/absentee votes in NY19, despite comprising only 52% of registered voters.''\par \par \uc2 ''Women have surged in registration in ways that we've never seen in this country,'' he said. Whether that dynamic lasts through November is an open question, he said, but for now, ''it shows no signs of slowing down,'' he added.\par \par \uc2 What was especially striking about the result in New York, Mr. Bonier noted, was that the Republican candidate, \uc2 Marc Molinaro\uc2 , could be regarded as a ''pre-Trump Republican.'' In other words, he is a county executive who is well-known locally and kept his messaging far more focused on crime and inflation than on the far-right cultural battles some candidates have embraced.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Molinaro has said both that he would vote against a federal law ensuring \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access nationwide and that he would oppose a nationwide ban. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal in New York.\par \par \uc2 Headed into the special election, some Republicans cautioned that Mr. Ryan could do better than public polling suggested because the contest was held at the same time as New York's primaries -- when independent voters, who appear deeply unhappy with President Biden, are unaccustomed to turning out. Other fundamentals, they argue -- including dissatisfaction with the direction of the country -- continue to work against the party in power.\par \par \uc2 ''Democratic voters were energized by the Supreme Court decision, and because we were running in the midst of a Democratic primary, they showed up to vote,'' Mr. Molinaro said in an interview.\par \par \uc2 Through a quirk of New York's complicated redistricting process, both candidates will run again in November but not against each other. Mr. Molinaro is running for a full term in the new 19th Congressional District. Mr. Ryan is seeking a full term in the neighboring 18th District, which will now contain his home in Ulster County.\par \par \uc2 ''November is going to be about, as midterm elections always are, a check on the party in power,'' Mr. Molinaro said, arguing that cost of living remained top of mind for many voters. ''The dynamic and the turnout and, I believe, the results, are going to be different.''\par \par \uc2 For now, though, it was evident that Mr. Ryan's efforts to frame the contest as a referendum on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights had resonated with some voters.\par \par \uc2 ''You're going to force a religious right, fundamentalist Christian whatever on the whole country?'' said John Ravine, as he came out to vote for Mr. Ryan on Tuesday. Describing himself as ''staunchly Catholic,'' Mr. Ravine expressed his concerns about the rollbacks of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights across the country. ''You can't do that. You can't hold a country hostage.''\par \par \uc2 Grace Ashford contributed reporting from Hudson, N.Y.\par \par \uc2 Grace Ashford contributed reporting from Hudson, N.Y.\par \par \uc2 Pat Ryan triumphed in the Hudson Valley with a message focusing on \uc2 , ''so people know where your heart is.'' (PHOTOGRAPH BY RICHARD BEAVEN FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220825ei8p00045\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
