\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Judge Pauses Part of Idaho's  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Ban\b0\par\par\uc2 By Glenn Thrush \par \uc2 887 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 August 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 13\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The preliminary injunction was a limited but significant victory for the Biden administration after the Justice Department sued Idaho this month.\par \par \uc2 A federal judge in Idaho blocked part of the state's strict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban on Wednesday, delivering a limited but significant victory to the Biden administration, which has tried to use its limited power to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade in June.\par \par \uc2 This month, the Justice Department sued Idaho, one of the most conservative states in the country, arguing that the law would prevent emergency room doctors from performing abortions necessary to stabilize the health of women facing medical emergencies.\par \par \uc2 Judge B. Lynn Winmill of the Federal District Court in Idaho wrote that doctors in the state could not be punished for acting to protect the health of endangered mothers, in a preliminary injunction issued a day before the ban was to be enacted.\par \par \uc2 But he emphasized the narrow scope of the decision, leaving intact most of the bill's other provisions, which constitute a near-total prohibition on the procedure in the state as allowed under the Supreme Court's Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization ruling in June.\par \par \uc2 ''It's not about the bygone constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' he wrote. ''The court is called upon to address a far more modest issue -- whether Idaho's criminal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  statute conflicts with a small but important corner of federal legislation. It does.''\par \par \uc2 Judge Winmill, who was appointed by President \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  in 1995, said some of the Idaho law's provisions required medical personnel to do ''the opposite'' of what their training dictates: ''to effectively identify problems and treat them promptly so patients are stabilized before they develop a life-threatening emergency.''\par \par \uc2 The pause on enforcement will continue until a lawsuit challenging the ban is resolved, the judge said.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department argued that the measure violated a federal law known as the Emergency Medical Treatment and Labor Act, which requires medical professionals in hospitals that accept Medicare funding to take whatever steps are necessary to ensure the physical well-being of patients, including pregnant women.\par \par \uc2 ''Today's decision by the District Court for the District of Idaho ensures that women in the state of Idaho can obtain the emergency medical treatment to which they are entitled under federal law,'' said Attorney General \uc2 Merrick B. Garland\uc2  in a statement. ''This includes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  when that is the necessary treatment.''\par \par \uc2 He added that the department would ''use every tool at its disposal to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  protected by federal law.''\par \par \uc2 The administration's victory in court came less than 24 hours after a defeat on a closely related conflict between a blue White House and a red state house in a much more populous state. On Tuesday, a federal judge in Texas ruled in favor of the state's lawsuit against the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2 , which directed hospitals receiving federal aid to follow federal laws when the health of a mother is at stake even if it clashes with new state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans.\par \par \uc2 But the Idaho case also illustrates the on-the-ground complexities of the situation -- especially the difficulties in drafting state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws that both satisfy the demands of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  politicians and shield medical practitioners from severe punishment for doing their jobs.\par \par \uc2 Doctors in Idaho who perform abortions face criminal penalties under the law, although they can appeal by proving their actions were necessary to prevent a pregnant woman from dying.\par \par \uc2 State officials did not immediately respond to news of the ruling, but are expected to appeal the decision. Gov. \uc2 Brad Little\uc2  of Idaho, a Republican, has described the Justice Department's lawsuit as ''federal meddling.'' The Supreme Court ''returned the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the states to regulate -- end of story,'' he said this month.\par \par \uc2 Most abortions are now banned in at least 10 states since the Supreme Court issued its ruling, and restrictions in a handful of other states, including Idaho, North Dakota, Tennessee and Texas, would take effect this month, barring court challenges. The Justice Department's ability to influence policies in Republican states that have adopted so-called trigger laws, which effectively banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  almost immediately after the Supreme Court's ruling, is limited, and the actions are likely to affect only a small percentage of pregnant women.\par \par \uc2 Idaho's trigger law does allow abortions in cases involving rape or when a woman's life is in danger. But there are no provisions made for women who are likely to incur serious health problems, including organ damage, by continuing their pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court's decision, and the race by conservative-leaning states to impose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, has prompted a powerful political backlash. In early August, voters in Kansas resoundingly decided to preserve a 2019 ruling by the state's Supreme Court interpreting the state Constitution as protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. The ballot initiative was the first referendum on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights since the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 's decision.\par \par \uc2 Charlie Savage contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Charlie Savage contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Under Idaho's ''trigger law,'' there are no provisions made for women who are likely to incur serious health problems. (PHOTOGRAPH BY ANGIE SMITH FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220825ei8p0003m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
