\par\par \uc2 ALEX KINGSBURY\par \uc2 Editorial Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 To Exist in 2022 Is to Be Tracked and Monitored\b0\par\par\uc2 By Alex Kingsbury \par \uc2 942 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 August 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 19\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Whenever I see one of those billboards that read: ''Privacy. That's iPhone,'' I'm overcome by the urge to cast my own iPhone into a river. Of lava.\par \par \uc2 That's not because the iPhone is any better or worse than other smartphones when it comes to digital privacy. (I'd take an iPhone over an Android phone in a second; I enjoy the illusion of control over my digital life as much as the next person.)\par \par \uc2 What's infuriating is the idea that carrying around the most sophisticated tracking and monitoring device ever forged by the hand of man is consistent with any understanding of privacy. It's not. At least not with any conception of privacy our species had pre-iPhone.\par \par \uc2 Reconciling the idea of privacy with our digital world demands embracing a profound cognitive dissonance. To exist in 2022 is to be surveilled, tracked, tagged and monitored -- most often for profit. Short of going off the grid, there's no way around it.\par \par \uc2 Consider just last week: \uc2 Apple\uc2  released a surprise software update for its iPhones, iPads and Macs meant to remove vulnerabilities the company says may have been exploited by sophisticated hackers. The week before that, a former \uc2 Google\uc2  engineer discovered that \uc2 Meta\uc2 , the parent company of \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , was using a piece of code to track users of the \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  apps across the internet without their knowledge. In Greece the prime minister and his government have been consumed by a widening scandal in which they are accused of spying on the smartphones of an opposition leader and a journalist.\par \par \uc2 And this month \uc2 Amazon\uc2  announced that it was creating a show called ''Ring Nation'' -- a sort of ''America's Funniest Home Videos'' made up of footage recorded by the company's Ring doorbells. These video doorbells, sold by \uc2 Amazon\uc2  and other companies, are now watching millions of American homes, and they are often used by police departments as, effectively, surveillance networks. All in the name of fighting crime, of course.\par \par \uc2 Step back, and what we're looking at is a world where privacy simply doesn't exist anymore. Instead of talking about old notions of privacy and how to defend or get back to that ideal state, we should start talking about what comes next.\par \par \uc2 That reality is becoming clearer to Americans after the Supreme Court's decision in Dobbs, which eliminated the federal right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . They now understand that their phone location data, internet searches and purchase history are all fair game for the police -- especially in states that do not protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and where women can be hunted down for their health care choices. If the courts once defended the right to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as part of a broader right to privacy, by vaporizing that right, the Roberts court shattered many of Americans' conceptions of privacy as well.\par \par \uc2 In 2019, Times Opinion investigated the location tracking industry. Whistleblowers gave us a data set that included millions of pings from individual cellphones around daily commutes, churches and mosques, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics, the Pentagon, even the headquarters of the \uc2 Central Intelligence Agency\uc2 . ''If the government ordered Americans to continuously provide such precise, real-time information about themselves, there would be a revolt,'' the editorial board wrote.\par \par \uc2 Yet despite years of talk, Congress is no closer to passing robust privacy legislation than it was two decades ago when the idea first came up. Even its baby steps aren't encouraging. Two bills in the current session aim to roll back some of this mass monitoring around \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  in particular, although neither one is likely to pass.\par \par \uc2 One, the Fourth Amendment Is Not for Sale Act, would prevent law enforcement and other government agencies from purchasing location data and other sensitive information from data brokers. Another, the My Body, My Data Act, would forbid tech companies to keep, use or share some personal health information absent written consent. Neither bill would prevent police officers with a court order from getting such information.\par \par \uc2 Some tech companies, like \uc2 Google\uc2 , have announced voluntary measures to protect some user data around \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care. A group of hundreds of \uc2 Google\uc2  employees is circulating a petition to strengthen privacy protections for users who look for information about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  through its search engine.\par \par \uc2 But even if those bills pass and some tech companies take more steps, there are simply too many tech companies, government entities, data brokers, internet service providers and others tracking everything we do.\par \par \uc2 Protecting digital privacy is not in the interest of the government, and voters don't seem to care much about privacy at all. Nor is it in the interest of tech companies, which sell users' private data for a profit to advertisers. There are too many cameras, cell towers and inscrutable artificial intelligence engines in operation to live an unobserved life.\par \par \uc2 For years, privacy advocates, who foresaw the contours of the surveilled world we now live in, warned that privacy was a necessary prerequisite for democracy, human rights and a flourishing of the human spirit. We're about to find out what happens when that privacy has all but vanished.\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Twitter\uc2  (@NYTopinion) and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY Dan Saelinger/Trunk Archive FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220824ei8o00049\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
