\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Drama and Key Choices Await Voters in Primaries For New York and Florida\b0\par\par\uc2 By Katie Glueck \par \uc2 1,315 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 August 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 11\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Democrats in Florida will choose who will face Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , and House races across New York could set the tone for November.\par \par \uc2 A race to take on \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . A battle of House committee chairs. A special election that will test the political power of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 A series of high-profile races will unfold on Tuesday in New York and Florida as the 2022 midterm primaries arrive in two of the nation's most populous states.\par \par \uc2 Late-August elections can be unpredictable. Here are a few dynamics to watch for as results arrive on Tuesday:\par \par \uc2 Who will emerge to take on DeSantis?\par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  of Florida is often discussed as a possible 2024 presidential contender, but first he needs to get through his re-election campaign.\par \par \uc2 Representative \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2 , who served as governor from 2007 to 2011, and \uc2 Nikki Fried\uc2 , Florida's agriculture commissioner, are the leading candidates in a bitter battle for the Democratic nomination to take him on.\par \par \uc2 Their contest, in which both candidates have questioned the other's liberal bona fides, has been defined less by sharp ideological differences than by contrasts in style.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Crist, a veteran of Florida politics, is the better-funded candidate. He has cast himself as a politically seasoned unifier who emphasizes civility. And while his electoral track record is mixed, he is a well-known figure in the state. He was elected governor in 2006 as a Republican, lost a Senate race as an independent in 2010 and was defeated as a Democrat in the 2014 governor's race. Since 2017, he has represented a St. Petersburg-area seat in Congress.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Fried, the only Democrat holding statewide office, has noted her potential as a history-maker -- Florida has never had a female governor -- and has tried to tap into the fury concerning the overturning of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 The winner of the primary is expected to face a difficult general election against the well-funded, well-known and nationally polarizing incumbent.\par \par \uc2 Can Democrats channel anger over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights into victory in a congressional special election?\par \par \uc2 Democrats were exuberant when Kansas voters overwhelmingly rejected an effort to strip away \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections from their state constitution. But that was a single-issue referendum.\par \par \uc2 A special election in the Hudson Valley region of New York on Tuesday will offer an important early measure of how powerful \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights can be in a congressional race, as voters assess a range of other issues.\par \par \uc2 In the race for the 19th District -- a battleground seat vacated by Antonio Delgado, a Democrat, when he became New York's lieutenant governor -- Pat Ryan, who serves as the executive of Ulster County, has put \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights at the center of his campaign.\par \par \uc2 ''Choice Is on the Ballot,'' one Ryan yard sign reads.\par \par \uc2 Marc Molinaro\uc2 , the Republican executive of Dutchess County who ran for governor in 2018, has focused more on themes of public safety and the cost of living, moving to take advantage of voter frustration with Democratic-controlled Washington.\par \par \uc2 Both parties are watching the race as a snapshot, however imperfect, of political energy less than three months before the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 In Manhattan, a Democratic primary pits House incumbents against each other\par \par \uc2 Representatives \uc2 Jerrold Nadler\uc2  and \uc2 Carolyn B. Maloney\uc2  are veteran New York City lawmakers who occupy powerful perches in Washington. But a court-ordered redrawing of New York's congressional district lines, combining the East and West Sides of Manhattan into a single district, ensured that at least one would not be returning to Congress, setting off a divisive and increasingly ugly Manhattan matchup.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Nadler, a fixture of the Upper West Side who leads the House Judiciary Committee, is running as a liberal lawmaker and his allies have emphasized his standing as the city's only remaining Jewish congressman (though that could change depending on the outcome of other races).\par \par \uc2 Ms. Maloney, who has long represented an affluent East Side district, is the chair of the House Committee on Oversight and Reform, the first woman to hold that role. She had made gender a central part of her pitch, especially after the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 And Suraj Patel, a lawyer, is running an underdog campaign, urging generational change as he competes against two lawmakers who were both elected to Congress three decades ago.\par \par \uc2 The race is not expected to be competitive in the general election, but given the stature of both Mr. Nadler and Ms. Maloney in Washington, it has drawn notable national attention.\par \par \uc2 New York House primaries will test the direction of both parties\par \par \uc2 New York is home to a pileup of primaries on Tuesday. There are House contests on Long Island and in the Syracuse area that will set the stage for competitive general election races and bitterly divisive New York City races that are revealing sharp divisions within the Democratic Party, among other battles.\par \par \uc2 The crowded race for New York's 10th District, which under new lines includes Lower Manhattan and swaths of Brooklyn, falls into the sharp-divisions category.\par \par \uc2 Daniel S. Goldman, the chief investigator for House Democrats in the first impeachment of \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 , has led the very limited recent polling conducted in the race, and has put at least $4 million of his own money into the contest. A number of candidates are seen as running to his left, including Carlina Rivera, a city councilwoman; Yuh-Line Niou, a state assemblywoman; and Representative \uc2 Mondaire Jones\uc2 , who currently represents a different district but moved to the area in the redistricting shuffle.\par \par \uc2 On the other side of the ideological spectrum, a Republican primary in Western New York has pitted the state party chairman, \uc2 Nick Langworthy\uc2 , against Carl Paladino, a fixture of New York Republican politics with a long history of making incendiary or racist comments. Mr. Paladino has said he has received support from a number of far-right Republicans including Representatives \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  and \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2  (Mr. Gaetz, in Florida, faces his own primary on Tuesday.). Representative \uc2 Elise Stefanik\uc2  of New York, a member of House Republican leadership, is also backing Mr. Paladino.\par \par \uc2 Separately, in Oklahoma, two conservative candidates have advanced to Tuesday's Republican runoff for the seat of retiring Senator \uc2 James Inhofe\uc2 : Representative \uc2 Markwayne Mullin\uc2 , who has former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 's endorsement and is heavily favored, and former state House Speaker T.W. Shannon.\par \par \uc2 Will there be any upsets?\par \par \uc2 Capturing voter attention in late August -- the height of vacation season for some families, the start of school for others -- can be a daunting task. Questions about turnout have injected a measure of notable uncertainty into Tuesday's races, with the possibility that some races will end up closer than expected, or that lower-profile challengers could pull off surprising upsets.\par \par \uc2 Perhaps no New York primary is of greater national consequence than the battle for the 17th District, which includes parts of Westchester County and the Hudson Valley. Representative \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2 , who leads the House Democratic campaign arm, appears to be in a strong position headed into Primary Day, but he faces a challenge from State Senator \uc2 Alessandra Biaggi\uc2 , a left-leaning lawmaker who defeated a powerful incumbent in 2018.\par \par \uc2 Nikki Fried, Florida agriculture commissioner, is in a bitter race to be the Democrats' challenger to Gov. Ron DeSantis.; Representative Charlie Crist, who was governor of Florida from 2007 to 2011, is the better-funded candidate. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY SAUL MARTINEZ FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); Pat Ryan, a Democratic congressional candidate for the 19th District in New York, has focused on \uc2  rights. (PHOTOGRAPH BY RICHARD BEAVEN FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); Marc Molinaro, the Republican candidate for the 19th District, has focused on public safety and the cost of living. (POOL  BY MARY ALTAFFER) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220823ei8n0004x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
