\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Abrams Puts Evolution On  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Rights At Center of Campaign\b0\par\par\uc2 By Maya King \par \uc2 1,704 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 August 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Georgia Democrat, a child of Methodist preachers, once identified as an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  foe. Now, she is putting her defense of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights -- and the story of her conversion -- at the center of her campaign for governor.\par \par \uc2 DUBLIN, Ga. -- On the day that a leaked draft opinion suggested the Supreme Court was poised to overturn Roe v. Wade, \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  addressed the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights group Emily's List and preached about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights with ''the zeal of the converted.''\par \par \uc2 Early in her professional career, she opposed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, she volunteered, adding that as a teenager she had criticized a friend who considered having an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''I was wrong,'' she said. ''But I've worked hard to make myself right.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Abrams is among scores of Democrats pushing their defense of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights to the center of their midterm campaigns, hoping anger over the Supreme Court decision overturning Roe v. Wade will energize the Democratic base and push fence-sitting moderates into her corner. But she is the rare Democrat eager to acknowledge that she didn't always support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 The daughter of Methodist ministers living in the Deep South, Ms. Abrams grew up believing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was morally wrong. Conversations with other women, a friend's deliberations over having an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and her own political ambitions led her to rethink her stance, she says.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Abrams's personal approach to talking about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is new for the longtime Georgia politician. She did not emphasize her shift when she first ran for governor in 2018. But today, Ms. Abrams says she uses the story to connect with voters who may personally oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  but, perhaps for the first time, are confronting the reality of new government restrictions. In Georgia, most abortions are now banned after six weeks of pregnancy, based on a law signed by Ms. Abrams's Republican rival, Gov. Brian Kemp.\par \par \uc2 Talking about her own story is ''giving them permission to say that choice should exist,'' Ms. Abrams said in an interview.\par \par \uc2 ''I want people to understand that I know where they're coming from,'' Ms. Abrams said. ''But it also creates the opportunity for people to tell you where they stand, as well.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Abrams's strategy is something of a throwback. For decades, Democrats treaded carefully when talking about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , often assuming voters were disapproving and uncomfortable with the procedure, even if they supported the rights protected by Roe v. Wade. For years, Democratic leaders, starting with Bill Clinton in 1992, declared that their goal was to make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ''safe, legal and rare,'' in an attempt to unite voters with a broad range of views on the issue.\par \par \uc2 For some Democrats, the phrase became emblematic of the party's willingness to cede ground to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights opponents and attach shame to the procedures. And in the wake of the court's decision this summer, some are again criticizing the party for using messaging that lets \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  foes frame the debate.\par \par \uc2 ''I don't think that Democrats as a whole -- as a party -- have talked enough about this issue,'' said Renitta Shannon, a Georgia state representative, who did not specifically criticize Ms. Abrams. ''All this time, we've been relying on the opinion of the court to hold intact people's reproductive freedom, and that is not a good strategy.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Abrams has clear reasons for trying to use the issue to cast as wide a net as possible. After voters in conservative Kansas overwhelmingly voted to guard \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections, Democrats across the country are hoping the issue shifts momentum in their direction during a year when other political forces -- ongoing economic anxiety and President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's weak approval ratings -- are working against them.\par \par \uc2 Nearly 55 percent of voters in Georgia oppose the Supreme Court's ruling reversing Roe v. Wade, according to an Atlanta Journal-Constitution poll released last month.\par \par \uc2 That poll also showed Ms. Abrams trailing Mr. Kemp by five points and, notably, losing ground with Black voters in the state.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Abrams's messaging on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is aimed, in part, at connecting with rural Black voters, who vote reliably Democratic and often share her religious upbringing. Ms. Abrams is also working to win over Asian American and Latino voters in the Atlanta suburbs and moderate or conservative-leaning white voters across the state. All are groups more likely to have reservations about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but also open to a discussion about rights.\par \par \uc2 ''I've been with rural Black communities in a couple of spaces, and I think most would hesitate to raise the decision as a conversation. And I do,'' Ms. Abrams said, adding that her pitch to some leaders is to focus on ''the life and health of women, especially Black women, and I get very little pushback. I think part of it is contextualizing it for the community.''\par \par \uc2 A large part of that context, as she tells it to voters, takes place at Spelman College, the historically Black women's college in Atlanta where as a student in the late 1990s Ms. Abrams talked openly about her views with her peers. She was liberal on issues like women's representation in the media or police accountability, as she was a sophomore when footage of police brutalizing Rodney King was released. But not when it came to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to people who spent time with her in student leadership and social circles.\par \par \uc2 Unlike some students who were combative on the topic, Ms. Abrams was eager to have measured conversations about it, said Donyelle McCray, a friend who supported \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and interned at Planned Parenthood while studying at Spelman.\par \par \uc2 ''We were talking about how complex this was, how hard it was to draw a sort of clean line that would be fair, and how important it was to accept that there are lots of different reasons why women seek abortions,'' said Ms. McCray, who is now an associate professor at Yale Divinity School. ''I remember her acknowledging that it was more complicated than it seemed.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Abrams now describes this discussion as ''really intense'' and integral to her thinking about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- although it did not change her mind.\par \par \uc2 ''That was the first time I had given really careful consideration to what, for me, had been a reflexive belief as opposed to an intentional belief,'' she said, saying that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not yet something she had fully ''thought through and decided.''\par \par \uc2 She recalled her regret for once criticizing a friend who was considering having an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a teenager. She was, Ms. Abrams said in her speech to Emily's List, facing ''the very real challenges of incest and pregnancy, poverty and destitution.''\par \par \uc2 ''I was wrong when I dismissed her question. I was wrong when I assumed I knew enough of her truth to tell her what she could or could not do, what she should or should not seek, what medical advice should be given and what support should be offered,'' Ms. Abrams said.\par \par \uc2 Still, Ms. Abrams considered herself opposed to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights after college and as she went through law school. She only shifted her views, she said, when she began to think about running for office. At 30, as a deputy city attorney, she interviewed for a position on the board of directors for the Georgia WIN List, a political action committee that backs women candidates who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. When she applied, Ms. Abrams disclosed that she was unsure of what her views of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  were, she said. She had reservations about calling herself ''pro-choice,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Melita Easters, founder and executive director of the Georgia WIN List, said she remembered Ms. Abrams's uncertainty, saying ''The Stacey who served on the Georgia WIN List board is not the same Stacey who has evolved into someone who commands the presence of a room and speaks with such great eloquence.''\par \par \uc2 ''She was the more thoughtful, introverted side of herself at that time period,'' Ms. Abrams said.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Easters and the organization's co-founder, Mary Long, introduced Ms. Abrams to several women affiliated with the organization in an effort to connect her with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters in Georgia politics. Ms. Abrams said she wrote an essay to help herself reason through it: While \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  might not be a procedure she wanted for herself, she decided, it was one she was willing to fight to protect access to.\par \par \uc2 ''It was when I got ready to stand for office that I had to really make myself confront what I meant by that,'' Ms. Abrams said. ''And what I meant was that I was no longer in the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  camp.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Abrams won her seat in the Georgia House in 2006 as an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporter. In 2011, as the Democratic minority leader in the House, she rallied all the chamber's Democrats to kill a Republican-proposed bill that would have attempted to ban abortions in the state in nearly all circumstances.\par \par \uc2 Today, Ms. Abrams talks about the issue as a fight to ''to control our bodies,'' and often notes that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions have a disproportionate impact on Black women. In Georgia, Black women make up 65 percent of people who get abortions despite being one-third of the population.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this summer, when Ms. Abrams talked to a crowd of supporters, most of them Black, in the rural town of Dublin, Ga., she evoked the biblical imagery and language she was surrounded by as a child.\par \par \uc2 ''I cannot strike down another person's rights simply because I don't agree. My shield is to say that you have the right to make your own decisions,'' she said. ''We don't all have to make the same choices but we have to have the right to make those choices.''\par \par \uc2 Stacey Abrams, who is running for Georgia governor, is a rare Democrat to acknowledge she did not always support \uc2  rights.; Ms. Abrams spoke at an event for the \uc2 -rights group NARAL in July. Nearly 55 percent of voters in Georgia oppose the Supreme Court's ruling overturning Roe v. Wade. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY NICOLE CRAINE FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220821ei8l00067\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
