\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Sensing Opportunity, Democrats Spotlight  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  in Their Ads\b0\par\par\uc2 By Shane Goldmacher and Katie Glueck \par \uc2 1,796 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 August 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade, Democrats have spent nearly eight times as much on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related ads as Republicans have.\par \par \uc2 In Michigan, Democrats took aim at the Republican nominee for governor almost immediately after the primary with a television ad highlighting her opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , without exceptions for rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 In Georgia, Democrats recently attacked the Republican governor in another television ad, with women speaking fearfully about the specter of being investigated and ''criminalized.''\par \par \uc2 And in Arizona, the Republican nominees for both Senate and governor were confronted almost instantly after their primaries with different ads calling them ''dangerous'' for their anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions.\par \par \uc2 All across America, Democrats are using \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a powerful cudgel in their 2022 television campaigns, paying for an onslaught of ads in House, Senate and governor's races that show how swiftly \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  politics have shifted since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade in late June.\par \par \uc2 With national protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights suddenly gone and bans going into effect in many states, senior White House officials and top Democratic strategists believe the issue has radically reshaped the 2022 landscape in their favor. They say it has not only reawakened the party's progressive base, but also provided a wedge issue that could wrest away independent voters and even some Republican women who believe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents have overreached.\par \par \uc2 In the fallout of the ruling, Democrats see the potential to upend the typical dynamic of midterm elections in which voters punish the party in power. In this case, although Democrats control the White House and both chambers of Congress, it is one of their top policy priorities -- access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- that has been most visibly stripped away.\par \par \uc2 ''Rarely has an issue been handed on a silver platter to Democrats that is so clear-cut,'' said Anna Greenberg, a Democratic pollster working with multiple 2022 campaigns. ''It took an election that was going to be mostly about inflation and immigration and made it also about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 In the roughly 50 days since the Supreme Court's ruling, Democrats have flooded the airwaves in many of the nation's most closely watched contests, spending nearly eight times as much as Republicans have on ads talking about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- $31.9 million compared with $4.2 million, according to data from AdImpact, a media tracking firm. And in the closest Senate and governor's contests, Republicans have spent virtually nothing countering the Democratic offensive.\par \par \uc2 By contrast, in the last midterms four years ago, Democrats spent less than $1 million on ads that mentioned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related issues in the same time period.\par \par \uc2 The 2022 advertising figures do not include money spent on the recent anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights referendum in Kansas. The landslide defeat of that measure, particularly in a traditionally conservative state, has only further emboldened Democratic strategists and candidates.\par \par \uc2 There are risks to focusing so heavily on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a moment when Americans are also expressing intense anxiety over the economy. But Democrats are plowing ahead, particularly in key Senate races.\par \par \uc2 They have spent more than $2 million on ads targeting Senator Ron Johnson, Republican of Wisconsin, for his position on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; $1.6 million on ads against Mehmet Oz, the Republican candidate for Senate in Pennsylvania; and $1.8 million on \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2 , the Republican Senate nominee in Nevada who recently wrote an op-ed defending his stance on the issue.\par \par \uc2 More \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ads have aired in the Senate races in North Carolina, New Hampshire, Arizona and Washington -- and even in Connecticut and Maryland, two states with secure Democratic incumbents.\par \par \uc2 ''I clearly believe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is going to matter because I think it cuts across demographics and it really does get into many voters, including Trump voters and independents, and their concept of personal freedom,'' said J.B. Poersch, the president of Senate Majority PAC, a Democratic super political action committee that has already funded \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  commercials in multiple states.\par \par \uc2 But Republicans say Democrats risk ignoring the economic concerns that polls have shown are paramount.\par \par \uc2 ''They've got a lot of bad news, and they think that's the only good news they've got,'' said former Representative \uc2 Steve Stivers\uc2  of Ohio, who led the House Republican campaign arm during the 2018 midterm elections. ''If they want to be a single-issue party, that's on them.''\par \par \uc2 If Democrats do focus overwhelmingly on the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the expense of other matters, Mr. Stivers suggested, ''they'll get smoked on the economy, where they're already losing ground.''\par \par \uc2 For months, Democrats have been bracing for a Republican wave this fall, prompted by President Biden's diminished popularity, high gas prices and inflation, and they still face a difficult political environment. But Mr. Biden is expected to sign a sweeping legislative package soon that addresses climate change and prescription drug prices. In addition, gas prices are declining, and there are at least some tentative signs that inflation may be slowing.\par \par \uc2 Those developments, combined with the backlash to the Supreme Court's ruling on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , have raised Democrats' hopes of maintaining power after November. Certainly, they plan to advertise their legislative achievements while making other attacks on Republicans, whom they argue are a threat to democracy.\par \par \uc2 For now, new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -focused Democratic advertisements are popping up seemingly daily, including in Alaska, Iowa and Virginia.\par \par \uc2 Some \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ads use the specific words and positions of Republican candidates against them. Some are narrated by women speaking in deeply raw and personal terms. Some use Republicans' unyielding stances on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cast them more broadly as extremists.\par \par \uc2 And some, like one early ad hitting \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2 , the Republican nominee for governor in Pennsylvania, do all three. ''\uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2  scares me,'' a woman declares at the beginning of the spot.\par \par \uc2 One particularly emotional spot came from \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , the Democratic nominee for governor of Georgia, who used a montage of women to target Gov. \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 's stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''He supports a total ban,'' one woman says in the ad. ''Even if I'm raped,'' another says. More women continue, one after another: ''A victim of incest. Forced pregnancy. Criminalized women. Women with jail time.''\par \par \uc2 Democrats aim to connect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  messaging to the broader argument that hard-line Republicans are seeking to strip away fundamental freedoms.\par \par \uc2 ''The arguments Democrats are using in those ads don't stay contained to the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  space,'' said Jennifer Palmieri, the former White House communications director under President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  and a longtime party strategist. ''You're telling them something about their temperament, their judgment and their values.''\par \par \uc2 In at least five states, Democrats have used the phrase ''too extreme'' to call out Republicans, using \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the example.\par \par \uc2 Often, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the Democrats' opening gambit at the start of general election ad campaigns. Just this month, ads have targeted Tudor Dixon in the governor's race in Michigan and Kari Lake in the governor's race in Arizona. And a day after Minnesota's primary for governor, Democrats began airing an ad calling Scott Jensen, the Republican nominee, ''too extreme'' on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The next major test of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 's political power comes in a special election on Aug. 23 in New York.\par \par \uc2 County Executive Pat Ryan in Ulster County, N.Y., the Democratic candidate in that race, has made \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  the focus of his campaign, even in a state where access remains protected. In a new ad this week, Mr. Ryan featured a carousel of national Republicans arguing that the party would pursue a nationwide ban.\par \par \uc2 A Democratic super PAC is spending $500,000 to promote Mr. Ryan, a veteran, with an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  message. ''He sure didn't fight for our freedom abroad to see it taken away from women here at home,'' the narrator says.\par \par \uc2 The election is being closely monitored as a barometer of the issue's power. Democrats have overperformed -- even in defeat -- in two other special elections since Roe v. Wade was overturned, in Minnesota and Nebraska.\par \par \uc2 Meredith Kelly, a Democratic strategist and ad maker, said one factor that made \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ''extremely powerful'' was the idea that ''Republicans are taking something away.''\par \par \uc2 Research has shown that the notion of losing rights can be galvanizing for voters, which Ms. Kelly saw firsthand in 2018 when she guided the messaging for the House Democratic campaign arm. The party took over the House in part by bludgeoning Republicans for their repeated efforts to repeal the Affordable Care Act.\par \par \uc2 ''When you take something away from voters, especially something as cherished and crucial as health care, which is what this is, that is a really politically perilous decision,'' she said of Republicans' approach to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Some Republicans are trying to backpedal or soften their stances.\par \par \uc2 In Arizona, ads are hammering \uc2 Blake Masters\uc2 , the Republican Senate candidate, for calling \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ''demonic,'' talking about punishing doctors who perform the procedure and opposing exceptions for rape and incest during the primary. In a post-primary interview with The Arizona Republic, Mr. Masters called the state's 15-week ban ''a reasonable solution'' and expressed his desire to ''reflect the will of Arizonans.''\par \par \uc2 On the airwaves, though, few Republicans have had an answer. One notable exception has come in the New Mexico governor's race; Mark Ronchetti, the Republican nominee to take on Gov. \uc2 Michelle Lujan Grisham\uc2 , has been under fire over his stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''I'm personally pro-life, but I believe we can all come together on a policy that reflects our shared values,'' Mr. Ronchetti said in a campaign spot that detailed his position on the issue.\par \par \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2 , the Pennsylvania attorney general and Democratic nominee for governor, opened his first ad of the general election by hitting Mr. Mastriano on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In an interview, Mr. Shapiro said voters were especially attuned to the issue because the state's Republican-led Legislature had passed strict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  limits that he would veto and that Mr. Mastriano would sign.\par \par \uc2 ''There is an intensity around this,'' he said. ''They know the next governor of Pennsylvania is going to decide this.''\par \par \uc2 The evening before, Mr. Shapiro said, he met a Republican woman in the Lehigh Valley who told him that she was voting for him -- her first Democratic ballot -- because of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''It has brought people into our campaign and brought people off the sidelines to get engaged unlike any other issue,'' Mr. Shapiro said of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 's influence after the Supreme Court's ruling. ''We just saw an explosion.''\par \par \uc2 Recent ads produced by Democrats highlight \uc2  as a powerful campaign issue. (PHOTOGRAPH BY Clockwise from top left, Kelly for AZ Senate; Put Michigan First; Shapiro for PA Governor; Gara for AK Governor FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220815ei8f00046\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
