\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Idaho Supreme Court Rules Near-Total Ban On  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Can Begin\b0\par\par\uc2 By Isabella Grull\u243\'00\'F3n Paz \par \uc2 631 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 August 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 30\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 In a late Friday ruling, the state's highest court said that a ban could begin at the end of the month while legal challenges are reviewed.\par \par \uc2 Idaho's near-total ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  can go into effect at the end of August while legal challenges to the restrictions are reviewed, the Idaho Supreme Court said in a ruling late Friday.\par \par \uc2 As a result of the decision, the ban is scheduled to begin on Aug. 25.\par \par \uc2 The Court issued the decision based on three lawsuits filed by a Planned Parenthood chapter and a local doctor this year to block three Idaho laws that were to take effect if the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  were to overturn Roe v. Wade, which it did in June. The petitioners had asked the court not enforce the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions until the suits were settled.\par \par \uc2 The first lawsuit was aimed at halting a law that would make it a felony to perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , though it allows doctors to cite rape, incest or an effort to save the life of a pregnant woman as a defense in a trial. Another suit sought to curb a law that criminalizes abortions after six weeks of a pregnancy. The chapter had also filed a suit against a law that allows relatives of a fetus or embryo to sue the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider, and establishes a reward of at least $20,000, plus legal fees, within four years of an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Though other states have banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at conception and instituted harsh penalties for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers, the federal government says that the Idaho law runs afoul of federal guidelines in providing health care when a pregnant woman's health is at risk. The Department of Justice took the rare step of filing its own suit against the state in federal court and has asked for the law to be put on hold. That lawsuit, the first federal suit filed to try to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, is scheduled to be heard on Aug. 22.\par \par \uc2 Idaho's highest court merged the three state lawsuits into one. The petitioners had initially argued that the laws were unconstitutional. When Roe was overturned, they argued that the laws violated the Idaho Constitution and the Idaho Human Rights Act. But the court ruled that the laws could take effect since the petitioners could not show that enforcing them would cause ''irreparable harm.'' The judges added that the petitioners did not meet the standard of ''substantial likelihood of success'' in overturning the laws.\par \par \uc2 ''What petitioners are asking this Court to ultimately do is to declare a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  under the Idaho Constitution when -- on its face -- there is none,'' wrote Justice Robin Brody for the majority, along with Chief Justice Richard Bevan and Justice Gregory Moeller. ''In fact, before Roe announced a federal constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 1973, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been a longstanding criminal offense in Idaho.''\par \par \uc2 Two dissenting justices -- Justices John Stegner and Colleen Zahn -- said that the laws should not be enforced until there was legal clarity.\par \par \uc2 ''It's been a little over a month since the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  disregarded 50 years of precedent and threw patients across the country into a world of chaos, fear and confusion,'' Rebecca Gibron, the acting chief executive of the Planned Parenthood chapter that had filed the suit, said in a statement. ''The Idaho state legislature has made it abundantly clear that this is the future they want for their constituents, and today, the court allowed their vision to become a reality.''\par \par \uc2 Anti-\uc2  activists in Boise, Idaho, after the overturning of Roe v. Wade in late June. (PHOTOGRAPH BY SARAH A. MILLER/IDAHO STATESMAN, VIA ASSOCIATED PRESS) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220814ei8e0005k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
