\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Democrats Try to Rally Around Freedom, Too\b0\par\par\uc2 By Katie Glueck \par \uc2 1,015 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 August 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 15\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Democrats at every level of the party are increasingly seeking to reclaim language about freedom and personal liberty from Republicans, as part of their midterms messaging.\par \par \uc2 For much of the midterm campaign, Democrats have grappled with how to define their message, weighing slogans like ''Democrats deliver'' and ''Build back better,'' and issuing warnings against ''ultra-MAGA'' Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Now, a coalition of progressive organizations has settled on what its leaders hope will be a unified pitch from the left. This November, they plan to argue, Americans must vote to protect the fundamental freedoms that ''Trump Republicans'' are trying to take away.\par \par \uc2 That pitch is the product of a monthslong midterms messaging project called the ''Protect Our Freedoms'' initiative, fueled by polling and ad testing.\par \par \uc2 The move is the latest evidence that Democrats at every level of the party and of varying ideological stripes -- including President Biden, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights activists in Kansas and, now, a constellation of left-leaning groups -- are increasingly seeking to reclaim language about freedom and personal liberty from Republicans. It is a dynamic that grew out of the overturning of Roe v. Wade in June, and one that is intensifying as more states navigate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans while Republicans nominate election deniers for high office.\par \par \uc2 The messaging project is a sprawling effort from progressive groups, activists and strategists aimed at getting Democrats on the same page, as they seek to crystallize the choice between the two parties and emphasize the consequential nature of the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 ''Freedom is a powerful frame for this election, to make clear what the stakes are,'' said Jenifer Fernandez Ancona, an architect of the messaging project as well as a co-founder and vice president of Way to Win, a collective of left-leaning Democratic donors and political strategists.\par \par \uc2 What is most striking about the initiative is not the initial size of the investment -- there is a $5 million national paid-media component associated with the campaign, a relatively modest sum -- but the fact that left-wing organizations are now embracing language that has been more closely associated with small-government-minded conservatives.\par \par \uc2 ''For far too long, we've witnessed how the right wing has masterfully sort of owned and captured the language of freedom,'' said Maurice Mitchell, the national director of the \uc2 Working Families Party\uc2 , one of the organizations involved in the ''Protect Our Freedoms'' effort.\par \par \uc2 Such language has never been limited to the right: former President \uc2 Franklin D. Roosevelt\uc2  famously promoted the ''Four Freedoms,'' for instance, and a major campaign for marriage equality was framed as Freedom to Marry. But Republicans have long cast their party as the bastion of freedom -- whether as the defender of free markets or more recently, in opposition to coronavirus-related mandates.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, Emma Vaughn, a spokeswoman for the \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2 , highlighted the effort to paint Democrats as anti-freedom over pandemic measures, calling them the party of ''shuttered businesses, school lockdowns, masks on toddlers and forced vaccines.''\par \par \uc2 Ads that draw from the ''Protect Our Freedoms'' messaging argue that core American values -- such as free elections in which the will of the people is upheld, or freedom for individuals to make decisions for their families -- are now uniquely jeopardized. Just last week, a group of academics issued stark warnings to Mr. Biden about the state of democracy, \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2  reported.\par \par \uc2 ''We've seen what happens when Trump Republicans have claimed to be for freedom, only to take it away and impose their will,'' says an ad paid for by Way to Win Action Fund, as images from the Jan. 6, 2021, attack on the U.S. Capitol flash across the screen. ''This November, remember: You're a part of the fight for freedom.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Fernandez Ancona said the national ad campaign offers ''the example of the message that we're trying to put forward,'' one that leaders of the initiative hope will be echoed in grass-roots efforts and advertising from individual organizations.\par \par \uc2 MoveOn Political Action is planning a television and digital ad buy for later this month, aimed at statewide races in Arizona and Pennsylvania, that will incorporate ''Protect Our Freedoms'' messaging, said Rahna Epting, the executive director of MoveOn. And officials with several organizations said the messaging lessons will mold how they engage voters at their doors or on the phone.\par \par \uc2 Ten organizations were involved in the ''Protect Our Freedoms'' messaging initiative, including Indivisible and Future Forward.\par \par \uc2 Within the broader Democratic ecosystem, candidates and party institutions are still pursuing a broad range of messaging tactics.\par \par \uc2 But in an interview last week after Kansas voters defeated an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  measure, Representative \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2 , the chair of the House Democratic campaign arm, also reached for the ''freedom'' language as he described the choice between the two political parties.\par \par \uc2 ''The MAGA movement will take away your rights, your benefits, your freedoms, and you have to vote Democratic if you care about those things,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Research assembled by the ''Protect Our Freedoms'' campaign helps explain why Democrats see such messaging as potent: A data point cited in a campaign presentation said that 42 percent of individuals, when asked in a survey to identify the values that mattered most to them as Americans, picked ''freedom and liberty,'' far outpacing other options like equality and and patriotism.\par \par \uc2 The presentation also said that the overturning of Roe v. Wade had opened a ''persuasion window,'' putting more voters into play. Anat Shenker-Osorio, a progressive consultant involved in the messaging project, said there was a powerful opening to tie ''actions on Roe and on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to this broader framework of taking away our freedoms.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Fernandez Ancona said that ''the idea of 'freedom' really pops.''\par \par \uc2 ''People need to understand that they're going to lose things if this Republican Party wins,'' she added.\par \par \uc2  rights activists in Kansas, where voters recently defeated an anti-\uc2  measure, are among the groups touting liberty as a value. (PHOTOGRAPH BY Arin Yoon for The New York Times FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220812ei8c0004b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
