\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Women Choose  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  by Pill As Barriers Rise\b0\par\par\uc2 By Roni Caryn Rabin \par \uc2 1,936 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 August 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 In states that have banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , some women with unwanted pregnancies are pursuing an unconventional workaround: They are ''self-managing'' their abortions, seeking out the necessary know-how online and obtaining the medications without the supervision of a clinic or a doctor.\par \par \uc2 At first glance, the practice may recall the days before Roe v. Wade, when women too often were forced to take risky measures to end an unwanted pregnancy. But the advent of medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- accomplished with drugs, rather than in-office procedures -- has transformed reproductive care, posing a significant challenge to anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legislation.\par \par \uc2 Even before the Supreme Court's decision to overturn Roe v. Wade, medication abortions accounted for more than half of abortions in the United States. Federal regulators made access to the pills even easier during the pandemic by dropping the requirement for an in-person visit and allowing the drugs to be mailed to patients after a virtual appointment.\par \par \uc2 But many states never allowed telehealth \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and new laws prohibiting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  apply to all forms of the procedure, including medications. So women in increasingly restrictive parts of the country are procuring the pills any way they can, often online, despite state prohibitions.\par \par \uc2 There are no reliable estimates of the number of women who undertake their own medication abortions, according to the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , which researches and supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.\par \par \uc2 With the overturning of Roe v. Wade, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is now banned in at least 10 states, according to a database maintained by \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 . Voters in Kansas on Tuesday rejected a ballot measure that would have removed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights protections from the state constitution.\par \par \uc2 Limits of one sort or another are nonetheless expected in at least half of U.S. states, and so both sides of the divide are bracing for an increase in self-managed abortions.\par \par \uc2 Critics of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in any form insist that medication abortions are riskier than claimed, and even more so without medical supervision. The procedure should not be undertaken beyond 10 weeks gestation, they note, or performed without a doctor's visit, because dating a pregnancy accurately is not always possible.\par \par \uc2 Other medical complications can be missed, they say -- including ectopic pregnancy, in which the fertilized egg implants outside the uterus.\par \par \uc2 Claims that medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is safe ''are based on flawed and incomplete data, which prioritize convenience and cost over the health and safety of patients,'' said Dr. Christina Francis, chair of the American Association of Pro-Life Obstetricians and Gynecologists, which opposes all abortions except to prevent permanent harm or death to the mother.\par \par \uc2 Physicians who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  tell a different story: There is plentiful evidence that medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is safe, and women already carry out the procedure almost entirely alone at home, even if they do see a doctor to obtain the drugs. Self-management is not so different, supporters argue.\par \par \uc2 ''It's quite safe and effective based on studies we've done, national data provided by the states and the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , and the experience of other countries,'' said Dr. Beverly Winikoff, the founder of Gynuity Health Projects, who performed much of the research on medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that led to its approval in the United States more than 20 years ago.\par \par \uc2 The procedure typically involves taking two drugs: mifepristone, which stops the pregnancy by blocking a hormone called progesterone, followed a day or two later by misoprostol, which causes the uterus to contract.\par \par \uc2 More than half a million women had medication abortions in 2020 in the United States, and fewer than half of 1 percent experience serious complications, studies show. Medical interventions like hospitalizations or blood transfusions were needed by fewer than 0.4 percent of patients, according to a 2013 review of dozens of studies involving tens of thousands of patients.\par \par \uc2 A 2018 review by the \uc2 National Academies of Sciences, Engineering and Medicine\uc2  found that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication ended pregnancies 96.7 percent of the time in gestations of up to nine weeks. The \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  endorses self-managed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and says it can be used up to 12 weeks gestation.\par \par \uc2 Medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ''is noninvasive, doesn't cause sepsis and doesn't cause ruptures of internal organs,'' like the illegal abortions of the pre-Roe era, Dr. Winikoff said.\par \par \uc2 ''It doesn't mean people can't have excessive bleeding and need to get care occasionally, but those are not the dire circumstances of people from 50 years ago,'' she added.\par \par \uc2 The drugs are regulated by the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2 , however, and are intended to be taken under a doctor's supervision. The agency discourages internet purchases of mifepristone because patients will be ''bypassing important safeguards,'' officials said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 But the F.D.A. does not advise against online purchases of misoprostol (brand name Cytotec), which is used to treat a number of medical conditions. Misoprostol can terminate pregnancies by itself, recent studies have shown.\par \par \uc2 While no treatment is 100 percent safe, taking the pills ''on your own at home does not affect your risk of complications,'' said Dr. Carolyn Westhoff, an obstetrician gynecologist and professor at \uc2 Columbia University\uc2  and the editor in chief of the journal Contraception.\par \par \uc2 But self-management also means a woman does not have a familiar health care professional nearby to call in case of an emergency or complications. Dr. Westhoff and other experts fear that women performing their own abortions may be reluctant to seek medical help in states that have criminalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Cassie, 20, who uses the pronouns they and them and asked that only a first name be used because they reside in Texas, where most abortions are banned after about six weeks of pregnancy, managed their own \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in January.\par \par \uc2 Cassie, who already had a child and was struggling financially, filled out an online request form for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills from Aid Access, which is based in Europe. The drugs took longer to arrive than expected, and when they did, Cassie's pregnancy was already 12 weeks along.\par \par \uc2 ''I just took them and prayed for the best,'' Cassie said. They experienced heavy bleeding, nausea and ''the worst cramps I've had in my entire life.''\par \par \uc2 ''I was crying, curled up in a ball of pain in the middle of my bed,'' they said.\par \par \uc2 When the bleeding did not subside, Cassie's partner drove them to the hospital, where the remaining tissue was removed.\par \par \uc2 ''That was its own horrifying experience of praying that they wouldn't know or suspect I'd caused it myself,'' Cassie said.\par \par \uc2 Both the know-how and the tools to perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  are increasingly easy to access.\par \par \uc2 Women who live in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal can turn to U.S.-based telehealth providers like \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Demand and Hey Jane, which offer detailed information to women seeking abortions and provide pills by mail after a video visit in states where these services are legal.\par \par \uc2 MYA Network provides physicians who answer questions about self-managed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Pill Info offers tips on keeping online research private.\par \par \uc2 For women in states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, Plan C offers a number of workarounds, including a list of online pharmacies selling \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drugs that the organization has tested and tutorials on setting up mail forwarding in another state to receive the drugs.\par \par \uc2 The site also refers people to Aid Access, which screens women online and orders \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills from overseas pharmacies that are sent in envelopes without return addresses, even to states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is illegal. The group charges $150 or less, depending on income.\par \par \uc2 Hannah, a 26-year-old in Oklahoma, said she managed her own \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  with pills from Aid Access late last year, when local clinics, overwhelmed with patients from Texas, could not accommodate her.\par \par \uc2 Hannah, who asked to not be identified because \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is now banned in her state, said she suffered from depression at times before she became pregnant, but had plummeted to a new low and was suicidal.\par \par \uc2 ''I couldn't afford a pregnancy and was not well enough, physically or mentally, to carry a pregnancy,'' she said. Her self-managed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was ''no worse than a normal period for me.''\par \par \uc2 A medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  cannot be distinguished from a miscarriage, and traces of the pills cannot be discovered if they are taken orally, said Dr. Rebecca Gomperts, a Dutch physician who founded Aid Access.\par \par \uc2 If a woman needs care after taking the pills, ''we always tell people to say they had a miscarriage,'' she said. ''It's exactly the same symptoms, and the treatment is exactly the same.''\par \par \uc2 A study of thousands of women in the United States who received \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills from a provider without an in-person visit during the pandemic found that the practice was safe.\par \par \uc2 Complications are the rare exception. Another recent study looked at self-managed abortions in Nigeria, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is banned except to save the life of the mother, and in Argentina (which legalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  up to 14 weeks in late 2020).\par \par \uc2 Twenty percent of the nearly 1,000 women who participated in the study sought care at hospitals after the procedure, but most only wanted to confirm the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was complete. About 4 percent reported ongoing pain, fever or bleeding. Seventeen required procedures to complete the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , 12 stayed in the hospital overnight, and six needed blood transfusions, according to the study, which was published in The Lancet Global Health in late 2021.\par \par \uc2 The surprise finding was that while some of the women took the mifepristone-misoprostol combination, the success rate for those taking misoprostol alone -- a widely used drug that can be purchased in countries like Mexico without a prescription and is fairly inexpensive -- was higher than that of the two-drug combination.\par \par \uc2 Most state laws that restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  make performing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  a crime for doctors, not patients. Only three states -- South Carolina, Oklahoma and Nevada -- have laws that explicitly make it a crime to end one's own pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Other states, however, have wielded child endangerment statutes or other laws against women suspected of terminating their pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 In Indiana, Purvi Patel was sentenced to 20 years in prison in 2015 for inducing a self-managed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; her conviction was overturned in 2016. In Texas, murder charges were brought against Lizelle Herrera earlier this year in relation to a self-managed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but prosecutors said they would not pursue the case.\par \par \uc2 At least 11 states have laws with broad personhood language that applies to fetuses, said Dana Sussman, deputy executive director of National Advocates for Pregnant Women. At least six -- Kentucky, Louisiana, Ohio, South Dakota, Texas and Wyoming -- define a fetus as a person throughout the criminal code, making it easier to prosecute women who terminate their own pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 Both the \uc2 American Medical Association\uc2  and the \uc2 American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists\uc2 , which support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as an essential component of health care, oppose criminalizing self-managed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as they say doing so will deter women from seeking medical attention.\par \par \uc2 At the moment, health care providers are not legally required by any state to report patients they suspect of self-managing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to If/When/How, an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights advocacy group. But laws are in flux.\par \par \uc2 ''We're operating in an area of complete uncertainty,'' Ms. Sussman said.\par \par \uc2 Hannah, a woman in Oklahoma, self-managed her own \uc2  with medication last year. (PHOTOGRAPH BY MELISSA LUKENBAUGH FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); \uc2  medications at a clinic in Oklahoma last year. In states where \uc2  is now banned, women are obtaining pills online. (PHOTOGRAPH BY EVELYN HOCKSTEIN/REUTERS) (A13) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220808ei880003r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
