\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Catholic Faith Makes  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Tricky for Biden\b0\par\par\uc2 By Michael D. Shear \par \uc2 2,219 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 August 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A practicing Catholic, President Biden has long sought a middle ground on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But activists see the decision to overturn Roe v. Wade as a sign that Democrats have tiptoed too carefully around the issue.\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- The Supreme Court's decision to end the constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the United States after nearly 50 years has set in motion a generational struggle over Republican efforts to ban the procedure in states across the country.\par \par \uc2 But inside the West Wing, President Biden has made it clear that he is uncomfortable even using the word \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to current and former advisers. In speeches and public statements, he prefers to use the word sparingly, focusing instead on broader phrases, like ''\b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2 '' and ''the right to choose,'' that might resonate more widely with the public.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden, a practicing Catholic who has drawn on his faith to shape his political identity, is now being called on to lead a fight he spent decades sidestepping -- and many \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates worry that he may not be the right messenger for the moment.\par \par \uc2 Once an outright critic of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and later a committed but quiet defender of them, Mr. Biden has a history that gives activists pause.\par \par \uc2 ''This is not necessarily the guy that I am sure most activists wanted in the seat when this happened,'' said Jamie L. Manson, the president of \uc2 Catholics for Choice\uc2 , referring to the court's decision overturning Roe v. Wade. ''It's unfortunate because he has so much power and we need him to really get out of his comfort zone.''\par \par \uc2 For five decades, Mr. Biden has talked openly about the power of his religion, portraying himself as an advocate for workers and a warrior for social justice. His faith also had guided him toward what he once called a ''middle of the road'' approach to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- essentially, not voting to limit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but not voting to fund it either.\par \par \uc2 And like other Democrats of his generation, Mr. Biden used the existence of Roe v. Wade's protections to avoid pushing for legislation that might have enshrined the ruling in federal law.\par \par \uc2 Now, a growing chorus of women's groups, progressive Democrats and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights activists see the decision to overturn Roe as an indictment of that middle-ground approach, saying Democrats like Mr. Biden have tiptoed too carefully around the issue for years.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court's decision, they say, must be met with an equally fierce legal, political and rhetorical response. And after a decisive vote this past week to defend \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in deeply conservative Kansas, many Democrats see this as the moment to run more assertively on the issue.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden's advisers say that his views on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  have changed over time and that he is deeply committed to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Laphonza Butler, the president of Emily's List, a group that helps elect Democratic women who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, said she was satisfied that Mr. Biden and his team were ''using every tool at their disposal'' to fight for the cause.\par \par \uc2 But the president's history on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- informed by his religion and the Democratic Party's years of careful political calculations -- has left him struggling to live up to the expectations of those in his party who want a new strategy and a new energy.\par \par \uc2 ''Yes, there are limits to executive branch power, there are limits to what the president can do,'' said Andrea Miller, the president of the National Institute for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Health\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ''But this just feels like you've got to push the boundaries right now. This is a time to pull out all the stops. This is a time to take risks.''\par \par \uc2 In 2007, Mr. Biden wrote in his memoir ''Promises to Keep'' that his position on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  had ''earned me the distrust of some women's groups.'' In the book, he recounted a 1973 conversation with a veteran senator who said his cautious approach was a ''tough'' one.\par \par \uc2 '''Yeah, everybody will be upset with me,' I told him, 'except me. But I'm intellectually and morally comfortable with my position,''' Mr. Biden wrote in the book.\par \par \uc2 Now, he finds himself championing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. In June, just days after the court's ruling, he appeared miffed when a reporter noted that some activists did not believe he was the right person to lead the fight against Republican efforts to ban the procedure.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm the only president they got,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Shaped by Faith\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden has often said that his views on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- and the proper role for government to play in regulating it -- are the result of his faith. In 1982, when he voted in favor of a constitutional amendment pushed by Republicans to allow individual states to overturn Roe v. Wade, he said: ''I'm probably a victim, or a product, however you want to phrase it, of my background.''\par \par \uc2 The Catholic Church considers human life to begin at conception and says that ''the intentional killing of a human being living in the womb'' is always immoral. Church teachings generally allow for ''indirect'' abortions when a medical procedure needed for another lifesaving reason results in the death of a fetus. But many Catholics disagree with the church's official position. In a \uc2 Pew Research Center\uc2  survey released last month, 60 percent of Catholics in the United States said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in all or most cases.\par \par \uc2 Other Democratic politicians have faced difficult moments navigating their stands on the issue. The leaders of the American Catholic Church have publicly rebuked Catholic politicians like John F. Kerry, the former Massachusetts senator, and Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  for their stances.\par \par \uc2 By his own admission, Mr. Biden is a deeply religious person who rarely misses a chance to attend Mass.\par \par \uc2 Last year in St. Ives, a seaside town in Cornwall on the southern tip of England, Mr. Biden, who was attending the annual Group of 7 meeting with world leaders, slipped into the back pews of the Sacred Heart and St. Ia Church for Mass with about 50 other parishioners. The Rev. Philip Dyson had been given a heads-up just minutes before the arrival of the president and his wife.\par \par \uc2 ''I did find him gracious and humble and a gentleman,'' Father Dyson said, recalling the brief conversation after the Mass. The priest would not talk about whether he offered communion to the president during the service. Some Roman Catholic bishops believe politicians who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be denied communion.\par \par \uc2 ''It's controversial, and it's between him and the Lord,'' Father Dyson said.\par \par \uc2 John Carr, the director of the Initiative on Catholic Social Thought and Public Life at \uc2 Georgetown University\uc2 , said that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been the one part of Mr. Biden's faith that had been a source of conflict for the president and his allies over the years.\par \par \uc2 ''He is a product of Catholic social teaching and Democratic orthodoxy,'' said Mr. Carr, who has participated in several small-group discussions with Mr. Biden about religion and politics. ''When the two go together, he's really comfortable with the way he talks, the way he acts. Where he is the least at home is where the two conflict.''\par \par \uc2 Shifting on \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 Allies of the president note that since the Supreme Court ruling, Mr. Biden has issued two executive orders aimed at protecting the right to travel for health care and the right to access medications. This past week, the Justice Department filed a lawsuit in Idaho, accusing the state of illegally restricting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the procedure is needed to stabilize a woman's health.\par \par \uc2 ''The president's faith is not the problem that we have,'' said Representative Katherine M. Clark, Democrat of Massachusetts and the assistant speaker. ''The problem is an extremist G.O.P. that says, 'We don't respect your faith, your medical history, your circumstance.'''\par \par \uc2 But for most of his career, Mr. Biden has been viewed with suspicion by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates because of his history on the issue.\par \par \uc2 In 1984, Mr. Biden voted to praise the ''Mexico City Policy,'' a decision by the Reagan administration to prevent funding of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services abroad. It was a position that would be anathema for a Democratic president today. Over the years since, Republican presidents have routinely reinstated the policy, and Democrats have eliminated it. Mr. Biden rescinded it eight days after taking office.\par \par \uc2 For years, Mr. Biden also declined to join other Democrats in opposing the Hyde Amendment, a federal ban on funding for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It was not until 2019 that he reversed himself. Facing intense backlash from within his party, he said he could ''no longer support an amendment'' that makes it harder for low-income women to get access to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Although he followed through by submitting budgets without Hyde's restrictive language, lawmakers added it back in.\par \par \uc2 As vice president, Mr. Biden fought to exempt Catholic institutions from the Affordable Care Act requirement to provide coverage for contraception. The provision was fiercely opposed by American Catholic bishops, and Mr. Biden tried to make the bishops' case.\par \par \uc2 He lost in the end, though the contraception mandate was later struck down by the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 Kathleen Sebelius\uc2 , who served as secretary of health and human services under President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , said that Mr. Biden wanted to ''just avoid a battle with the church.''\par \par \uc2 ''I think that's sort of where he started the conversation,'' she said. But she recalled that Mr. Biden eventually acknowledged the impact that denying contraceptive coverage would have for people who worked at Catholic institutions.\par \par \uc2 ''He started in one place, and then gradually moved to a very different place,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 On other issues where Democratic Party positions clashed with Catholic teaching, like support for same-sex marriage, Mr. Biden was quicker to change his position, said Mr. Carr, noting what he called the president's ''passion and eloquence'' on L.G.B.T.Q. issues.\par \par \uc2 But he said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  had always seemed more difficult for the president.\par \par \uc2 ''Biden has never sought power to make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  more available,'' Mr. Carr said. ''It's just not part of who he is.''\par \par \uc2 The president admitted as much in an interview on NBC's ''Meet the Press'' in 2007.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm a practicing Catholic,'' he said. ''And it is the biggest dilemma for me in terms of comporting my religious and cultural views with my political responsibility.''\par \par \uc2 A Lack of Trust\par \par \uc2 Two days before the Supreme Court's ruling overturning Roe v. Wade, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates met at the White House with some of Mr. Biden's top aides and with Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , who has become a forceful voice of the administration on the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Everyone knew what was likely to happen, after Politico's publication weeks earlier of a draft opinion in the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case of Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization. But some of the people around the table left unhappy with the administration's plans to respond to the ruling.\par \par \uc2 ''It was a very frustrating meeting where we were looking to the White House for guidance,'' Ms. Manson, of \uc2 Catholics for Choice\uc2 , said. ''And instead, what we got was a recap of all the conversations they had had with all of us.''\par \par \uc2 Others at the meeting described it differently, saying the administration had spent weeks preparing for the Dobbs ruling in a series of productive meetings with activists.\par \par \uc2 But the frustration clearly underscored the tension between Mr. Biden and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights activists, many of whom have said publicly that the president's past positions make it hard for them to trust that he is all-in on the fight.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden's aides note that he has used the word ''\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 '' a handful of times since the ruling. And in a statement on Saturday condemning a new Indiana law banning almost all abortions, the White House used the term in reiterating support for \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But some veterans of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights movement say they remain wary of a president who is uncomfortable with using the word. Others say they are willing to judge Mr. Biden by his actions.\par \par \uc2 Mini Timmaraju, the president of \uc2 NARAL Pro-Choice America\uc2 , said there was value in Mr. Biden's approach, which can appeal to a broader audience. But she said the president should not avoid using direct, forceful language at a moment when people are scared.\par \par \uc2 ''He's done that,'' she said. ''And he's going to need to get more comfortable with that because this is the modern-day Democratic Party. He's getting there, from what I can see.''\par \par \uc2 Katie Rogers contributed reporting from Washington, and Maggie Haberman from New York.\par \par \uc2 Katie Rogers contributed reporting from Washington, and Maggie Haberman from New York.\par \par \uc2 Joseph R. Biden Jr., then the president-elect, and Dr. Jill Biden attending Mass on Jan. 20, 2021, at the Cathedral of St. Matthew the Apostle in Washington. (PHOTOGRAPH BY DOUG MILLS/THE NEW YORK TIMES); Supporters of \uc2  rights protesting in front of the White House last month, left. Mr. Biden with Pope Francis in October, right. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY SHURAN HUANG FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES; DIVISIONE PRODUZIONE FOTOGRAFICA/VATICAN MEDIA, VIA ASSOCIATED PRESS) (A21) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220807ei870004q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
