\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Indiana's New  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Law Draws Ire of Major Employers\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lora Kelley \par \uc2 580 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 August 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 25\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The drug company \uc2 Eli Lilly\uc2  said it ''will be forced'' to look outside the state for employment growth. The engine maker \uc2 Cummins\uc2  said the law will ''impede our ability to attract and retain top talent.''\par \par \uc2 On Friday, the governor of Indiana signed into law a near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, making the state the first to approve sweeping new restrictions since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade in June.\par \par \uc2 On Saturday morning, one of Indiana's biggest employers, the pharmaceutical company \uc2 Eli Lilly\uc2 , issued a strong objection to the new restrictions. ''Given this new law,'' it said in a statement, ''we will be forced to plan for more employment growth outside our home state.''\par \par \uc2 The company, which employs more than 10,000 people in Indiana, began by saying that ''\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a divisive and deeply personal issue with no clear consensus among the citizens of Indiana.'' It noted that \uc2 Eli Lilly\uc2  has expanded its employee health plan coverage to include travel for reproductive services. But, it added, ''that may not be enough for some current and potential employees.''\par \par \uc2 It was among the first major employers in the state to weigh in on the new law.\par \par \uc2 Shortly after, Jon Mills, a spokesman for \uc2 Cummins\uc2 , an engine company that employs about 10,000 people in the state, said: ''The right to make decisions regarding \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  ensures that women have the same opportunity as others to participate fully in our work force and that our work force is diverse. There are provisions in the bill that conflict with this, impact our people and impede our ability to attract and retain top talent.'' He added that \uc2 Cummins\uc2 's health care benefits cover elective \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  procedures, including medical travel benefits.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Mills also said that, ''prior to, and during the legislative process, we shared our concerns about this legislation with legislative leadership.''\par \par \uc2 Roche, the diagnostics company that has its North American headquarters in Indianapolis, did not have an immediate comment. Other companies with headquarters or large offices in Indiana did not immediately respond to requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 After the Supreme Court's decision, few companies weighed in directly on the ruling. Far more did say they would expand their employer health care coverage to cover travel and other expenses for employees who may need to seek \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care out of state.\par \par \uc2 Some companies with a large presence in Indiana have previously stated that they will cover travel for employees. In June, \uc2 Kroger\uc2  said that it would cover up to $4,000 in travel expenses for employees on its health care plan. The software company \uc2 Salesforce\uc2 , which has about 2,300 employees in Indianapolis, has also said that it would move employees who want to leave states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is banned. \uc2 Kroger\uc2  declined to comment. \uc2 Salesforce\uc2  did not immediately respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 In its statement, \uc2 Eli Lilly\uc2  described the Indiana law as ''one of the most restrictive anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws in the United States.'' It went on: ''As a global company headquartered in Indianapolis for more than 145 years, we work hard to retain and attract thousands of people who are important drivers of our state's economy. Given this new law, we will be forced to plan for more employment growth outside our home state.''\par \par \uc2 Eli Lilly's corporate headquarters in Indianapolis. (PHOTOGRAPH BY Darron Cummings/Associated Press FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220807ei8700051\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
