\par\par \uc2 ROSS DOUTHAT\par \uc2 Sunday Review Desk; SECTSR\par \b \uc2 The New Politics of  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ross Douthat \par \uc2 918 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 August 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Some liberals seemed genuinely surprised by the results of the Kansas referendum on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . A reliably Republican state, a sweeping pro-choice victory. Who could have foreseen it?\par \par \uc2 Others suggested that only the pro-life side should be shocked. ''The anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement has long claimed that voters would reward Republicans for overturning Roe,'' wrote Slate's Mark Joseph Stern. ''They are now discovering how delusional that conviction has always been.''\par \par \uc2 It's true that activists often tend toward unrealistic optimism. But nobody who favored overturning Roe ought to be particularly surprised by the Kansas result. By the margin, maybe -- but a Republican state voting to preserve a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  emphasizes what's always been apparent: With the end of Roe, the pro-life movement now has to adapt to the democratic contest that it sought.\par \par \uc2 Right now, majorities of Americans favor \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions that were ruled out under Roe, but only slightly over a third of the country takes the position that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be largely illegal, a number that shrinks if you remove various exceptions.\par \par \uc2 That means that millions of Americans who voted for Donald Trump favor a right to a first-trimester \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- some of them old-fashioned country-club Republicans, others secular working-class voters or anti-woke ''Barstool conservatives'' who dislike elite progressivism but find religious conservatism alienating as well.\par \par \uc2 In many red as well as purple states, those constituencies hold the balance of power. Even with exceptions, a state probably needs to be either very Republican or very religious for a first-trimester \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban to be popular, which basically means the Deep South and Mountain (and especially Mormon) West. That was clear before Roe fell -- that outright bans would be the exceptions, and the contest in many states would be over how far restrictions can go.\par \par \uc2 The Kansas result confirms that assumption. The state already has a late-term ban, and the prolix ballot measure didn't specify an alternative, it just promised the legislature a general power to write new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws. Would the result have been different if the referendum had proposed restrictions around 12 weeks? I suspect so. Can the pro-life movement settle for that kind of goal? Well, that's the question, with different states supplying different answers.\par \par \uc2 In purple-ish Georgia, Gov. \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  signed a law in 2019, which is now taking effect, banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after around six weeks with various exceptions; he looks like he's on his way to re-election. In reddish Florida, the popular governor, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , is making his stand for now on a ban after 15 weeks.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, Republican gubernatorial nominees in Pennsylvania and Michigan have a record of taking few-exceptions stances that seem ill-suited to their states.\par \par \uc2 I suspect that liberals are deceiving themselves if they imagine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  becoming a dominant issue in an environment as economically and geopolitically fraught as this one. But at the margins there are clear opportunities: If Republicans run on no-exceptions platforms in moderately conservative states or back first-trimester bans in swing states, they will lose some winnable elections.\par \par \uc2 But again, serious pro-lifers have always known that if you bring \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  back to the democratic process, you have to deal with public opinion as it actually exists. And the way you change opinion is by proving the incremental version of your ideas workable, so that voters trust you more and more.\par \par \uc2 That requires addressing immediate anxieties head-on. It is not enough, for instance, for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents to react to stories about delayed care for miscarriages or ectopic pregnancies in pro-life states by pointing out that the laws are being misinterpreted. All officialdom in those states should be mobilized to make hospitals fear malpractice suits more than hypothetical pro-life prosecution.\par \par \uc2 And it requires longer-term creativity, so that every new protection for the unborn is combined with reassurances that mothers and children alike will be better supported than they are today.\par \par \uc2 When I make the latter point I get a reliable liberal retort, to the effect that Republicans could have done more for families already, and didn't, so why would that ever change?\par \par \uc2 But this is the point of bringing democratic pressure to bear. Religious conservatives have pushed Republicans away from libertarian economics in the past -- ''compassionate conservatism'' emerged from evangelicals and Catholics -- but so long as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was essentially a judicial battle, the link to family policy was indirect.\par \par \uc2 Now that Republicans have to legislate on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , though, there are incentives to make the link explicit -- especially in states where socially conservative Democrats, especially Hispanic voters, might join a pro-life coalition.\par \par \uc2 That doesn't mean it will happen, just that the incentives of democratic politics are how it would happen. The end of Roe opens the door wide to a pro-life movement that's incrementalist and creative; it doesn't ensure that such a movement will emerge. But the results in Kansas show what will happen if it doesn't.\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Twitter\uc2  (@NYTOpinion) and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY Marco Bello/Reuters FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220807ei870004w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
