\par\par \uc2 MICHELLE GOLDBERG\par \uc2 Sunday Review Desk; SECTSR\par \b \uc2 Democracy And Choice Win in Kansas\b0\par\par\uc2 By Michelle Goldberg \par \uc2 996 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 August 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 One side effect of Roe v. Wade was that it allowed the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement to pretend to be on the side of democracy. True, the decision was popular, and majorities since the 1970s have wanted to see \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legal in at least some circumstances. But Roe prevented duly elected state governments from passing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions that were in some cases also popular with their constituents. The goal of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement was and remains national prohibition. Its language called for returning the matter to the state voters.\par \par \uc2 The stunning result in an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  referendum in Kansas on Tuesday, however, shows that even in a very red state, bans cannot necessarily survive contact with democracy. Kansas has its own version of Roe, a 2019 State Supreme Court decision holding that the state Constitution protects ''a woman's right to make decisions about her body, including the decision whether to continue her pregnancy.'' The referendum, the first statewide test of electoral sentiment about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  post-Roe, asked voters whether they wanted to change the Constitution so the Republican-controlled Legislature could ban abortions. They did not.\par \par \uc2 When I spoke to pro-choice organizers last month, they were cautiously optimistic that the vote would be close, though they worried about its timing. Rather than scheduling the referendum for the general election, Republicans put it on the primary ballot, when conservative turnout is typically higher. The pro-choice side needed to get people to show up on a day when they weren't used to voting. As far as I can tell, no one expected the 18-point landslide in a state that voted for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  by 15 points.\par \par \uc2 Then again, maybe we should have. It's not uncommon for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans to fail in state referendums. In 2006, South Dakota voters overturned a strict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, a direct challenge to Roe, by 11 points. In 2011, Mississippi voters rejected a constitutional amendment defining a fertilized egg as a person by 17 percentage points. Even in the most conservative parts of the country, many people recoil from strict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans.\par \par \uc2 I hope Kansas sends a message to other red states that have passed draconian \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  prohibitions or are weighing them. I'm not sure it will, because those bans are often an expression not of democratic wishes, but of lawmakers' insulation from democratic accountability. An extreme example is Wisconsin, a purple state with a Democratic governor that voted for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in the last election. When Roe was overturned, there was widespread confusion about whether an 1849 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban had gone back into effect, and as a result, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services have been halted.\par \par \uc2 There is little reason to think that this is what the people of Wisconsin want, but it's not clear if they can pass a law to change it, because state legislative maps are drawn in a way that gives Republicans an overwhelming advantage. According to a University of Wisconsin Law School analysis, if Democrats and Republicans got the same number of votes, Republicans would win 64.8 percent of State Senate seats, and Democrats around 35.2 percent.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, this doesn't mean that the backlash to the Supreme Court decision jettisoning Roe won't have important electoral implications. Since it came down, polls show a shift toward Democrats in the midterm congressional vote. Some politicians, like \uc2 Nicole Malliotakis\uc2 , the only Republican member of Congress from New York City, will likely be damaged by their opposition to legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Still, votes for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights don't automatically translate into votes for Democrats, because partisan identification is often more powerful than issue preference. In 2020, Missouri voters approved a constitutional amendment expanding Medicaid eligibility, but the state has continued to favor Republicans overwhelmingly. The same year, Florida's voters opted to raise the minimum wage to $15 an hour even as they gave a majority to Trump.\par \par \uc2 Clearly, however, there are substantial numbers of voters outraged by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans and ready to express their anger at the ballot box. Kansas's secretary of state predicted that turnout on Tuesday would be 36 percent. It ended up being closer to 50 percent, almost as high as in the 2018 general election.\par \par \uc2 The anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement has already been aided by minority rule. Roe's end was made possible because a president who lost the popular vote was able to put three judges on the Supreme Court. The filibuster means that even with the support of the pro-choice Republican senators \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  and \uc2 Lisa Murkowski\uc2 , the Senate can't codify any of Roe's protections.\par \par \uc2 As time goes on, and the harrowing consequences of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans pile up, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents will need ever greater limits on popular sovereignty in order to impose their regime on an unwilling nation. The cause of ''life,'' as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents define it, will likely merge with the broader Republican campaign to disenfranchise those it defines as outside the blessed circle of real Americanness.\par \par \uc2 In a recent \uc2 New York Times Magazine\uc2  cover story, Charles Homans described how the ''Stop the Steal'' movement transcended \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . ''The hole he punched in American democracy, out of sheer self-interest, had allowed his followers to glimpse a vision of the country restored to its divinely ordained promise that lay beyond that democracy -- but also beyond him,'' wrote Homans. The Kansas referendum demonstrated that democracy in America can still work, and why the forces of religious authoritarianism are so set on destroying it.\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Twitter\uc2  (@NYTopinion) and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY Brendan Smialowski/Agence France-Presse -- Getty Images FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220807ei8700037\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
