\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Stung by Kansas Vote, Republicans Shift Tone On Full  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Bans\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jonathan Weisman and Katie Glueck \par \uc2 1,940 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 August 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 After Kansans voted to preserve \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, Republicans who once said the economy reigns supreme are acknowledging the issue will be a centerpiece in the fall campaigns.\par \par \uc2 Republican candidates, facing a stark reality check from Kansas voters, are softening their once-uncompromising stands against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as they move toward the general election, recognizing that strict bans are unpopular and that the issue may be a major driver in the fall campaigns.\par \par \uc2 In swing states and even conservative corners of the country, several Republicans have shifted their talk on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, newly emphasizing support for exceptions. Some have noticeably stopped discussing details at all. Pitched battles in Republican-dominated state legislatures have broken out now that the Supreme Court has made what has long been a theoretical argument a reality.\par \par \uc2 In Pennsylvania, \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2 , the Republicans' ardently anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  candidate for governor, has lately taken to saying ''the people of Pennsylvania'' will ''decide what \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  looks like'' in the state, not the governor. In Minnesota, Scott Jensen, a family physician who said in March that he would ''try to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 '' as governor, said in a video released before the Kansas vote that he does support some exceptions: ''If I've been unclear previously, I want to be clear now.''\par \par \uc2 Republican consultants for Senate and House campaigns said Thursday that while they still believe inflation and the economy will drive voters to the G.O.P., candidates are going to have to talk about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to blunt Democratic attacks that the party's position is extreme. They have started advising Republicans to endorse bans that allow exceptions for pregnancies from rape or incest or those that threaten the life of the mother. They have told candidates to emphasize care for women during and after their pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 ''If we are going to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , there are things we've got to do to make sure the need for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is reduced, and that women are not endangered,'' said Representative Nancy Mace, Republican of South Carolina, who won an exemption for rape and incest in her state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law as a state representative. Now, she says Republicans need to press to expand access to gynecological and obstetrics care, contraception, including emergency contraception, and even protect the right of women to leave their states to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  without fear of prosecution.\par \par \uc2 Messaging alone will not free the G.O.P. from accusations that it is out of step with voters. Several Republican-led states have passed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans that do not include allowances for victims of rape or incest, including Alabama, Arkansas, Florida, Louisiana and Texas. While all bans allow an exception to save the life of the woman, those in some states do not also cite protection of her health.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, after a prolonged debate, the Indiana House approved a ban that included exceptions for rape, incest and the life and physical health of the mother.\par \par \uc2 Republican are also contending with drumbeat of news after the Supreme Court's decision, including the story of a 10-year-old rape victim in Indiana who crossed state lines to receive an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and headlines about women who confronted serious health problems under new, far-reaching restrictions or bans.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  of Florida, who has recently avoided talking about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , suspended a state attorney from Hillsborough County who refused to prosecute people who try to provide abortions prohibited by the state's new 15-week ban, prompting angry recriminations from Democrats.\par \par \uc2 The recalibration for some began before voters of deeply Republican Kansas voted overwhelmingly on Tuesday against removing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights from the state's constitution. Since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade, retracting the constitutional right to the procedure, many Republicans were slow to detail what would come next. As they rush to enact long-promised laws, Republican-led legislatures have learned how difficult banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  can be.\par \par \uc2 ''Not just the pro-choice movement but the pro-life movement was caught by surprise'' by the Supreme Court, said Brandon Steele, a West Virginia delegate who pressed for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban without exceptions in a special session of the legislature that ended this week with the Republican supermajority stymied. ''Without having the talking points, without being told what to do, legislators had to start saying what they were actually going to do. You could see the confusion in the room.''\par \par \uc2 ''We're finding out who is really pro-life and who is pro-life only to get elected, not just in West Virginia but across the country,'' Mr. Steele said.\par \par \uc2 In Indiana, the special session of the state legislature to consider a ban has included brutal debates over whether to include exemptions and how far those exemptions should go.\par \par \uc2 ''For some it's very black and white: if you're pro-life with no exceptions or if you're pro-choice with no restrictions,'' said State Senator Kyle Walker, an Indiana Republican who said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal during at least the first trimester of pregnancy. ''When you are in the gray area, you are forced to reconcile in your own mind where your own limits are.''\par \par \uc2 For months, Republicans have maintained that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights would be a footnote in a midterm campaign driven by the worst inflation in 40 years, crime, immigration and a Democratic president whose approval ratings are mired around 40 percent.\par \par \uc2 That is still the public line, even after the Kansas referendum, where voters faced a single issue, not the multiplicity of factors they will be considering in November.\par \par \uc2 But the reality on the campaign trail is different. Sarah Longwell, a Republican pollster, said in her focus groups that swing voters do bring up inflation and the economy when asked what issues are on their minds. But when prompted to discuss \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , real passion flares. That indicates that if Democrats can prosecute a campaign to keep the issue front and center, they will find an audience, she said.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Mace agreed, saying that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is rising fast and that Republicans have to respond.\par \par \uc2 In Minnesota, Dr. Jensen, the Republican candidate expected to take on Gov. \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , suggested it was interactions with voters after the fall of Roe that, he said, prompted him to clarify his position on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''Once the Roe v. Wade decision was overturned, we told Minnesota, and basically told everybody that we would engage in a conversation,'' he said. ''During that conversation, I learned of the need for me to elaborate on my position.''\par \par \uc2 That elaboration included embracing a family and maternity leave program, promoting a $2,500-per-child adoption tax credit, and improving access to birth control, including providing oral contraceptives over the counter with a price ceiling. And, like \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2 , the G.O.P. Senate nominee in Nevada, Dr. Jensen pointed to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections already in place in Minnesota to cast the matter as settled rather than on the ballot this year.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Walz said he would stay on offense, and not accept any softening of the Republican line.\par \par \uc2 ''I take them at their first word,'' he said of Dr. Jensen and his running mate, Matt Birk, a former N.F.L. player and anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocate. ''If they get the opportunity they will criminalize this while we're trying to protect it. So it's become a central theme, obviously, I think that flip on their part was in response to that.''\par \par \uc2 The Kansas vote implies that around 65 percent of voters nationwide would reject rolling back \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, including a majority in more than 40 of the 50 states, according to a New York Times analysis.\par \par \uc2 Republicans believe their party can grab the mantle of moderation from Democrats, in part by conveying empathy toward pregnant women and offering exemptions to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, and casting Democrats as the extremists when it comes to regulating \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . If Democrats insist on making \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  the centerpiece of their campaigns, they argue, they risk looking out of touch with voters in an uncertain economy.\par \par \uc2 But Republicans who moderate their views must still contend with a core base of support that remains staunchly anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents said Thursday that Republican candidates should not read too much into the Kansas vote, a single-issue referendum with language that was criticized by voters on both sides as confusing.\par \par \uc2 ''Regardless of what the consultant class is telling the candidates, they would be wise to recognize that the right-to-life community is an important constituency and an important demographic of voters,'' warned Penny Nance, chief executive and president of Concerned Women for America, a conservative organization that opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 After the Kansas vote, Democrats stepped up efforts to squeeze their opponents between a conservative base eager for quick action to ban all abortions and a broader electorate that wants no such thing. Representative \uc2 Elaine Luria\uc2 , a moderate Democrat running in a Republican-leaning district in southeastern Virginia, released a new advertisement against her Republican opponent, Jen Kiggans, painting her as ''too extreme'' on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Ms. Luria had initially said she would campaign on her work for the district and her support for the Navy, a big force in the region, but the landscape has shifted. Ms. Kiggans's campaign did not respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 A group aligned with the \uc2 Democratic Governors Association\uc2  is already advertising off \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related remarks made by Tudor Dixon of Michigan, who won the Republican nomination for governor this week.\par \par \uc2 ''If you take Tudor Dixon at her word when it comes to outlawing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , she's told us exactly who she is,'' the spot, titled ''No Exceptions,'' intones, featuring clips of Ms. Dixon highlighting her opposition to a range of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related exceptions. Ms. Dixon was unambiguous about her position earlier this summer, writing on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , ''My only exception is to protect the LIFE of the mother.''\par \par \uc2 In a lengthy statement that highlighted her opposition to an expected ballot measure in Michigan intended to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, Ms. Dixon also insisted that her race would be defined by jobs, schools, crime and being ''able to afford your gas and groceries.''\par \par \uc2 For Republicans, one problem might be the extensive trail on the issue they left during the primary season.\par \par \uc2 In May, Mr. Mastriano was unequivocal in Pennsylvania as he courted Republican primary voters: ''That baby deserves a right to life whether it is conceived in incest or rape or there are concerns otherwise for the mom.''\par \par \uc2 Last month, he said it was not up to him. ''You decide on exceptions. You decide on how early. And that's in the hands of the people,'' he said on Philadelphia talk radio. ''That's a fact. That's not a dodge.''\par \par \uc2 Mitch Smith, Trip Gabriel and Reid J. Epstein contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Mitch Smith, Trip Gabriel and Reid J. Epstein contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 A rally for \uc2  rights in Kansas this week, before voters chose to keep \uc2  protections in the state Constitution. (PHOTOGRAPH BY ARIN YOON FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); Representative Nancy Mace, Republican of South Carolina, won an exception for incest and rape in her state's \uc2  law. (PHOTOGRAPH BY LOGAN R. CYRUS FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); Anti-\uc2  protesters last week at the Indiana Statehouse, where the House approved a ban with several exceptions. (PHOTOGRAPH BY KAITI SULLIVAN FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); Scott Jensen, a Republican running for governor of Minnesota, appeared to soften his plan to ''try to ban \uc2 '' if he won. (PHOTOGRAPH BY GLEN STUBBE/STAR TRIBUNE, VIA ASSOCIATED PRESS) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220806ei8600031\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
