\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Rights Might Win Voter Support in 40 States\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nate Cohn \par \uc2 1,150 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 August 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The referendum in a reliably Republican state shows which party has the most energy on the issue.\par \par \uc2 There was every reason to expect a close election.\par \par \uc2 Instead, Tuesday's resounding victory for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters in Kansas offered some of the most concrete evidence yet that the Supreme Court's decision to overturn Roe v. Wade has shifted the political landscape. The victory, by a 59-41 margin in a Republican stronghold, suggests Democrats will be the energized party on an issue where Republicans have usually had an enthusiasm advantage.\par \par \uc2 The Kansas vote implies that around 65 percent of voters nationwide would reject a similar initiative to roll back \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, including in more than 40 of the 50 states (a few states on each side are very close to 50-50). This is a rough estimate, based on how demographic characteristics predicted the results of recent \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  referendums. But it is an evidence-based way of arriving at a fairly obvious conclusion: If \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights wins 59 percent support in Kansas, it's doing even better than that nationwide.\par \par \uc2 It's a tally that's in line with recent national surveys that showed greater support for legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the court's decision. And the high turnout, especially among Democrats, confirms that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not just some wedge issue of importance to political activists. The stakes of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy have become high enough that it can drive a high midterm-like turnout on its own.\par \par \uc2 None of this proves that the issue will help Democrats in the midterm elections. And there are limits to what can be gleaned from the Kansas data. But the lopsided margin makes one thing clear: The political winds are now at the backs of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters.\par \par \uc2 A surprisingly decisive outcome\par \par \uc2 There was not much public polling in the run-up to the Kansas election, but the best available data suggested that voters would probably split fairly evenly on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In a Times compilation of national polling published this spring, 48 percent of Kansas voters said they thought \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be mostly legal compared with 47 percent who thought it should be mostly illegal. Similarly, the Cooperative Election Study in 2020 found that the state's registered voters were evenly split on whether \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal.\par \par \uc2 The results of similar recent referendums in Alabama, Louisiana, Tennessee and West Virginia also pointed toward a close race in Kansas -- perhaps even one in which a ''no'' vote to preserve \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights would have the edge.\par \par \uc2 As with the Kansas vote, a ''yes'' vote in each of those four states' initiatives would have amended a state constitution to allow significant restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights or funding for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In contrast with Kansas, the initiatives passed in all four states, including a 24-point victory in Louisiana in 2020. But support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights outpaced support for Democratic presidential candidates in relatively white areas across all four states, especially in less religious areas outside the Deep South.\par \par \uc2 It's a pattern that suggests \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights would have much greater support than \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  did as a candidate in a relatively white state like Kansas -- perhaps even enough to make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights favored to survive.\par \par \uc2 It may seem surprising that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  supporters would even have a chance in Kansas, given the state's long tradition of voting for Republicans. But Kansas is more reliably Republican than it is conservative. The state has an above-average number of college graduates, a group that has swung toward Democrats in recent years.\par \par \uc2 Kansas voted for Donald J. Trump by around 15 percentage points in 2020, enough to make it pretty safely Republican. Yet it's not quite off the board for Democrats. Republicans have learned this the hard way; look no further than the 2018 Democratic victory in the governor's race.\par \par \uc2 Even so, a landslide victory for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in Kansas did not appear to be a probable outcome, whether based on the polls or the recent initiatives. The likeliest explanations for the surprise: Voters may be more supportive of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the aftermath of the overturning of Roe (as national polls imply); they may be more cautious about eliminating \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights now that there are real policy consequences to these initiatives; \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters may be more energized to go to the polls.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters may not always find it so easy to advance their cause. They were defending the status quo in Kansas; elsewhere, they will be trying to overturn \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans.\par \par \uc2 Whatever the explanation, if \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  supporters could fare as well as they did in Kansas, they would have a good chance to defend \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights almost anywhere in the country. The state may not be as conservative as Alabama, but it is much more conservative than the nation as a whole -- and the result was not close. There are only seven states -- in the Deep South and the Mountain West -- where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters would be expected to fail in a hypothetically similar initiative.\par \par \uc2 A shift in turnout\par \par \uc2 If there's any rule about partisan turnout in American politics, it's that registered Republicans turn out at higher rates than registered Democrats.\par \par \uc2 While the Kansas figures are still preliminary, it appears that registered Democrats were likelier to vote than registered Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Overall, 276,000 voters participated in the Democratic primary, which was held on Tuesday as well, compared with 451,000 who voted in the Republican primary. The Democratic tally amounted to 56 percent of the number of registered Democrats in the state, while the number of Republican primary voters was 53 percent of the number of registered Republicans. (Unaffiliated voters are the second-largest group in Kansas.)\par \par \uc2 In Johnson County, outside Kansas City, 67 percent of registered Democrats turned out, compared with 60 percent of registered Republicans.\par \par \uc2 This is a rare feat for Democrats in a high-turnout election. In nearby Iowa, where historical turnout data is easily accessible, turnout among registered Democrats in a general election has never eclipsed turnout among registered Republicans in at least 40 years.\par \par \uc2 The superior Democratic turnout helps explain why the result was less favorable for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents than expected. And it confirms that Democrats are now far more energized on the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue, reversing a pattern from recent elections. It may even raise Democrats' hopes that they could defy the longstanding tendency for the president's party to have poor turnout in midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 For Republicans, the turnout figures may offer a modest silver lining. They might reasonably hope that turnout will be more favorable in the midterms in November, when \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't be the only issue on the ballot and Republicans will have many more reasons to vote -- including control of Congress.\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY  FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220805ei850004z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
