\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Claim Fetus as Dependent? You Can on Georgia Taxes.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ava Sasani \par \uc2 958 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 August 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The state's ''fetal personhood'' provision now includes a $3,000 tax exemption starting at around six weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Georgia's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban counts a fetus as a person. And now, so does its tax code.\par \par \uc2 The state's Department of Revenue announced this week that ''any unborn child with a detectable human heartbeat'' can be claimed as dependent, providing a $3,000 tax exemption for each pregnancy within a household, months before the child is born. Georgia's law bans most abortions after six weeks, which is usually around when doctors can begin to detect fetal cardiac activity.\par \par \uc2 The announcement marks a new frontier of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policymaking in a post-Roe America, where conservative lawmakers have moved beyond banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and are now trying to expand the legal rights and protections afforded to a fetus under ''fetal personhood'' laws. Georgia, Alabama and Arizona have passed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans that include language broadly defining a fetus as a person.\par \par \uc2 Separately, nearly 40 states, including Texas and California, define a fetus as a person in cases involving homicide. For example, Scott Peterson in 2004 was convicted in California of murdering his wife and unborn child. His wife, Laci Peterson, was eight months pregnant when she was killed.\par \par \uc2 Georgia's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law goes further than any other fetal personhood provision. Called the Living Infants Fairness and Equality, or LIFE, Act, it prohibits \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after six weeks and explicitly recognizes the fetus as a person.\par \par \uc2 A federal judge struck down the legislation last summer, finding that it violated a woman's constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The appeals court delayed a final decision, pending a ruling from the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 After the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade on June 24, a three-judge panel from the U.S. Court of Appeals for the 11th Circuit handed Georgia conservatives their long-anticipated victory, allowing the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions to take effect.\par \par \uc2 In the appeals court's July 20 ruling, Chief Judge William H. Pryor Jr. wrote that ''a person of reasonable intelligence is capable of understanding that the 'core meaning [of]' the provision is to expand the definition of person to include unborn humans who are carried in the womb of their mother at any stage of development.''\par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 , who signed the law in 2019, celebrated the court's decision.\par \par \uc2 ''Georgia is a state that values and supports life at all stages -- and the Georgia LIFE Act and this provision both reflect that commitment,'' said a spokesman for Mr. Kemp.\par \par \uc2 State Representative Ed Setzler, a Republican sponsor of the law, said in a leaked 2019 video that the ultimate goal of the law is to have the Supreme Court acknowledge the personhood of a fetus.\par \par \uc2 ''It is about establishing personhood of the unborn child, in the tax code, for child support for mothers, in our census counts, across our code, so that we can lay the foundation that no other state in the nation in the last 46 years has ever done, which is to establish the personhood of this child, and we're going to take this to the highest court in the land,'' Mr. Setzler said in the video.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Setzler did not respond to requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 Georgia's push to recognize a fetus as a person could lay the blueprint for other conservative states, which have not yet clarified the meaning and effects of such an approach, legal experts said.\par \par \uc2 ''The anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement has always been a personhood movement, there was just no consensus on what that actually meant,'' said Mary Ziegler, a law professor at the \uc2 University of California, Davis\uc2 , who has written several books on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the law.\par \par \uc2 Before the demise of Roe, conservatives were united around the singular goal of restricting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. But Ms. Ziegler says the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement is struggling to find consensus on what fetal personhood means in a post-Roe legal landscape.\par \par \uc2 ''Nobody has really worked out, how do you enforce personhood beyond just 'you can't have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,''' she said. ''Georgia is starting to work that out, but they've really only looked at a handful of situations. How do you enforce this in HOV lanes? Do you give a fetus its own attorney? There are so many questions left open.''\par \par \uc2 Last month, a pregnant Texas woman ticketed for riding in the HOV lane alone argued that her fetus counted as a person under the state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. Texas' \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban does not included fetal personhood, but its penal code does.\par \par \uc2 Georgia's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law also allows the mother to collect child support to cover the cost of ''direct medical and pregnancy related expenses'' before the child is born.\par \par \uc2 Democrats said the law might also expose women who experience miscarriages to unknown consequences.\par \par \uc2 ''So what happens when you claim your fetus as a dependent and then miscarry later in the pregnancy, you get investigated both for tax fraud and an illegal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?'' tweeted Lauren Groh-Wargo, the campaign manager of the Democratic candidate for governor of Georgia, \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Monday's announcement says taxpayers who claim the new deduction may be asked to provide medical records or documentation of the pregnancy. The Revenue Department has not clarified if and how families who lose a pregnancy might protect themselves from allegations of fraud.\par \par \uc2 Specific instructions on how to claim a fetus on a tax return are expected later this year.\par \par \uc2 A law signed by Gov. Brian Kemp in 2019 expands the legal definition of personhood to include a fetus and an embryo.  (PHOTOGRAPH BY Nicole Craine for The New York Times FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220805ei850003y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
