\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 DeSantis, Citing Incompetence, Suspends Prosecutor Who Vowed Not to Criminalize  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Patricia Mazzei \par \uc2 1,160 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 August 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The stance of Andrew H. Warren, the elected state attorney of Hillsborough County, was reflected by several other prosecutors across the country.\par \par \uc2 MIAMI -- Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  of Florida suspended the top prosecutor in Tampa on Thursday, accusing him of incompetence and neglect of duty for vowing not to prosecute those who seek or provide abortions.\par \par \uc2 In a startling announcement, Mr. DeSantis, a Republican, suspended from office Andrew H. Warren, the elected state attorney of Hillsborough County. In June, Mr. Warren, a Democrat, was among 90 elected prosecutors across the country who vowed not to prosecute those who seek or provide abortions after the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade. Florida imposed a 15-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in April.\par \par \uc2 The decision immediately raised concerns among Democrats, including Mr. Warren, who say that the governor has become increasingly heavy-handed.\par \par \uc2 Mr. DeSantis said that the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  statement and other actions by Mr. Warren amounted to ''incompetence and willful defiance of his duties,'' and that the prosecutor's approach to the job left Mr. DeSantis with no choice but to suspend him.\par \par \uc2 ''When you flagrantly violate your oath of office, when you make yourself above the law, you have violated your duty, you have neglected your duty and you are displaying a lack of competence to be able to perform those duties,'' Mr. DeSantis said to cheers at the Hillsborough County Sheriff's Office, where he was flanked by a gaggle of uniformed sheriffs and police chiefs.\par \par \uc2 The suspension of Mr. Warren, a prominent Democrat and frequent DeSantis critic who is serving in his second term as the chief prosecutor for the 13th Judicial Circuit in Hillsborough County, evidently came as news even to his office.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Warren said in a statement that his suspension ''spits in the face of the voters.''\par \par \uc2 ''Today's political stunt is an illegal overreach that continues a dangerous pattern by \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  of using his office to further his own political ambition,'' Mr. Warren said, adding, ''The people have the right to elect their own leaders -- not have them dictated by an aspiring presidential candidate who has shown time and again he feels accountable to no one.''\par \par \uc2 The law enforcement officials who appeared with Mr. DeSantis expressed frustration with Mr. Warren for not prosecuting certain crimes. ''Andrew Warren is a fraud,'' said Brian Dugan, a former chief of the Tampa Police Department.\par \par \uc2 The sheriffs and police chiefs took turns praising Mr. DeSantis and criticizing liberal-leaning cities -- New Orleans, Chicago, Los Angeles, San Francisco -- by name. Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups also applauded Mr. DeSantis.\par \par \uc2 ''Want to know why some people are moving to Florida?'' Mr. DeSantis said. ''Because their communities are no longer safe, thanks to prosecutors who think they know better.''\par \par \uc2 Even in a state where governors and prosecutors have been known to tussle in the open, Mr. DeSantis's intervention -- and his trumpeting of it -- surprised officials in Florida. Under Florida law, a governor can suspend state officials for wrongdoing that includes neglect of duty, incompetence, malfeasance, drunkenness or commission of a felony. Mr. DeSantis's Republican predecessor, \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 , tended to suspend elected officials only after they had been charged with a crime.\par \par \uc2 In 2017, when Aramis D. Ayala, a Democrat who was then the state attorney in Orlando, startled the state by saying she would not seek the death penalty in any cases, Mr. Scott reassigned more than two dozen cases to another state attorney's office. But he did not suspend Ms. Ayala, who did not seek re-election after her term and is now running for Florida attorney general.\par \par \uc2 Mr. DeSantis has been much more aggressive. Shortly after taking office in 2019, he suspended Sheriff \uc2 Scott Israel\uc2  of Broward County, a Democrat, faulting him for his handling of the mass shooting at a Parkland high school in 2018, even though Mr. Israel had not been criminally charged. Mr. Israel unsuccessfully appealed his suspension to the courts and the State Senate and later lost a re-election bid.\par \par \uc2 Mr. DeSantis is up for re-election in November and has faced growing criticism from Democrats that his approach to governing has become increasingly authoritarian. On Thursday, the two leading Democrats vying to challenge him, Representative \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2  and \uc2 Nikki Fried\uc2 , the state's agriculture commissioner, reacted to Mr. Warren's suspension by referring to Mr. DeSantis in statements as a ''wannabe dictator'' (Mr. Crist) and a ''dictator'' (Ms. Fried).\par \par \uc2 Miriam Krinsky, the executive director of Fair and Just Prosecution, which put out the statement against criminalizing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that Mr. Warren signed in June, called his suspension ''an unprecedented and dangerous intrusion on the separation of powers and the will of the voters.''\par \par \uc2 ''Governors don't hand pick elected prosecutors, the voters do,'' she said in a statement. ''With this outrageous overreach, Gov. DeSantis is sending a clear message that the will of the people of Hillsborough County matters less than his own political agenda.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Warren was elected to a second four-year term with about 53 percent of the vote in 2020. He was one of many prosecutors who were backed in 2016 by groups supported by the billionaire liberal investor \uc2 George Soros\uc2 . Mr. DeSantis did not name Mr. Soros on Thursday but seemed to allude to him, saying that Mr. Warren had run a campaign funded by out-of-state donors. Mr. DeSantis himself has amassed a campaign war chest brimming with out-of-state donations.\par \par \uc2 ''Criminalizing and prosecuting individuals who seek or provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care makes a mockery of justice,'' Mr. Warren and the other prosecutors said in their joint statement in June. ''Prosecutors should not be part of that.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Warren had previously criticized Mr. DeSantis for enacting anti-protest legislation and creating an election crimes office.\par \par \uc2 In his remarks on Thursday, Mr. DeSantis cited a second statement from Fair and Just Prosecution that Mr. Warren signed that pledged not to criminalize ''transgender people and gender-affirming health care.''\par \par \uc2 ''I don't think the people of Hillsborough want to have an agenda that is basically woke, where you're deciding that your view of social justice means certain laws shouldn't be enforced,'' Mr. DeSantis said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. DeSantis has recently blasted surgeries for transgender adolescents, saying doctors who perform such procedures should be sued. Major medical groups have endorsed so-called gender-affirming medical care for teenage patients, including the use of puberty blockers and hormones when necessary. Genital surgeries are not recommended for those under 18, while guidelines say mastectomies can be offered to teenagers 15 and up.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Florida Department of Health\uc2  has said that it opposes all transition-related care for children, including social changes like names and pronouns.\par \par \uc2 Azeen Ghorayshi contributed reporting. Kitty Bennett contributed research.\par \par \uc2 Azeen Ghorayshi contributed reporting. Kitty Bennett contributed research.\par \par \uc2 Gov. Ron DeSantis has aggressively punished elected attorneys. (PHOTOGRAPH BY CHRIS O'MEARA/ASSOCIATED PRESS) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220805ei8500032\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
