\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Emerging From G.O.P. Pack in a Governor's Race\b0\par\par\uc2 By Julie Bosman \par \uc2 1,092 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 August 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Now comes the hard part, expanding beyond her conservative base to defeat Gov. \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 STERLING HEIGHTS, Mich. -- Tudor Dixon, a former conservative media personality, just pulled off a feat that might have seemed impossible a few months ago, catapulting over her better known competitors in a crowded and chaotic primary to win the Republican nomination for governor in Michigan.\par \par \uc2 The more formidable challenge lies ahead: facing off in the general election against the relatively popular incumbent, Gov. \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 , who enjoys the support of an energized Democratic base.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, Ms. Dixon, who was previously an executive at her family's steel company, spoke of the late-breaking endorsement from former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  that helped woo Republican primary voters -- and of the crucial task of winning over voters beyond her conservative base.\par \par \uc2 ''We wanted to make sure that we could bring the party together,'' Ms. Dixon said in a radio interview. ''We wanted to make sure that we would be solid on the Republican side because we knew going into the general, we have to win independents and we have to win some Democrats.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Dixon, who also had the backing of the wealthy DeVos family in Michigan, will now have to contend with broader political forces. The sweeping rejection by Kansas voters on Tuesday of a constitutional amendment that would have let state legislators ban or significantly restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has further elevated the politics of \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The issue has mobilized voters who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, who could be an especially potent force in Michigan in the general election.\par \par \uc2 Governor Whitmer is solidly in favor of protecting access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Ms. Dixon has said that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be allowed only if necessary to save the life of a mother, and that she would not support exceptions for rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 ''This is going to be an epic battle,'' Ms. Dixon told supporters in a victory speech on Tuesday evening.\par \par \uc2 Matt Grossmann, a professor of American politics at \uc2 Michigan State University\uc2 , said that Ms. Dixon was starting her race against Ms. Whitmer at a disadvantage, given her political inexperience and her relative obscurity for most voters.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Dixon emerged from a Republican primary that was unusually messy, as five candidates -- including two of the most recognizable names in the race -- were removed from the ballot after submitting nominating petitions that contained forged signatures.\par \par \uc2 ''This should have been a good opportunity for Republicans,'' Mr. Grossmann said on Wednesday. ''There was a time when Whitmer was less popular. Tudor Dixon is starting from a much lower position than you would expect the Republican nominee to begin the general election from.''\par \par \uc2 In interviews across the state this week, Republican primary voters said they were fearful of Ms. Whitmer winning a second term but uncertain about Ms. Dixon's ability to unseat her.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Whitmer has drawn criticism from conservatives over rising inflation, her handling of the coronavirus pandemic and the perpetually run-down state of Michigan's roads. But the governor is entering the general election campaign with solid popularity in the state -- her approval rating was 55 percent in a July survey conducted for The Detroit News -- and a base of Democratic voters who have been outraged by a looming threat to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal in Michigan, but conservatives are pushing to enforce a 1931 law that bans the procedure in nearly all cases. Ms. Whitmer has made the issue a centerpiece of her campaign.\par \par \uc2 Dozens of voters interviewed in Michigan this week said that they were supporting Ms. Dixon largely because of Mr. Trump's endorsement, believing that Mr. Trump would have backed the candidate with the strongest conservative credentials.\par \par \uc2 They said they were still learning about what kind of candidate Ms. Dixon was: At an elementary school in Sterling Heights, Mich., on Tuesday, two Republican voters struggled to remember Ms. Dixon's name, though they intended to cast a ballot for her. One man said he was going to vote for the candidate Mr. Trump had endorsed -- ''the lady,'' he said, though he didn't know anything about her.\par \par \uc2 Fred Starcher, 61, a truck driver, said he had only recently heard of Ms. Dixon but was hopeful that she would ease the financial burdens of the working class. He heard her talking during a recent interview, he said, and she impressed him with her promises to advocate for people like farmers.\par \par \uc2 People like him have been barely getting by financially, Mr. Starcher said. When gas prices were at their peak recently, he said, he was paying at least $300 a month to fill up his Jeep, dipping into his savings to make ends meet.\par \par \uc2 ''We're almost depleted,'' Mr. Starcher said. ''I think she'd be for the blue collar. A Republican governor would make a difference.''\par \par \uc2 Don VanSyckel, a member of the board of commissioners in the Republican stronghold of Macomb County, said he voted for Kevin Rinke, a businessman from Oakland County, in the primary, and remained doubtful that Ms. Dixon was qualified for the job.\par \par \uc2 ''Michigan needs help. We need to bring back our small businesses and infrastructure,'' he said. ''I don't think she has the depth of managerial experience.''\par \par \uc2 Many Republican voters said they were eager to support Ms. Dixon, seeing her as a reliable conservative who would push back against Ms. Whitmer. They are still angry over the governor's handling of the coronavirus pandemic, during which she imposed strict rules on business and schools.\par \par \uc2 ''That's what frosted me,'' said T.J. Giraud, 65, a pastor, adding that he opposed masks and restrictions.\par \par \uc2 Democrats who supported Ms. Whitmer said they believed she had to contend with a host of serious issues in the last four years, including an uneven economy and a pandemic that hit Michigan especially hard.\par \par \uc2 After casting a ballot in Ferndale, Mich., Chrissi Novak, 60, said that she wanted to give Ms. Whitmer another term, hoping that she could move beyond the crises that had come to define her tenure.\par \par \uc2 ''I think she's done a decent job and she deserves more time,'' she said. ''She didn't get the best hand.''\par \par \uc2 ''This is going to be an epic battle,'' Tudor Dixon, center, told supporters of the challenge ahead.; Gov. Gretchen Whitmer of Michigan, center, has a base of voters who have been outraged by a looming threat to \uc2  rights. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY EMILY ELCONIN FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220804ei8400046\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
