\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Voters Turned Out in Huge Numbers And Crashed Through Party Lines\b0\par\par\uc2 By Mitch Smith, Lauren Fox and Elizabeth Dias \par \uc2 1,326 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 August 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 15\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Rural Republicans joined suburban Democrats to create huge turnout and a broad coalition that voted to preserve \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights by a fat margin.\par \par \uc2 PRAIRIE VILLAGE, Kan. -- DeAnn Hupe Seib is a fiscally conservative, churchgoing Republican from rural Kansas. When faced with a ballot question about whether \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights ought to be removed from her state's constitution, she voted no. So did her home, Jefferson County, which favored Donald J. Trump by a 32-point margin in 2020.\par \par \uc2 ''I was old enough that I remember stories of women who could not get abortions or had to defy their church in order to get in and get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in order to save their lives,'' said Ms. Hupe Seib, 63, a lawyer. ''So it's a very real issue to me, and I know it can be again.''\par \par \uc2 The sweeping victory for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in Kansas on Tuesday -- the country's first post-Roe vote on the issue -- relied on a broad coalition of voters who turned out in huge numbers and crashed through party and geographic lines to maintain \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in the state. The result was an election with a stunning 18-point margin that is shaking up national politics ahead of the midterm elections.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters used conservative-sounding language about government mandates and personal freedom in their pitch to voters, and made a point of reaching out to independents, Libertarians and moderate Republicans.\par \par \uc2 ''Some of these issues really are nonpartisan -- people don't see it through the partisan lens the way a lot of us do,'' Ashley All, a spokeswoman for Kansans for Constitutional Freedom, which led the effort to defeat the amendment, told reporters on Wednesday. ''We were able to engage folks in a nonpartisan conversation about this issue, and that is something I think that really played to our strengths.''\par \par \uc2 Going into Tuesday, there were many reasons to doubt that supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights could fend off a well-financed effort in a reliably conservative state to open the door for lawmakers to ban or severely restrict the procedure.\par \par \uc2 Republicans in the state hold commanding legislative majorities and have long campaigned on restricting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Roman Catholic Church donated millions of dollars to the effort to pass the amendment. And the issue was strategically placed on the August primary ballot, a time of year when Kansas Republicans usually have competitive races, but when Democrats and political independents often have little to draw them to the polls.\par \par \uc2 But not only did \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters win, they won big -- 59 percent to 41 with most of the votes counted as of Wednesday afternoon -- in a result that was seen nationally as a reflection of voter anger over the demise of Roe v. Wade and a hopeful sign for Democrats in an otherwise foreboding political climate.\par \par \uc2 ''We won this race blindfolded and with one arm tied behind our back. And we still beat the tar out of them,'' said State Representative Stephanie Clayton, a Democrat from Overland Park, in suburban Kansas City, who until a few years ago served as a moderate Republican in the Statehouse.\par \par \uc2 The victory, however, reflects the state's specific brand of politics: conservative and pragmatic, with an independent streak that often defies expectations. The state hasn't supported a Democrat for president since 1964, but routinely elects Democratic governors.\par \par \uc2 The ballot referendum was rooted in a 2019 ruling by the Kansas Supreme Court that found that the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was guaranteed by the State Constitution. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Kansas is legal up to 22 weeks but requires a waiting period and parental consent for minors.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights victory included overwhelming margins on Kansas' few patches of left-leaning political turf: 81 percent to 19 in the county that includes the college town of Lawrence; 74 percent to 26 in the county that includes Kansas City; and 68 percent to 32 in populous Johnson County, a suburban area that was once reliably Republican but that has trended rapidly toward Democrats since Mr. Trump's entrance into national politics.\par \par \uc2 But the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights side also won convincingly in the county that includes Wichita, a more conservative place with a long, sometimes violent, history at the center of America's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters won in Topeka, the politically mixed state capital, and in Republican-leaning areas around military bases. Perhaps most surprising, they carried several rural and exurban counties where Republican candidates routinely trounce Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Kansas is also home to a Catholic archbishop who has ardently opposed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and played a leading role in the ballot campaign, mobilizing Catholic voters.\par \par \uc2 Value Them Both, a group that led the vote-yes effort, reported raising $2.45 million this year from the Archdiocese of Kansas City, Kan., which Archbishop Joseph F. Naumann leads, and about half a million from the Diocese of Wichita. Churches held vigils, and parishioners donated money, which he hoped would offer an example to other dioceses on how to engage the issue.\par \par \uc2 Not all Catholics were persuaded.\par \par \uc2 Lorraine Durie, 57, a Democrat from Overland Park, said the amendment first came up in a homily at her church this spring. ''Although I feel like I'm a really good Catholic, it just didn't line up with how I felt,'' she said. She voted against the amendment and in favor of preserving \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 The significant margin of loss, even in areas with a strong Catholic presence, presents an evangelistic challenge for the church, Archbishop Naumann said in an interview on Wednesday. A large number of Catholics may be baptized but do not follow church teaching or participate in church life, and instead are overly shaped by the broader society, he said.\par \par \uc2 ''There's this reaction of people, like this tremendous right has been taken away from them,'' he said. ''When you ask, Well, what is the right that has been lost, it is really the right to kill our own children, people in our culture and society have been conditioned unfortunately to see this as some great advancement.''\par \par \uc2 Archbishop Naumann said some Kansans would feel ''buyer's remorse,'' as the state would increasingly become ''a destination state for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .'' Two bordering states have now banned the procedure in nearly all cases, and, even before Roe fell, nearly half the women who received abortions in Kansas lived elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 Kansans have complicated political views, and extrapolating the amendment results to partisan races has pitfalls. Sharon Schnieders, a Republican from Ottawa, Kan., voted in favor of the amendment to remove \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections, in part because of her experience as an adoptive parent. But Ms. Schnieders, a retired teacher, said she also planned to support the re-election of Gov. \uc2 Laura Kelly\uc2 , a Democrat who supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, because of her work on education issues.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Hupe Seib, the Republican voter in rural Jefferson County who came out against the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  amendment, also plans to vote for Governor Kelly.\par \par \uc2 Though she still votes Republican in local races, Ms. Hupe Seib did not support Mr. Trump and she said she feared her fellow Republicans in state office would keep trying to restrict access even after the vote on Tuesday. She planned to support Ms. Kelly in November to guard against that.\par \par \uc2 ''Unless we vote out some of these right-wingers on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' Ms. Hupe Seib said, ''they are going to be back next year and the year after that, continually trying to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 Mitch Smith reported from Prairie Village, Kan., and Overland Park, Kan.; Lauren Fox reported from Ottawa, Kan., and Perry, Kan.; and Elizabeth Dias reported from Washington.\par \par \uc2 ''We won this race blindfolded and with one arm tied behind our back,'' said State Representative Stephanie Clayton, a Democrat from Overland Park, a suburb of Kansas City, Kan. (PHOTOGRAPH BY EVERT NELSON/USA TODAY NETWORK, VIA REUTERS) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220804ei8400042\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
